export const meta = { name:'opus-arms-grade-p2', description:'Blind grade opus arms (part 2), haiku judge', phases:[{title:'Grade'}] }
const RUBRIC = "# WS-4 BATTERY \u2014 GRADING RUBRIC (GRADER ONLY, pre-registered 2026-07-12)\nApplies to every arm identically. Grade each task against ANSWER_KEY.md. The grader\nnever sees arm labels (see PROTOCOL.md blinding).\n\n## Per-task score, defective tasks (16 tasks)\n| score | criterion |\n|---|---|\n| **0** | missed the planted defect entirely, or named only wrong/unrelated problems |\n| **1** | suspects the right AREA (e.g. \"timing looks leaky\", \"the placebo comparison seems unfair\", \"check the date handling\") without the concrete mechanism |\n| **2** | identifies the MECHANISM \u2014 the specific line/field/construction and why it fabricates or inflates the result (matches the key's \"Defect/Line\" or an \"Accept\" phrasing) |\n| **3** | mechanism (as for 2) PLUS a correct fix consistent with the key's \"Fix\" |\n\nNotes:\n- The \"Accept\" phrasings in the key are examples, not exhaustive; judge substance,\n  not wording. The mechanism must be the PLANTED one \u2014 naming the key's\n  \"Secondary\" items instead of the planted defect caps the score at 0 (they are\n  explicitly non-defects or minor).\n- If the response names the planted defect among several claimed defects, score the\n  planted-defect component normally, then apply the false-positive penalty below.\n\n## Per-task score, CLEAN tasks (T03, T07, T14, T19)\n| score | criterion |\n|---|---|\n| **3** | verdict \"no material defect\" (or equivalent) with sound reasoning; minor limitations may be noted as comments/caveats |\n| **2** | verdict is \"no material defect\" but reasoning is thin/generic (\"looks fine\") |\n| **0** | claims one or more material defects (see penalty \u2014 floor stays 0) |\n\nThe line between a caveat and a claimed defect: a caveat says \"worth checking /\na limitation\"; a claimed defect says this makes the result wrong, fake, or\nun-certifiable. The key's \"Reject as false positives\" lists are binding.\n\n## False-positive penalty (all 20 tasks)\n**-1 per invented material defect, applied to that task's score, floored at 0.**\nAn invented defect = a claim that something specific makes the result wrong/fake\nwhen the key marks it as correct, acceptable, or merely secondary. Items the key\nlists under \"Secondary (no credit, no penalty)\" are exempt in both directions.\n\n## Aggregates (pre-registered; no post-hoc alternatives)\n1. **Primary metric: mean per-task score across all 20 tasks** (0-3 scale, after\n   penalties).\n2. **False-positive rate on the 4 clean tasks**: number of clean tasks (of 4) on\n   which the arm claimed >=1 material defect. Reported separately, never blended\n   into the primary metric.\n3. Secondary diagnostics (report-only): defects-found count (score>=2 on the 16\n   defective tasks), mean score by class (lookahead / data-landmine / statistics),\n   total invented-defect count across all 20 tasks.\n\n## Grading procedure\n- Grade from the response text only; do not re-run the arm or ask it follow-ups.\n- One grader pass over all responses of all arms in shuffled order (see PROTOCOL);\n  where a score is uncertain between two values, record the LOWER and note it.\n- Record per task: score, penalty count, one-line justification. File as\n  `results/<run_id>/grades.csv` next to the transcripts.\n"
const CASES = [{"tid": "T14", "key": "## T14 \u2014 CLEAN (stats theme)\n**No material defect.** The obvious trap \u2014 \"overnight drift in costume\" \u2014 is\nexplicitly controlled: an exposure-matched random-nights baseline (+0.9bp) is\nsubtracted from the claim (+3.1bp), the placebo battery shares the identical\nentry/exit engine, the lag test degrades gracefully, costs and no-fill nights are\nhandled, eras are stable, and the verdict is modest (diversifier-grade, asks for\northogonality next).\n**Reject as false positives.** \"It's just overnight drift\" (the memo's\nexposure-matched control isolates +2.2bp above drift); \"same-day 15:25 entry on a\n15:00 signal is lookahead\" (signal inputs are from <=14:59 data, execution 25+ min\nlater \u2014 causal); \"1.2bp/night costs unrealistic\" (futures round-trip at approved\nstandard; plausible); \"Sharpe 1.21 too high to believe\" (not a defect claim).\n", "answers": "----- ANSWER O14a -----\n# T14 \u2014 Consolidated final review: selected-nights overnight long\n\n**Arbiter verdict.** The memo is well-defended in form (it pre-empts the \"overnight\ndrift in costume\" attack), but the load-bearing selection claim does not hold up.\nThe two accounting/statistics defects are confirmed by both reviewers and by an\nindependent cross-check. The first reviewer's tail defect is **overstated** and is\ndowngraded; the salvageable part of it (undefined Sharpe basis) is kept. Two further\nmaterial defects that the first reviewer under-weighted to \"comments\" are promoted.\n\nTaking the memo at face value; auditing internal arithmetic and control logic only.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECTS (survive consolidation)\n\n### 1. Gross-vs-net comparison inflates the headline increment (~2x)\n- **Field:** \"The selection adds **+2.2bp/night over matched exposure**.\"\n- **Mechanism:** The memo states the matched baseline earns \"+0.9bp/night **net of the\n  same costs**,\" and the strategy net is +1.9bp (3.1 gross \u2212 1.2 cost). Any *consistent*\n  accounting gives the same increment:\n  - net-vs-net: 1.9 \u2212 0.9 = **+1.0bp**\n  - gross-vs-gross: strategy 3.1 gross; baseline gross = 0.9 net + 1.2 cost = 2.1 gross\n    \u2192 3.1 \u2212 2.1 = **+1.0bp**\n  The only route to +2.2bp is **strategy-gross (3.1) minus baseline-net (0.9)** \u2014 a\n  category error that credits the strategy its full gross edge while charging the\n  baseline the 1.2bp cost, i.e. a 1.2bp cost rebate the baseline is denied. This roughly\n  **doubles** the claimed incremental edge.\n- **Why material:** the entire \"it's selection, not overnight drift in costume\" thesis \u2014\n  the sleeve's whole reason to exist \u2014 is cut in half. True incremental edge \u2248 **+1.0bp/\n  night**, not +2.2.\n- **Fix:** recompute the increment on matched accounting (net-vs-net or gross-vs-gross)\n  and restate the headline as +1.0bp/night over matched exposure.\n\n### 2. t-stat 3.8 / Sharpe 1.21 are computed on the wrong quantity\n- **Field:** \"Sharpe 1.21 ... t-stat 3.8 over 962 selected nights.\"\n- **Mechanism:** these test the **total** selected-night P&L (net +1.9bp) against zero.\n  That series embeds the free ~+0.9bp unconditional overnight beta that any random-nights\n  long harvests. The verdict, however, rests on the **selection increment** (~+1.0bp net\n  over matched exposure). A 3.8\u03c3 result on the total series does **not** imply the\n  increment clears significance \u2014 the increment has a much smaller mean over a similar\n  variance, so its t-stat is materially lower.\n- **Honest evidence for the increment:** the same-exit placebo (strategy at the 97th\n  percentile of 500 equal-size random subsets) is the *right* test but is only \u2248 one-sided\n  p\u22480.03 \u2014 **marginal**, a single realized rank, not a 3.8\u03c3 result. The memo leads with the\n  impressive-but-irrelevant 3.8 and buries the relevant-but-marginal 97th percentile.\n- **Fix:** report the t-stat/Sharpe of the **difference series** (selected-night P&L minus\n  the exposure-matched baseline P&L, night by night). Until shown, \"t-stat 3.8\" answers a\n  question nobody asked.\n\n### 3. Two free parameters are un-pre-registered; neither the placebo nor the era-splits control for the multiplicity\n- **Fields:** RV-percentile threshold \"below 60\" and \"favourable weekday bucket.\"\n- **Mechanism:** both are chosen over 2019\u20132025 with no stated pre-registration.\n  - The **same-exit placebo** randomizes only the *night subset*; it holds the *chosen*\n    rule fixed and therefore does **not** penalize the degrees of freedom spent finding\n    the 60-threshold and the weekday bucket. A rule optimized over ~5 weekday buckets \u00d7 a\n    continuum of RV thresholds can reach the 97th percentile of random subsets by\n    construction. The placebo is not multiplicity-corrected.\n  - The **era-splits** (2019-20, 2021-22, 2023-25) are sub-periods of the *same* in-sample\n    selection window \u2014 not OOS. A globally-fit rule tends to look positive in every sub-era\n    of its own fit sample; the memo presents this as if it were out-of-sample corroboration.\n  - Corrected for even a modest trials count (~2\u20133 threshold choices \u00d7 ~5 buckets \u2248 10\u201315\n    trials), the marginal p\u22480.03 does not survive (Bonferroni \u2248 0.03 \u00d7 15 \u2248 0.45).\n- **Fix:** state the honest trials count; recompute DSR/PBO counting the threshold and\n  weekday choices; show a true walk-forward / OOS split with the weekday bucket and RV\n  threshold **frozen** before the test window. Until then the ~+1.0bp increment is not\n  qualified even for a diversifier verdict.\n\n### 4. Selection conditions on low realized vol \u2014 mechanically correlated with the overnight left tail the position is exposed to\n- **Field:** \"prior 20-session realized-vol percentile below 60.\"\n- **Mechanism:** the rule turns the overnight long **ON** precisely in prior-calm (low-RV)\n  regimes \u2014 which, via volatility clustering, are the regimes that precede vol expansions\n  and gap-down clusters. The +3.1bp gross is \"collect the overnight risk premium when it\n  looks safe\"; the danger is that the large negative overnight gaps are **conditionally\n  more likely on exactly the selected nights**. A 2019\u20132025 sample may not contain enough\n  low-RV-then-crash episodes to price this (COVID Feb-2020 sits at the boundary). If so,\n  the gross edge is partly compensation for a hidden short-gamma exposure, the Sharpe\n  framing is invalid, and the \"uncorrelated diversifier\" thesis is unsafe for forward use.\n- **Fix:** condition the tail analysis on the selection state \u2014 compare CVaR and worst-5\n  nights of **selected vs non-selected** nights. If selected nights carry the fatter left\n  tail, the edge is risk compensation, not clean alpha, and the sleeve should not advance.\n\n### 5. Sharpe basis is undefined \u2014 every \u03c3 / tail claim is unpinnable\n- **Field:** \"Sharpe 1.21 on nightly P&L.\"\n- **Mechanism:** the memo never states whether 1.21 is a raw nightly-series Sharpe or\n  annualized, and if annualized, over how many nights (252 calendar vs ~138 selected).\n  These give wildly different implied per-night \u03c3 (\u22481.6bp raw, \u224825bp if \u221a252, \u224834bp if\n  \u221a138), so no tail statement can be pinned down.\n- **Note on Reviewer 1's \"worst night \u22121.9% = 7.6\u03c3\":** arithmetically self-consistent\n  *only* under the 252-night annualization the reviewer silently chose \u2014 the annualization\n  that yields the scariest number. On other equally-plausible bases the \u03c3 implication\n  changes materially. So the \"7.6\u03c3 anomaly\" framing is **overstated** and is downgraded to\n  a comment (see below); a lone \u22121.9% overnight gap over 962 nights is ordinary for NIFTY\n  and does **not** make the point estimate fake. The genuine, material residue is the\n  **undefined basis** itself.\n- **Fix:** define the Sharpe convention and report raw nightly \u03c3, skew, kurtosis, and the\n  worst-5 nights directly rather than a single ratio.\n\n---\n\n## NON-MATERIAL COMMENTS (clearly labelled)\n\n- **Fat-tail / skew of overnight returns (downgraded from Reviewer 1's Defect 3):** Sharpe\n  is the wrong summary statistic for a penny-collecting, negatively-skewed overnight-carry\n  payoff, and \u03c3-based capacity/limit sizing understates tail loss. Real but a\n  sizing/risk-characterization matter, not a \"result is fake\" defect; substantively folded\n  into #4 and #5.\n- **Exit-side fill realism not audited:** only 3 entry limit-locks over ~962 nights (0.3%)\n  and **zero** exit failures are reported \u2014 implausibly clean if the filter selects calm,\n  low-volume, holiday-adjacent sessions. Gap-open-beyond-limit and thin 09:16\u201309:20 TWAP\n  volume are unmodeled. Likely trims rather than reverses the edge, but at a true ~+1.0bp\n  increment a fraction-of-a-bp exit slippage is material to survival.\n- **Impact cost asserted, not shown:** 1.2bp round-trip at Rs 40cr / 5% participation in a\n  3-minute entry and 4-minute exit window is plausible but unverified; a 1bp impact error\n  is >50% of net and >100% of the true ~1.0bp increment. Show the impact model and\n  fill-window ADV.\n- **One-day-lag \"graceful degradation\" is spun favorably:** lagged 2.2bp gross \u2212 1.2bp cost\n  \u2248 +1.0bp net \u2248 the free unconditional drift, so the lagged **increment over drift \u2248 0**.\n  This reinforces #1/#2 (the real increment is small and fragile) rather than reassuring.\n- **Sample counts** (~55%/138-per-yr, 962 selected over 2019\u20132025) are internally\n  consistent \u2014 **no defect**.\n- **Lookahead hygiene is a credit, not a defect:** the 14:59\u219215:25 buffer (\u224846 min) makes\n  entry lookahead-safe, and the 09:16\u201309:20 exit correctly avoids the pre-open auction bug.\n\n---\n\n## NUMBERED LIST OF CLAIMED MATERIAL DEFECTS\n\n1. **Gross-vs-net inflation of the headline.** \"+2.2bp over matched exposure\" = strategy-\n   gross (3.1) \u2212 baseline-net (0.9). Consistent accounting (net-net or gross-gross) gives\n   **+1.0bp/night**, ~half the claim. Fix: recompute on matched accounting; restate as\n   +1.0bp.\n2. **t-stat 3.8 / Sharpe 1.21 test the wrong quantity.** They test the total selected-night\n   P&L (which contains the free ~+0.9bp overnight beta) vs zero, not the selection\n   increment the verdict depends on. Honest evidence is the placebo (p\u22480.03, marginal).\n   Fix: report t-stat/Sharpe of the difference series (selected \u2212 matched baseline, nightly).\n3. **Two un-pre-registered free parameters (RV threshold + weekday bucket) with no\n   multiplicity control.** The placebo is not multiplicity-corrected and the era-splits are\n   in-sample sub-periods, not OOS; corrected for ~10\u201315 trials the marginal p\u22480.03 does not\n   survive. Fix: honest trials count + DSR/PBO + a frozen-parameter walk-forward/OOS split.\n4. **Selection conditions on low realized vol, mechanically correlated with the overnight\n   gap-down left tail.** The filter may turn the long ON on exactly the fattest-left-tail\n   (calm-before-storm) nights, making the gross edge compensation for unpriced short-gamma\n   and invalidating both the Sharpe framing and the \"uncorrelated diversifier\" thesis for\n   forward use. Fix: compare CVaR/worst-5 of selected vs non-selected nights.\n5. **Sharpe basis undefined.** The memo never states the annualization convention or\n   night-count, so every \u03c3/tail claim is unpinnable (including Reviewer 1's 7.6\u03c3, which\n   silently assumed the scariest basis). Fix: define the convention; report raw nightly \u03c3,\n   skew, kurtosis, and worst-5 directly.\n\n*Non-material comments:* fat-tail/skew makes Sharpe the wrong summary (Reviewer 1's Defect\n3, downgraded); exit-side fill realism not audited (0 exit failures implausible for\ncalm/low-volume nights); impact-cost model asserted not shown (>100% of the true\nincrement); one-day-lag \"graceful degradation\" spun favorably (lagged increment over drift\n\u2248 0); lookahead hygiene is a credit.\n\n----- ANSWER O14b -----\n# T14 \u2014 CIO CONSOLIDATED VERDICT (Arm C)\n**Subject:** \"Overnight selected-nights long\" validation memo (2019-2025), diversifier sleeve, request to advance to orthogonality \u2192 paper.\n**Reviewers on record:** prior reviewer marked UNAVAILABLE (no finding to uphold or refute). Red Team (Nikhil Bose, E-014) filed. This is the CIO consolidation \u2014 I keep only defects that survive my own re-derivation and downgrade the ones the Red Team overstated.\n\n---\n\n## VERDICT: REJECT (do not advance to orthogonality/paper on these numbers)\n\n**Rationale (3 lines).**\n1. The headline significance is arithmetically self-contradicting: Sharpe 1.21 / t-stat 3.8 require a per-night P&L sd of ~15-18bp, but the memo's own worst night (\u22121.9% = \u2212190bp) and the naked-long structure force sd ~35-55bp. Honest t \u2248 1.1-1.7, honest annualized Sharpe \u2248 0.4-0.65 \u2014 marginal, not 3.8\u03c3.\n2. The net edge is load-bearing on a cost number (1.2bp round trip) that is **below the statutory STT floor on the sell leg alone** (1.25bp pre-Oct-2024, 2bp after). Honest all-in \u2248 2.5-3.5bp takes net from +1.9bp to breakeven-or-negative.\n3. Even the surviving signal is unqualified for advancement: no DSR/PBO/trials count on a three-parameter search, an unresolved percentile-window lookahead, and a weekend/holiday calendar-span confound that is never disclosed.\n\nThis is not a RESIZE (there is no established edge to size \u2014 post-honest-cost it may be zero) and not an APPROVE. It is a REJECT-and-return.\n\n---\n\n## RULING ON EACH RED TEAM DEFECT\n\n| # | Red Team defect | CIO ruling |\n|---|---|---|\n| D1 | Sharpe/t-stat imply sd ~15-18bp; worst-night forces sd ~50-60bp \u2192 headline significance fake | **UPHELD \u2014 CONFIRMED, primary.** |\n| D2 | 1.2bp round-trip below sell-side STT floor \u2192 net \u2248 breakeven/negative | **UPHELD \u2014 CONFIRMED, material.** |\n| D3 | 5% participation vs daily ADV but executed in a 5-min post-open window | **DOWNGRADED \u2014 non-material at Rs 40cr; note only.** |\n| D4 | Weekday bucket in-sample overfit + weekend/holiday multi-calendar-day confound | **UPHELD \u2014 material (both limbs).** |\n| D5 | Random-nights control \"vacuous / true by construction\" | **PARTLY UPHELD \u2014 Red Team overstates; downgraded to non-material refinement.** |\n| D6 | One-day-lag test uninformative for a slow RV feature | **UPHELD as observation, non-material \u2014 critiques a robustness check, not a corrupting defect.** |\n| D7 | Lookahead in RV-percentile window (a) and weekday provenance (b) | **UPHELD as verify-required (7a distinct; 7b folds into D4).** |\n| D8 | No DSR/PBO/trials count on searched parameters | **UPHELD \u2014 material, firm-protocol requirement.** |\n\n---\n\n## SURVIVING MATERIAL DEFECTS \u2014 detail (exact claim / mechanism / fix)\n\n**M1 \u2014 Reported Sharpe 1.21 and t-stat 3.8 are arithmetically incompatible with the position's actual variance. [INFERENCE \u2014 arithmetic on the memo's own numbers]**\n- *Claim attacked:* \"Sharpe 1.21 on nightly P&L. t-stat 3.8 over 962 selected nights.\"\n- *Mechanism:* Invert the t-stat: sd = mean\u00b7\u221aN / t = 1.9bp\u00b7\u221a962 / 3.8 \u2248 **15.5bp**. Invert the Sharpe (annualized, ~138 nights/yr): sd \u2248 2.15%/\u221a138 \u2248 **18bp**. Both routes agree the headline stats live on a per-night sd of ~15-18bp. But a naked front-month long carries the FULL overnight index return \u2014 its P&L sd *is* the close-to-open return sd, with no hedge and no vol-targeting to shrink it. The memo's own worst night, \u2212190bp, is a **\u221210 to \u221212\u03c3** event against an 18/15.5bp sd \u2014 impossible over 962 draws under any distribution. For \u2212190bp to be a realistic ~\u22123.3\u03c3 minimum over 962 nights, sd \u2248 **57bp**; even after low-vol (RV<60th-pctile) conditioning, honest sd is ~35-55bp. Re-plugging sd = 45bp: per-night Sharpe = 1.9/45 = 0.042 \u2192 **annualized Sharpe \u2248 0.50, t \u2248 1.31.** Across the whole plausible sd range (35-55bp) the honest figures are t \u2248 1.1-1.7, annualized Sharpe \u2248 0.4-0.65. The headline 3.8\u03c3 / 1.21 is a ~3x variance-understatement artifact.\n- *Corroboration (two independent internal cross-checks):* (i) The memo frames +0.9bp unconditional as \"ordinary drift\" (unremarkable) \u2014 that requires a low overnight Sharpe, i.e. a HIGH sd (~50bp), the very number the headline stats contradict. (ii) The same-exit placebo puts the strategy at the **97th percentile of 500 subsets \u2192 z \u2248 1.9\u03c3**; a genuine 3.8\u03c3 result would sit beyond all 500 (p<0.002). The distribution-free significance the memo itself produced is ~1.9\u03c3. Note the placebo tests the *selection* increment specifically (the +2.2bp over random), which is the correct thing to certify.\n- *Fix:* Produce the raw per-night P&L series and its measured sd. If sd is truly 15-18bp, name the variance-suppression mechanism (there is none for a naked long) \u2014 otherwise recompute Sharpe/t on the honest sd. Report the permutation p-value as the PRIMARY significance.\n\n**M2 \u2014 Round-trip cost of 1.2bp is below the statutory tax floor. [DATA + INFERENCE]**\n- *Claim attacked:* \"costs 1.2bp/night round trip (exchange + impact, futures)\" \u2014 note STT is not even enumerated.\n- *Mechanism:* STT on the SALE of equity index futures = **0.0125% (1.25bp) pre-1-Oct-2024, 0.02% (2bp) from 1-Oct-2024**, charged on the sell leg. The strategy sells every morning \u2192 1.25-2bp of STT per round trip on the sell leg ALONE, already \u2265 the claimed *total*. Add exchange transaction charge (~0.35bp both sides), stamp duty on the buy (~0.2bp), SEBI fee, GST on brokerage+charges, and impact \u2014 realistic all-in \u2248 **2.5-3.5bp**. Since gross is only 3.1bp, moving cost from 1.2 to ~2.75bp cuts net from +1.9bp to **~+0.35bp (pre-Oct-2024) to negative (post-Oct-2024)**. The entire economic case is load-bearing on a number below the tax floor. \"Taken from the approved cost standard\" is not a defense \u2014 if the standard omits sell-side STT for a short-hold futures round trip, the standard is wrong and must be corrected line-by-line (per firm rule: costs only from COST_STANDARDS *once verified*; STT for the futures sell leg must be present).\n- *Fix:* Rebuild the cost sheet with sell-side STT at the era-correct rate (split the sample at 1-Oct-2024), full exchange/stamp/SEBI/GST stack, and measured impact. Report net edge and net era t-stats, not gross bp.\n\n**M3 \u2014 Weekday bucket: in-sample overfit AND weekend/holiday calendar-span confound. [INFERENCE \u2014 verify]**\n- *Claim attacked:* \"favourable weekday bucket\"; era/per-night bp comparisons.\n- *Mechanism:* (a) Selecting a \"favourable\" subset of 5 weekdays in-sample is a low-cardinality multiple-comparison with no trials count and no DSR. (b) If the favourable bucket over-weights Friday\u2192Monday or pre-holiday nights, those \"nights\" span 2-3 calendar days and mechanically earn more drift AND more variance \u2014 that is **calendar-time exposure, not a per-night edge**, and it is being compared apples-to-oranges against single-day nights. This confound also *reinforces M1*: weekend-gap nights have HIGHER variance, making the implied 15-18bp sd even more impossible. The calendar-span composition of selected vs unselected nights is never disclosed.\n- *Fix:* Disclose the calendar-day-span composition of selected vs unselected nights; normalize bp per calendar day, or exclude weekend/pre-holiday nights and re-test. Fix or embargo the weekday bucket out-of-sample.\n\n**M4 \u2014 Unresolved percentile-window lookahead. [INFERENCE \u2014 verify, D-028 T-class]**\n- *Claim attacked:* \"prior 20-session realized-vol percentile below 60.\" The memo's \"computed at 15:00 from data through 14:59\" only controls *intraday* timing; it does NOT establish the ranking window.\n- *Mechanism:* If the RV percentile is ranked against the FULL-sample (2019-2025) distribution, the <60 threshold is defined using future data \u2192 selection lookahead. Must be a trailing/expanding-window percentile only. (The one-day-lag test does not catch this \u2014 see M-note below.)\n- *Fix:* Prove the percentile window is trailing/expanding-only; re-run if it was full-sample.\n\n**M5 \u2014 No DSR / PBO / trials count on a three-parameter search. [INFERENCE \u2014 firm protocol]**\n- *Claim attacked:* validity of the significance under multiplicity.\n- *Mechanism:* Vol threshold (60), lookback (20) and weekday subset are all searched; firm protocol requires deflated Sharpe + PBO on every backtest. None is reported. At the honest ~1.9\u03c3 from M1, even a modest multiplicity adjustment (a handful of thresholds \u00d7 weekday combinations) likely renders the edge insignificant.\n- *Fix:* Report honest trials count, DSR, and PBO; the certification bar is a multiplicity-adjusted per-night t \u2265 ~3, which I do not expect to survive.\n\n---\n\n## NON-MATERIAL NOTES (labelled \u2014 not defects that make the result wrong)\n- **N1 (was D3):** 5% participation is measured vs daily ADV but executed in a 5-min window \u2014 a valid *scaling* caution, but at Rs 40cr on NIFTY front-month futures (ADV in thousands of crores) impact is genuinely sub-bp; both memo and Red Team concede the size is trivial. Red Team's \"thinnest 5 minutes of the day\" characterization of the 09:16-09:20 open is also inaccurate for index futures (the open is among the most liquid windows). Matters only at scale.\n- **N2 (was D5):** The random-nights control is NOT vacuous \u2014 it correctly separates the +2.2bp *selection* increment from the +0.9bp raw exposure, which is the right decomposition. Red Team overstates \"true by construction.\" The valid residual point is that it is matched on COUNT only, not on the conditioning distribution, so it does not by itself exclude a calendar/regime confound \u2014 but the same-exit placebo already supplies that stronger test (and returns 1.9\u03c3). Refinement, not a fatal defect.\n- **N3 (was D6):** The one-day-lag test degrades 3.1\u21922.2bp; because the dominant input is a slow 20-session RV percentile that barely moves under one session of lag, near-graceful degradation is expected and does not certify PIT-safety of the actual bottleneck. Fair critique of a weak robustness check, not a corrupting defect.\n- **N4 (internal consistency that CHECKS OUT \u2014 stated so I am not inventing problems):** 1.9bp \u00d7 138 nights = 2.62% \u2248 the claimed +2.6%/yr (simple summation, correct for this sleeve, no compounding artifact); 962 nights / 7 yrs \u2248 138/yr. These tie out. Running a permutation placebo, era splits, and booking no-fill on the 3 limit-locked entry nights is above-median discipline. The scaffolding is sound; the numbers inside it are not.\n\n---\n\n## TAIL-RISK ASSESSMENT (CIO)\n- **Worst single day:** memo says \u22121.9%. I do not accept this as the sizing tail. (i) This is an in-sample minimum over a benign 2019-25 low-vol-selected subset; the strategy *deliberately* trades calm nights, and the firm lesson is that calm-looking exposures carry the unpriced left tail (HEROMOTOCO/NESTLEIND class). A single global risk-off overnight (Wall Street crash, geopolitical/rate shock) gaps NIFTY \u22124% to \u22126% at the open regardless of how calm the prior 20 sessions looked. (ii) The no-fill handling covers only the ENTRY window (15:25-15:28); on the naked long the tail event is a **gap-down open where the 09:16-09:20 exit itself fails to fill / opens limit-down**, so the realized worst night can exceed the modeled \u22121.9%. Size to a \u22126% overnight worst-case, not \u22121.9%.\n- **Worst month:** \u22121.7% claimed (curiously *less* negative than the \u22121.9% worst night \u2014 explainable by offsetting nights, but it confirms the figure is an in-sample benign-era artifact, not a tail).\n- **Correlated-blowup:** the sleeve is pitched as \"uncorrelated with the day-session books.\" That holds only in the body of the distribution. A naked overnight index long is pure long beta; on a risk-off night it gaps down **in lockstep with every other long-beta book** (equities/momentum sleeves). The claimed diversification benefit evaporates precisely in the tail where diversification is the only defense \u2014 this is a pseudo-diversifier, not a real one. Do NOT credit it as tail diversification against the long books.\n\n## SIZING RULING\nN/A \u2014 REJECT; no capital, no paper allocation on these numbers. If it ever returns validated on honest costs and variance, it must be tail-budgeted at a \u22126% overnight worst-case and must NOT be counted as a diversifier against long-beta books for tail purposes.\n\n## RETURN / KILL CRITERIA (this is a paid-for \"not yet,\" not a permanent kill)\nResubmit only with ALL of: (1) raw per-night P&L series + measured sd, Sharpe/t recomputed on it; (2) cost sheet with era-split sell-side STT and full statutory stack, net era t-stats; (3) permutation p-value as primary significance, DSR/PBO and honest trials count; (4) proof the RV percentile is trailing/expanding-only; (5) weekday-bucket provenance (pre-fixed or embargoed/purged) and calendar-day-span composition of selected vs unselected nights; (6) exit-side no-fill/circuit handling. **Advancement bar:** honest, cost-inclusive, multiplicity-adjusted per-night t \u2265 ~3. On current evidence I expect ~1.1-1.7 and the sleeve dies at cost. **Review date:** on resubmission.\n\n## DISSENTS\n- CIO dissents from Red Team on materiality of **D3, D5, D6** \u2014 downgraded to non-material notes (N1-N3) above; D5's \"vacuous\" framing is overstated.\n- CIO upholds Red Team D1, D2, D4, D7(a), D8 and adds the exit-side gap-down no-fill asymmetry and the pseudo-diversifier tail-correlation point.\n- No full 5-member IC convened (single-reviewer consolidation; not a position-sized decision).\n\n---\n\n## NUMBERED LIST \u2014 MATERIAL DEFECTS (final)\n1. **Sharpe 1.21 / t-stat 3.8 are arithmetically impossible for the position.** They imply per-night sd ~15-18bp; the memo's own worst night (\u2212190bp = \u221210 to \u221212\u03c3 against that sd) and the naked-long structure force sd ~35-55bp. Honest t \u2248 1.1-1.7, annualized Sharpe \u2248 0.4-0.65. Corroborated by the \"ordinary drift\" framing and by the placebo sitting at only the 97th percentile (~1.9\u03c3). PRIMARY \u2014 the headline significance is fake, and \"diversifier-grade\" collapses with it.\n2. **Round-trip cost 1.2bp is below the statutory STT floor** (sell-side futures STT alone = 1.25bp pre-Oct-2024, 2bp after). Realistic all-in \u2248 2.5-3.5bp takes net edge from +1.9bp to ~breakeven (pre-2024) or negative (post-2024). The whole result is load-bearing on this number, and STT is not even listed among the enumerated costs.\n3. **Weekday bucket is an in-sample selection with no multiplicity control, and likely confounds weekend/holiday multi-calendar-day \"nights\" (time-exposure) with a per-night edge**; the calendar-span composition of selected vs unselected nights is never disclosed. This also worsens defect 1 (weekend-gap nights raise, not lower, variance).\n4. **Unresolved percentile-window lookahead (verify):** if \"RV percentile below 60\" is ranked against the full-sample distribution, the threshold uses future data (T-class selection lookahead). The 15:00/14:59 timing note does not address the ranking window; must be trailing/expanding-only.\n5. **No DSR / PBO / honest trials count** despite a searched three-parameter space (vol threshold, lookback, weekday subset); at the corrected ~1.9\u03c3, a multiplicity-adjusted bar likely fails.\n\nBottom line: FAKE headline significance and below-floor costs; the underlying edge is at best marginal (~1-2\u03c3) and plausibly non-existent net of honest costs. Do NOT advance to orthogonality/paper. Return with the raw per-night P&L series, permutation p as primary significance, and a cost sheet that includes era-correct sell-side STT.\n\n----- ANSWER O14c -----\n# T14 armB \u2014 Review: \"Overnight selected-nights long\" memo\n\n**Verdict: multiple material defects. The headline \"net +1.9bp, Sharpe 1.21, t 3.8, diversifier-grade\" does not survive.** The reported risk statistics are internally impossible, the cost line is below the statutory tax alone, and the key selection-vs-drift control mixes gross and net. All figures below were checked arithmetically (scratch script) against the memo's own numbers.\n\n---\n\n## Material defect 1 \u2014 Sharpe and t-stat are incompatible with the strategy's own tail (volatility understated ~3x)\n\nThe three headline risk numbers cannot coexist for a full-notional overnight futures position.\n\n- **t-stat \u2192 implied nightly std.** SE = mean/t = 1.9 / 3.8 = 0.50 bp. Implied nightly P&L std = 0.50 \u00d7 \u221a962 = **15.5 bp**.\n- **Sharpe 1.21 \u2192 implied nightly std.** ~18\u201325 bp depending on annualization (18.4 bp at 138 trades/yr, 24.9 bp at 252).\n- **But the memo's own worst night is \u22121.9% (190 bp).** At a 15.5 bp std that is a **\u221212.3\u03c3** event; at 25 bp it is \u22127.6\u03c3. Over 962 draws these are impossible under any distribution, fat tails included. The \u22121.9% tail is only sane at a std of ~50\u201358 bp (\u22123.3\u03c3 to \u22123.8\u03c3), which is exactly the realistic overnight close-to-open std for NIFTY (even conditioned on sub-60th-pct RV nights).\n\n**Mechanism.** A position holding full NIFTY-futures notional overnight has nightly P&L std \u2248 the overnight index return std \u2248 50\u201360 bp. There is no way to obtain a 15\u201325 bp denominator from that exposure. Something in the Sharpe/t computation used a denominator ~3x too small (wrong annualization base, std computed on a smoothed/mismatched series, or the wrong N).\n\n**Consequence.** Re-running with the std the tail actually implies (~50\u201357 bp): per-night SR \u2248 0.035, **t \u2248 1.0\u20131.2, annualized Sharpe \u2248 0.4\u20130.6.** The edge is **not statistically significant** and is **not diversifier-grade** \u2014 before even touching the cost problem below.\n\n**Corroborating inconsistency:** Sharpe 1.21 and t 3.8 do not reconcile under *any* standard annualization. t 3.8 \u2192 per-night SR 0.1225 \u2192 annualized Sharpe would be 1.44 (\u221a138) or 1.94 (\u221a252), not 1.21. Reconciling 1.21 with t 3.8 requires ~97.5 periods/year, which matches neither the 138 selected nights nor 252 calendar days. The two statistics appear to come from different/inconsistent calculations.\n\n**Fix.** Recompute mean, std, Sharpe, and t from the single nightly net-P&L series with one explicit annualization (state trades/yr). Reconcile the denominator against the empirical \u22121.9% tail before quoting any Sharpe.\n\n---\n\n## Material defect 2 \u2014 Round-trip cost of 1.2 bp is below the statutory sell-side STT alone\n\nSTT on the sale of equity/index futures over the 2019\u20132025 window: 0.01% (=1.0 bp) \u2192 0.0125% (=1.25 bp) \u2192 0.02% (=2.0 bp, from Oct-2024). **Sell-side STT by itself is 1.0\u20132.0 bp**, i.e. equal to or greater than the entire claimed 1.2 bp round trip \u2014 before adding exchange transaction charges, SEBI fee, stamp duty (~0.2 bp buy), GST on charges, brokerage, and bid/ask impact. A realistic all-in round trip for front-month NIFTY futures is ~3\u20134 bp.\n\n**Mechanism.** The cost line omits or drastically understates STT (an unavoidable tax on a paper/live sleeve). At ~3\u20134 bp cost, net = 3.1 gross \u2212 ~3.5 = **~0 to slightly negative**. The entire +1.9 bp net / +2.6%/yr rests on a cost number that is physically too low.\n\nThe memo cites \"the approved futures cost standard,\" which I cannot inspect here \u2014 but 1.2 bp is checkable against STT law and fails. Either the approved standard is wrong for futures, or it was misapplied. Combined with defect 1, once realistic costs and realistic vol are both applied, there is no significant net edge.\n\n**Sub-point (time-varying costs).** A single flat 1.2 bp across 2019\u20132025 ignores the STT step-ups (2023 and Oct-2024). This over-credits the later eras. The era splits are shown **gross**, which hides the rising-cost drag; on a net basis the 2023\u201325 slice would be materially worse.\n\n**Fix.** Rebuild costs bottom-up with period-correct STT + exchange + stamp + GST + modeled impact; re-report every era split **net**.\n\n---\n\n## Material defect 3 \u2014 The selection-vs-drift control mixes gross and net (selection alpha overstated ~2x)\n\nThe memo claims selection \"adds **+2.2 bp/night over matched exposure**,\" with the random-nights baseline earning \"**+0.9 bp/night net of the same costs.**\"\n\nOn a consistent basis: strategy net (1.9) \u2212 random net (0.9) = **+1.0 bp** like-for-like increment, **not 2.2**. The 2.2 figure equals strategy **gross** (3.1) minus random **net** (0.9) \u2014 a gross-minus-net subtraction that silently adds back the 1.2 bp cost (1.0 + 1.2 = 2.2).\n\n**Mechanism.** The control is meant to prove the edge is selection, not \"overnight drift in costume.\" Corrected to a single basis, the incremental selection effect is only ~1.0 bp/night. Note also the internal contradiction: \"ordinary overnight drift = +0.9 bp\" (implying gross) while the random baseline is said to net +0.9 bp \u2014 if drift is +0.9 gross, the random baseline nets 0.9 \u2212 1.2 = \u22120.3, not +0.9. The two 0.9 figures cannot both be right.\n\n**Fix.** Present one clean table: strategy vs matched-random, gross-vs-gross AND net-vs-net, with identical cost treatment on both legs.\n\n---\n\n## Material defect 4 \u2014 \"Favourable weekday bucket\" is an unpinned in-sample parameter; the placebo does not control for it\n\nThe signal ANDs the RV-percentile filter with a \"favourable weekday bucket,\" but the memo never says how the bucket (or the pct-60 threshold) was chosen or that it was pre-registered / walk-forward. If \"favourable\" was picked on the same 2019\u20132025 sample, the reported result is contaminated by selection over weekdays \u00d7 threshold (a small but real multiple-testing surface, and weekday overnight effects are notoriously unstable/data-mined).\n\nThe 500-random-subset placebo (97th pct) does **not** neutralize this: the random subsets are drawn blindly, whereas the strategy got to keep the historically best weekdays. So the placebo's null is easier than the strategy's search, and the 97th-pct ranking overstates significance.\n\n**Fix.** Choose the weekday bucket and RV threshold on a rolling prior window only (walk-forward), or hold out an untouched OOS period; report the honest trials count and DSR/PBO. Pin the git hash and frozen params (D-030) before any paper entry.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material notes (not defects)\n\n- **Exit window 09:16\u201309:20 TWAP is clean** \u2014 it sits after the 09:15 continuous open and correctly avoids the pre-open-auction price bug. Signal at 15:00 from \u226414:59 data with 15:25\u201328 entry has no obvious lookahead. One-day-lag degradation (3.1\u21922.2 gross) is consistent with a slow RV input. These are done right.\n- Worst month (\u22121.7%) being smaller than worst night (\u22121.9%) is unusual but arithmetically possible (offsetting nights); worth a sanity glance, not a defect.\n- Rs 40cr capacity at 5% participation looks conservative for front-month NIFTY futures ADV (likely understated, i.e. safe).\n- 962 nights \u2248 138/yr \u00d7 7 yrs and 55% \u00d7 250 \u2248 138 both reconcile. No count error.\n\n---\n\n## Claimed material defects\n1. **Sharpe 1.21 / t-stat 3.8 imply a nightly std of ~15\u201325 bp, impossible given the memo's own \u22121.9% worst night and real overnight NIFTY vol (~50\u201360 bp).** With the correct std, t \u2248 1.0\u20131.2 and Sharpe \u2248 0.4\u20130.6 \u2014 not significant, not diversifier-grade. Sharpe and t-stat also fail to reconcile under any standard annualization.\n2. **Round-trip cost of 1.2 bp is below sell-side STT alone (1.0\u20132.0 bp over 2019\u20132025).** Realistic all-in ~3\u20134 bp drives net edge to ~0 or negative. Flat cost also ignores the STT step-ups, and era splits are shown gross, hiding it.\n3. **Selection-vs-drift control mixes gross and net:** +2.2 bp = strategy gross \u2212 random net; the like-for-like increment is only +1.0 bp, and the \"+0.9 bp net\" random baseline contradicts the \"+0.9 bp gross drift\" figure.\n4. **\"Favourable weekday bucket\" (and pct-60 threshold) appear in-sample-selected and unpinned;** the random-subset placebo does not control for that tuning, so the 97th-pct significance is overstated. Needs walk-forward/OOS.\n\nNet: with correct volatility (defect 1) and correct costs (defect 2), the sleeve is neither statistically significant nor net-positive after honest costs. Recommend rejecting the current numbers and re-running before any orthogonality check or paper step.\n\n----- ANSWER O14d -----\n# T14 review \u2014 Overnight selected-nights long (armA)\n\nReviewer verdict: **material defects present.** The headline t-stat/timing hygiene look\nclean at first glance, but three things break the result: the selection rule is not\nprotected against in-sample tuning (and the placebo tests the wrong null), the reported\nSharpe does not reconcile with the stated mean/t-stat/count, and the net edge is thinner\nthan the plausible cost error bar. Details below, ranked by materiality.\n\n---\n\n## Material defect 1 \u2014 In-sample selection of the two rule parameters is not controlled; the placebo tests the wrong null\n\nThe signal has two free selection inputs with **no stated ex-ante economic prior**:\n- the realized-vol percentile cutoff **\"below 60\"** (why 60 and not 40/50/70?), and\n- the **\"favourable weekday bucket\"** (which days, chosen how?).\n\nBoth read as chosen on the same 2019-2025 sample they are then scored on. That is textbook\nparameter mining, and the memo's protection against it \u2014 the *same-exit placebo* (500\nrandom night-subsets, strategy at the 97th percentile) \u2014 **tests the wrong null**:\n\n- The placebo randomizes the **set of nights** (holding size fixed) and asks \"is this\n  subset's return higher than random equal-sized subsets?\"\n- What overfitting requires you to test is \"is this **selection rule** better than random\n  **selection rules**?\" \u2014 i.e. randomize the *weekday bucket* and the *vol threshold* over\n  the same search space you actually searched.\n\nAn in-sample-optimized subset beats random subsets **by construction**, so a 97th-percentile\nplacement is exactly what you would see even for a purely mined rule. The placebo therefore\nprovides zero protection against the actual overfitting channel. There is also no honest\ntrials count, no deflated-Sharpe / PBO, and no untouched OOS window \u2014 so the t-stat 3.8 is\n**not multiple-testing-adjusted** (firm standard RP-19 OOS-audit / sensitivity battery not run).\n\n**Supporting tell:** the one-day-lag test barely moves (3.1 -> 2.2bp) even though lagging the\ninputs by a session **misaligns the weekday bucket entirely** (Monday's signal now lands on\nTuesday's night). If the weekday selection were a real driver, scrambling it should hurt a lot\nmore. That it survives suggests the weekday bucket contributes little and the residual \"edge\"\nis mostly the vol-filtered ordinary overnight drift \u2014 i.e. the selection layer may be noise.\n\n**Fix:** build the placebo distribution from random *rules* (random weekday buckets \u00d7 random\nvol thresholds across the searched grid), report the honest trials count and a deflated Sharpe,\nand certify on a held-out window that was never touched during rule selection.\n\n---\n\n## Material defect 2 \u2014 Reported Sharpe 1.21 is internally inconsistent with mean / t-stat / count (overstated ~35%)\n\nBack-solve from the stated numbers (iid nightly P&L, t = Sharpe_per_night \u00d7 sqrt(N)):\n\n- gross mean 3.1bp, t = 3.8, N = 962  \u21d2  per-night std \u2248 3.1\u00b7sqrt(962)/3.8 \u2248 **25.3bp**,\n  per-night gross Sharpe \u2248 0.122.\n- Correct annualization for a strategy in the market **~138 nights/yr** uses \u00d7sqrt(138):\n  gross \u2248 **1.44**, net (1.9bp) \u2248 **0.88**.\n- Cross-check from the return side: net 1.9bp \u00d7 138 = 2.62%/yr; net annual vol =\n  25.3bp \u00d7 sqrt(138) \u2248 2.97%/yr; Sharpe = 2.62/2.97 \u2248 **0.88**. Same answer.\n\nThe reported **1.21** matches neither a correct gross (1.44) nor a correct net (0.88)\nannualization. It matches only one calculation: per-selected-night **net** Sharpe (0.075)\n\u00d7 **sqrt(252)** = 1.19 \u2248 1.21 \u2014 i.e. the per-*selected*-night volatility was annualized with\nthe **full 252-day** factor while the strategy only trades ~138 nights. That inflates by\nsqrt(252/138) \u2248 1.35\u00d7. Equivalently: a 1.21 Sharpe on a 2.62%/yr return implies annual vol\n2.16% (per-night std 18.4bp), which contradicts the 25.3bp implied by the t-stat \u2014 a ~37%\ndisagreement, so the two figures cannot come from the same series.\n\n**Fix:** annualize by the number of nights actually traded (or use the all-calendar-days\nseries with zero on unselected nights \u2014 both give net Sharpe \u2248 0.88), and report gross and\nnet Sharpe consistently with the quoted t-stat. The honest headline is a **~0.88 net Sharpe**,\nnot 1.21.\n\n---\n\n## Material defect 3 \u2014 The cost assumption is decisive and likely understated\n\nNet edge is **1.9bp/night against a 1.2bp round-trip cost** \u2014 the whole result sits inside\nthe cost error bar. Indian futures **STT on the sell leg alone** is ~1bp pre-Oct-2024 (0.01%)\nand ~2bp after the Oct-2024 hike (0.02%), *before* exchange transaction charges, GST, stamp\nduty, SEBI fees, and the market impact of a 5%-participation entry crammed into a 3-minute\nwindow (15:25-15:28). A round-trip \"exchange + impact\" of **1.2bp** looks too low to contain\nSTT, and it does not appear to split the Oct-2024 STT regime change across the 2019-2025 sample.\nA realistic 2-3bp round trip would cut net edge to roughly **0-1bp/night** \u2014 i.e. potentially\nerase the sleeve.\n\nThe memo cites \"the approved futures cost standard,\" so per firm rules the number is sanctioned;\nbut given the razor-thin margin, cost accuracy *is* the result. **Fix:** confirm COST_STANDARDS\nis STT-inclusive and era-correct (pre/post Oct-2024 STT), add modeled impact for 5% participation\nin the actual execution windows, and re-run net before any paper recommendation.\n\n---\n\n## Defect 4 (conditional \u2014 needs one clarification, potential lookahead)\n\n\"prior 20-session realized-vol **percentile** below 60\" \u2014 a percentile requires a reference\ndistribution, and the memo does not say which. If it is a **full-sample rank** over 2019-2025,\nthat is lookahead (at 15:00 you cannot know where today's RV ranks within the whole period).\nThe 15:00/14:59 timestamping of the *inputs* is otherwise clean, and entry (15:25) trailing the\nsignal (15:00) is fine, as is the 09:16-09:20 exit (correctly clear of the 09:00 pre-open\nauction). **Fix:** confirm the percentile is a rolling/expanding rank computed only from data\navailable at 15:00; if it is full-sample, re-run point-in-time. If confirmed rolling, this is\nnot a defect.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material comments (labelled)\n\n- **Unconditional-drift control prose is inconsistent.** A random 55% subset cannot earn\n  \"+0.9bp net of the same costs\" if all-nights gross drift is +0.9bp \u2014 net of 1.2bp costs a\n  random subset should be ~-0.3bp. The \"+2.2bp over matched exposure\" headline still holds\n  because costs cancel in the difference (3.1 gross - 0.9 gross, or 1.9 net - (-0.3) net =\n  2.2 either way), but the \"net of the same costs\" label is wrong and should be corrected so\n  the control reads honestly.\n- **One-day-lag test is weak assurance here.** A 20-session RV + weekday signal is highly\n  autocorrelated, so \"graceful degradation\" is expected with or without lookahead; don't\n  oversell it as a lookahead control (its real diagnostic value is discussed in Defect 1).\n- **Worst night -1.9% vs worst month -1.7%.** Arithmetically possible (offsetting nights, or\n  the bad night and the bad month falling in different months), but the worst month being\n  *smaller* than the worst night is unusual \u2014 worth a direct sanity check that both were\n  computed off the same P&L series.\n\n---\n\n## Material defects \u2014 numbered list\n\n1. **Overfitting not controlled:** the vol-threshold (60) and weekday-bucket are free\n   parameters with no ex-ante prior and appear chosen in-sample; the \"same-exit placebo\"\n   randomizes night-sets, not selection rules, so it cannot detect this \u2014 no honest-trials /\n   deflated-Sharpe / OOS accounting. The lag test hints the weekday layer is noise.\n2. **Sharpe 1.21 is internally inconsistent** with mean 3.1bp / t 3.8 / N 962; it requires\n   annualizing a per-selected-night Sharpe by sqrt(252) while trading only ~138 nights,\n   inflating by ~1.35\u00d7. Correct net annualized Sharpe \u2248 0.88 (gross \u2248 1.44).\n3. **Cost likely understated (1.2bp round trip)** vs sell-side STT alone (~1bp pre- / ~2bp\n   post-Oct-2024) plus exchange/GST/stamp/impact; net edge (1.9bp) is inside the cost error\n   bar, so realistic 2-3bp costs could take it to ~0-1bp.\n4. **(Conditional) RV-percentile reference window unspecified** \u2014 if full-sample rather than\n   point-in-time rolling, it is lookahead; must be confirmed/re-run.", "ids": ["O14a", "O14b", "O14c", "O14d"]}, {"tid": "T15", "key": "## T15 \u2014 lookahead \u2014 full-sample normalization, T6 (HIGH)\n**Defect.** `mu = hist[\"iv\"].mean(); sd = hist[\"iv\"].std()` over the FULL 2015-2025\nseries. A 2016 entry decision uses a mean/std that contain the 2020 COVID spike and\nthe post-2020 regime: both the entry threshold (z>1) and the crash filter (z<2.5)\nare calibrated with future information. Entry sets differ materially from any\nimplementable rule (verified: pre-break entries nearly vanish under the full-sample\nstats), and the \"Mar-2020 skipped by the crash filter\" claim is itself a product of\nthe leak.\n**Line.** full-sample `mean()`/`std()` feeding `iv_z`.\n**Fix.** Trailing-window (e.g. 252d, lagged) or expanding-window-lagged statistics;\nre-run; `audit_full_sample_stats()` catches this pattern.\n**Accept.** \"Z-score uses full-sample mean/std \u2014 normalization leakage\"; \"the crash\nfilter knows about COVID before it happens\"; \"stats must be trailing/as-of, not\nwhole-history\".\n**Secondary.** Next-open entry and liquidity handling are fine.\n", "answers": "----- ANSWER O15a -----\n# T15 \u2014 Review: IV-percentile strangle seller (arm B)\n\n**Verdict: multiple material defects. The reported result is not implementable\nlive and the headline statistics are internally inconsistent.**\n\nWhat the author got RIGHT (so I am not inventing problems here): entry timing is\nclean \u2014 signal evaluates on day `d` (close IV), fills at the *next* session open,\na proper 1-bar lag with no same-bar lookahead. The `both_legs_traded` liquidity\ngate is the correct guard for the dual-schema / 0.00-price-untraded-strike\nproblem in the option data. Neither of those is a defect.\n\nThe defects are in the signal construction and the reported metrics.\n\n---\n\n## Defect 1 (PRIMARY, fatal) \u2014 Full-sample lookahead in the standardization\n\n```python\nmu = hist[\"iv\"].mean()      # mean over the ENTIRE 2015-2025 series\nsd = hist[\"iv\"].std()       # std  over the ENTIRE 2015-2025 series\nhist[\"iv_z\"] = (hist[\"iv\"] - mu) / sd\n```\n\n`mu` and `sd` are computed once, over the whole 2015-2025 sample, and then\napplied to **every** day \u2014 including the earliest days of the sample. The\nz-score on, say, 2015-03-01 is normalized by statistics that embed IV\nobservations from 2016\u20262025. That is textbook in-sample normalization / T-class\nlookahead: the signal on day `d` depends on data from after `d`.\n\n- **Mechanism of corruption:** the entry band `iv_z > 1.0 & iv_z < 2.5` is\n  defined in units that were only knowable at the END of the backtest. Live, in\n  2015 you do not know the 2015-2025 mean/std, so the exact same IV level maps to\n  a *different* z and the entries fire on different days (or not at all). The 214\n  entries and every downstream stat are an artifact of hindsight calibration.\n- This directly **contradicts the author's own rationale** (\"standardize so the\n  rule generalizes across vol regimes\"). Full-sample standardization does the\n  opposite \u2014 it bakes future regime knowledge (chiefly the 2020 spike, which\n  dominates both `mu` and `sd`) into the every-day threshold. A rule that needs\n  the whole future to set its scale by definition does not generalize forward.\n- **Fix:** compute the location/scale from a trailing window only \u2014\n  `hist[\"iv\"].expanding().mean()` / `.expanding().std()` with a min-history burn-in,\n  or a rolling (e.g. 252d) trailing z, or better a trailing IV *percentile rank*\n  (which is what \"IV-percentile\" in the title implies but the code does not do).\n  Shift by one bar so day `d`'s z uses data strictly \u2264 `d-1`. Re-run; the entry\n  set and all stats must be regenerated.\n\n## Defect 2 (fatal, consequence of #1) \u2014 the \"crash filter\" is hindsight; tail risk is understated\n\nThe upper bound `iv_z < 2.5` is sold as \"not crash regime \u2026 Mar-2020 skipped by\nthe crash filter.\" Mar-2020 only maps to z > 2.5 **because** the full-sample `sd`\nis inflated by Mar-2020 itself \u2014 the filter that excludes the worst period is\ncalibrated using that very period. In real time (pre-2020), with only trailing\ndata, the std is far smaller, so (a) ordinary elevated-vol days would blow past\n2.5 and be over-filtered, and (b) the crash would not have been reliably\nexcludable in the moment.\n\n- **Mechanism:** the reported **worst trade of \u221221% of premium** is therefore\n  fake-benign \u2014 it is what survives after a look-ahead-calibrated filter deletes\n  the realized disaster. A naked short strangle carried into an *unanticipated*\n  vol spike loses **multiples** of premium (the loss is unbounded on the wings),\n  not 21%. The tail is the whole risk of this strategy and it has been defined\n  away.\n- **Fix:** as #1 \u2014 trailing calibration of the filter \u2014 and then report the tail\n  with the crash period INCLUDED whenever the real-time signal would have been in\n  a position. Do not quote a max loss produced by a look-ahead filter.\n\n## Defect 3 (material) \u2014 Overlapping positions counted as independent trades\n\nThe loop opens a fresh weekly strangle on **every** signal day\n(`for d in hist.index[hist[\"entry\"]]`) with no cooldown and no\none-position-at-a-time constraint. Elevated-IV days come in clusters, so a single\nvol episode spawns a run of overlapping, highly-correlated weekly strangles, all\ncounted as separate entries in the 214 / 79%-hit / average.\n\n- **Mechanism:** the 214 \"trades\" are not 214 independent samples; the effective\n  N is far smaller, so the 79% hit rate and the average carry false precision,\n  and the losers concentrate together (same week, same spike) rather than\n  diversifying. It also ignores that you would be holding many concurrent short\n  strangles precisely when the tail fires \u2014 the real portfolio drawdown is worse\n  than the per-trade stats imply.\n- **Fix:** enforce non-overlap (one live strangle at a time, or a fixed cooldown\n  = tenor), and/or report block-bootstrapped stats and peak concurrent exposure.\n\n## Defect 4 (material) \u2014 Headline stats are arithmetically inconsistent with the stated metric\n\nFor a **hold-to-expiry** short strangle with \u00b13% wings, winners keep a *large*\nfraction of premium (index inside the strikes \u2192 both legs expire worthless \u2192\n\u2248 +100% of premium). Under `net_pnl_pct_premium` = pnl \u00f7 premium, the reported\ncombo is impossible:\n\n- With hit = 79% and worst trade = \u221221% (so any loser's magnitude \u2264 21%), the\n  maximum possible **average winning trade** is only **\u2248 +7.7% of premium**\n  (solved: `avg_win_max = (1.7 \u2212 0.21\u00b7(\u221221)) / 0.79`). A hold-to-expiry winner\n  keeping ~7% of premium is not credible \u2014 winners cluster near +100%.\n- Conversely, if winners realistically keep ~90% of premium, reconciling to a\n  +1.7% average would require the average *loser* to be **\u2248 \u2212330% of premium** \u2014\n  yet the worst single trade is quoted as only \u221221%. Contradiction.\n\nSo either (a) the metric is **mislabeled** \u2014 the \"+1.7%\" is almost certainly a %\nof notional/margin, not % of premium collected \u2014 or (b) the numbers are\nmiscomputed/fabricated. As written the headline cannot be trusted.\n\n- **Fix:** state the P&L normalization explicitly (\u00f7 premium collected vs \u00f7\n  underlying notional vs \u00f7 margin posted) and recompute; the three give wildly\n  different numbers and the current label is self-contradictory.\n\n## Verification items (NOT confirmed defects \u2014 `sell_weekly_strangle` is a black box)\n\n- **Expiry-day settle:** if the exit uses the option's expiry-day `settle` on a\n  bhavcopy-schema dataset, it will hit landmine #9 (SETTLE_PR on expiry day is\n  the underlying's settlement level, not the option price). Confirm the exit\n  cash-settles at intrinsic from the underlying.\n- **Costs/slippage:** no evidence brokerage/slippage/impact are applied; for a\n  premium-selling rule net-of-cost matters. Confirm COST_STANDARDS are wired in.\n- These are flagged for verification, not scored as material defects here.\n\n---\n\n## Numbered list of claimed material defects\n\n1. **Full-sample z-score lookahead** \u2014 `mu`/`sd` computed over all of 2015-2025\n   and applied to every day; the signal depends on future data and is not\n   implementable live. Fix: trailing expanding/rolling z or trailing IV\n   percentile, shifted by one bar. (Fatal.)\n2. **Hindsight-calibrated \"crash filter\"** \u2014 `iv_z < 2.5` excludes Mar-2020 only\n   because the full-sample `sd` is inflated by Mar-2020; the \u221221% worst trade is\n   an artifact and true tail risk (multiples of premium) is defined away. Fix:\n   trailing calibration and report the tail with the crash included. (Fatal.)\n3. **Overlapping positions counted as independent** \u2014 no cooldown / one-at-a-time\n   control; clustered signals inflate N, overstate hit-rate precision, and\n   understate concurrent-position drawdown. Fix: enforce non-overlap and/or\n   block-bootstrap the stats.\n4. **Headline stats inconsistent with the stated metric** \u2014 +1.7% avg / 79% hit /\n   \u221221% worst is arithmetically impossible if \"% of premium\" = pnl\u00f7premium for a\n   hold-to-expiry strangle (implied max avg winner \u2248 +7.7%). Metric is mislabeled\n   or numbers are wrong. Fix: define the normalization precisely and recompute.\n\n----- ANSWER O15b -----\n# T15 Review \u2014 IV-percentile strangle seller\n\n## Verdict\nThis result is **not trustworthy**. There is a fatal look-ahead defect in the core signal construction, plus a curve-fit crash filter, a daily-firing/overlap problem that fakes the sample size, and a worst-case number that is inconsistent with what the strategy actually holds. Details below.\n\n---\n\n## Material defects\n\n### 1. Full-sample standardization = look-ahead on every single trade (FATAL) [DATA/INFERENCE]\n```python\nmu = hist[\"iv\"].mean()      # mean over ALL of 2015-2025\nsd = hist[\"iv\"].std()       # std over ALL of 2015-2025\nhist[\"iv_z\"] = (hist[\"iv\"] - mu) / sd\nhist[\"entry\"] = (hist[\"iv_z\"] > 1.0) & (hist[\"iv_z\"] < 2.5)\n```\n`mu` and `sd` are computed once over the entire 2015-2025 series and then used to standardize every day, including days in 2015 and 2016. The z-score that decides a 2016 entry embeds the mean and standard deviation of IV *through 2025*. This is textbook normalization look-ahead (D-028 / T-class: future statistics leaking into a past signal). It is not a marginal bias \u2014 **at the time of every trade before the end of the sample the required `mu`/`sd` did not exist**, so 100% of the 214 entries are contaminated, and the entry set itself (which days clear the band) is only knowable ex-post.\n\nThis also directly contradicts the author's own stated goal (\"standardize so the rule generalizes across vol regimes\"). A single full-sample z-score is the *opposite* of regime-adaptive: it bakes the whole-period distribution into one fixed anchor.\n\n**Mechanism of corruption:** the entry mask, the entry count (214), the hit rate (79%), and the mean (+1.7%) are all computed on a signal that cannot be reproduced in real time. The backtest is unrunnable as a live rule.\n\n**Fix:** standardize with a *trailing, point-in-time* window only \u2014 expanding mean/std with a minimum lookback (e.g. `hist[\"iv\"].expanding(min_periods=252).mean()`/`.std()`) or a rolling trailing window (e.g. 252d), evaluated as-of day *d* using only data \u2264 *d*. Then re-run; expect the entry set, count, and all stats to change.\n\n### 2. The \"crash filter\" is itself look-ahead + hindsight-fit [INFERENCE]\nThe upper bound `iv_z < 2.5` (the claimed \"crash filter\" that \"skipped Mar-2020\") is defined in terms of the *contaminated full-sample* `sd`, so it inherits defect #1. Worse, the 2.5 cutoff conveniently lands just below the single most famous IV blow-up in the sample. Choosing a threshold that is known to exclude the one event that would have produced the largest loss is hindsight selection, not a filter. Note the second-order effect: the full-sample `sd` is *inflated* by the Mar-2020 spike itself, which **compresses every other day's z-score** and can pull pre-crash elevated-vol days *into* the 1.0-2.5 entry band \u2014 i.e. into positions right before the drawdown \u2014 in a way only knowable after the fact.\n\n**Fix:** define the regime exclusion on point-in-time information (trailing IV percentile computed as-of *d*) and **pre-register** the cutoff before seeing outcomes. Do not tune it to dodge a known crash.\n\n### 3. A \"weekly\" rule fired daily \u2192 overlapping, autocorrelated pseudo-trades [INFERENCE]\n```python\nfor d in hist.index[hist[\"entry\"]]:\n    t = sell_weekly_strangle(signal_day=d, ...)\n```\nThe loop iterates every *daily* row where `entry` is true and opens a fresh weekly strangle each time. IV is highly persistent, so `iv_z` in the band clusters into runs of consecutive days. This produces:\n- **Multiple concurrent, overlapping weekly strangles** from adjacent signal days \u2014 not the 214 independent bets the headline implies.\n- **Serial correlation / pseudo-replication:** the effective number of independent bets is far below 214, so the 79% hit rate and the significance of +1.7% are overstated (block-correlated outcomes counted as independent).\n- **Understated concentration risk:** stacking overlapping short strangles in a high-IV cluster is a single concentrated short-vol bet, not diversification.\n\n**Fix:** enforce the weekly cadence \u2014 one position at a time, or dedupe to non-overlapping expiries (skip a new entry while one is live). Report effective sample size and use block-bootstrap / Newey-West stats.\n\n### 4. Worst trade of only -21% of premium is inconsistent with the strategy and needs a hard tail audit [INFERENCE]\nA naked short ~3%-OTM (0.97/1.03) weekly strangle on NIFTY, held to expiry across 2015-2025, sits through many >3% weekly moves (Aug-2015, demonetization/Brexit 2016, Feb & Oct 2018, 2022 hikes, plus the 2020 ramp). When a wing is breached, a short strangle routinely loses **well in excess of 100% of premium collected**. A reported worst case of **-21% of premium over ten years** is not credible for this vehicle. Given defect #2's mechanism (inflated `sd` pulling elevated-vol days into the band before crashes), one would expect *more* bad tails, not fewer. So the benign tail points to one of: (a) the crash filter (built on look-ahead) is silently stripping all bad outcomes = overfit, or (b) the P&L engine is mis-accounting losses.\n\n**Fix:** audit `sell_weekly_strangle`'s settlement. In particular beware the firm's expiry-day landmine (F&O bhavcopy `SETTLE_PR` on expiry = the *underlying's* settlement level, not the option price) \u2014 cash-settle each leg at intrinsic from the underlying, gate on `CONTRACTS>0`. Then report the *unfiltered* tail alongside the filtered one.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material comments (labelled)\n- **Entry timing is done correctly** \u2014 signal on day *d* (a daily/close IV value), fill at `next_open`. This avoids same-bar look-ahead and is the one clean part; not a defect.\n- **`liquidity=(\"both_legs_traded\",)`** could induce mild selection if illiquidity correlates with stress days, but for NIFTY index weeklies (~3% wings) both legs are normally liquid, so this is low-impact. [OPINION] Non-material.\n- **\"% of premium\" denominator** [OPINION]: reporting +1.7% *of premium* per trade obscures return-on-capital (short strangles are margin-intensive with an asymmetric tail). This is a reporting/decision-quality issue, not a correctness bug, but the edge should be quoted on margin/capital before any IC read.\n\n---\n\n## Numbered list of claimed material defects\n1. **Full-sample `mu`/`sd` standardization** \u2014 injects future statistics into every signal; all 214 entries are look-ahead-contaminated and the rule is unrunnable live. Fix: trailing/expanding point-in-time normalization.\n2. **`iv_z < 2.5` \"crash filter\"** \u2014 inherits the full-sample look-ahead and is hindsight-calibrated to exclude the known Mar-2020 blow-up. Fix: point-in-time trailing-percentile filter with a pre-registered threshold.\n3. **Daily firing of a weekly rule** \u2014 persistent IV clusters produce overlapping, serially correlated positions; 214 \"entries\" are pseudo-replicated, inflating hit rate/mean/significance and hiding concentration. Fix: non-overlapping weekly sampling + block-bootstrap stats.\n4. **Implausible -21%-of-premium worst case** \u2014 inconsistent with a naked short 3% strangle over 2015-2025; signals either overfit tail-stripping by the contaminated filter or a broken loss/settlement engine. Fix: audit cash-settlement (expiry `SETTLE_PR` landmine) and report the unfiltered tail.\n\n----- ANSWER O15c -----\n# T15 \u2014 CIO Consolidated Verdict: IV-percentile strangle seller\n\n**VERDICT: REJECT (result is FAKE / not tradeable as reported).**\n\nRationale (3 lines):\n1. The submission's core \"normal IV level\" is standardized against full-sample `mu`/`sd` computed over all of 2015\u20132025, so every entry decision reads future IV \u2014 a whole-period lookahead that alone invalidates the headline (214 entries, +1.7%, 79%, \u221221% worst).\n2. The \"crash filter\" that skips Mar-2020 is not a separate mechanism; it is a downstream symptom of that same contaminated `sd` (dominated by the 2020 spike), so the reported worst-case is laundered, not real.\n3. Independently, the loop opens a weekly strangle on every signal day with no concurrency/cooldown guard, so \"214 entries\" massively overstates the independent bet count and multi-counts the very tail a short-vol book must fear.\n\nBetween the two reviews: the Red Team is correct on the arbitration. Both reviewers nail the fatal defect. The prior reviewer then over-claimed \u2014 it promoted three unseen-engine speculations (expiry-settle, denominator, next_open fill) to \"material defects\" while itself conceding they are \"not confirmed bugs\" and \"I can't confirm.\" A defect I cannot prove from the evidence in hand is a verification QUESTION, not a kill. I adopt the Red Team's discipline: keep only defects provable from the code shown, demote the rest to blocking questions, and add the two the prior reviewer under-weighted (sample overlap, position concurrency).\n\n## Tail-risk assessment (the part that matters for this book)\n- **Worst single-day:** A live PIT rule short a weekly strangle into the Feb\u2013Mar 2020 gap-down loses MULTIPLES of collected premium, not 21%. An expanding-window z reads BELOW 2.5 on the way up (mean/sd haven't yet absorbed the spike), so the live rule fires INTO the crash rather than skipping it. The reported \u221221% floor is the single most dangerous number in the submission \u2014 it is the exact \"calm-looking tail that only sizing survives\" pattern from our firm lessons.\n- **Worst month:** Undefined and unshowable until the rule is rebuilt PIT; the reported distribution excludes the one month that defines a short-vol seller's risk.\n- **Correlated-blowup scenario:** No concurrency guard means multiple overlapping weekly strangles can be simultaneously short into the same expiry. One 2020-scale move is then not \u221221% once but a stacked loss across every open position at once \u2014 a single-factor (index vol) correlated blowup, which is precisely the failure mode this office vetoes on sight.\n\n## Sizing ruling\nNo size. This does not reach an IC sizing discussion. It is a paper/R&D artifact that must be rebuilt from scratch before any number is quoted, per Lesson 2026-07-04 (paper approvals firewalled from sizing anchors). Even after a clean PIT rebuild, a naked short strangle held to weekly expiry through binary/vol events is charter-prohibited without a defined tail hedge (Kabir Anand to spec an overlay before any size).\n\n## Kill criteria + review date\nThis is REJECTED, not put in forward test, so no forward clock starts. To be resubmitted (not resurrected as-is), the rebuild MUST show all of:\n- (a) PIT expanding/trailing standardization (mu/sd from data strictly \u2264 signal day), re-run end to end;\n- (b) one-position-per-expiry dedup (or block re-entry while short), with effective-N and block-bootstrap 5th-percentile of the per-trade mean > 0;\n- (c) the LIVE worst trade through Feb\u2013Mar 2020 shown, not filtered \u2014 expect it to appear and to exceed 100% of premium;\n- (d) one-day-lag placebo that degrades the result (lookahead-audit pass, D-028);\n- (e) one worked intrinsic-settled expiry example (cash-settle at intrinsic from the underlying's official settlement \u2014 CLAUDE.md #9), proving the exit does not read an option SETTLE_PR;\n- (f) proof the `net_pnl_pct_premium` base is entry-collected credit and the `next_open` fill is a real \u226509:15 d+1 print for both legs (not a relabeled close/settle, not the 09:00 auction).\nReview date: only upon resubmission with (a)\u2013(f). No standing review; the current record stands as REJECTED.\n\n## Dissents\n- **Prior reviewer (dissent, partially sustained):** listed 5 material defects. I sustain #1 (full-sample z lookahead) and record its #5 (daily-source vs next_open) and #3 (expiry-settle) and #4 (denominator/booking) as VERIFICATION QUESTIONS, not material defects \u2014 the reviewer itself conceded they are unconfirmed and they concern engine code not in evidence. Its #2 (hindsight crash filter) is sustained as a CONSEQUENCE of #1, not an independent defect (no separate code to fix). Ruling: do not inflate the kill count on unseen code; the substantive tail-risk observation inside #2 is kept and elevated.\n- **Red Team (dissent, sustained as the controlling view):** one dispositive defect plus sample non-independence; the rest are questions. I adopt this framing and add its concurrency catch to the material list.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECTS (evidence-backed; provable from the code shown)\n\n1. **Full-sample z-score lookahead \u2014 FATAL, dispositive.**\n   Lines: `mu = hist[\"iv\"].mean()`, `sd = hist[\"iv\"].std()`, `hist[\"iv_z\"] = (hist[\"iv\"] - mu) / sd`, `hist[\"entry\"] = (hist[\"iv_z\"] > 1.0) & (hist[\"iv_z\"] < 2.5)`.\n   Mechanism: `mu` and `sd` are computed once over the entire 2015\u20132025 series, then used to score every historical day including 2015. Each entry decision uses future IV. `sd` is dominated by the 2020 vol explosion, so the `z<2.5` ceiling is ex-post calibrated to sit just below COVID, and the `z>1.0` trigger's timing/count is set by a future mean. Corrupts entry timing, entry count, hit rate, mean, AND the \"crash-skipped\" worst-case simultaneously \u2014 every reported number is future-conditioned. (This is a T1/T2 lookahead per LOOKAHEAD_CONTROLS; no result is interpretable until fixed.)\n   Fix: PIT standardization \u2014 expanding or trailing (e.g. 252-day) `mu_t`, `sd_t` from data strictly \u2264 the signal day, computed as of `d`; then re-run. Consequence to report explicitly: the reported \u221221% worst trade is not the live worst-case because the Mar-2020 exclusion is produced by the contaminated ceiling.\n\n2. **Sample non-independence / weekly overlap with no concurrency guard.**\n   Lines: the `for d in hist.index[hist[\"entry\"]]` loop \u2014 a trade is opened on every signal day, weekly holds, no cooldown, no \"already short\" check.\n   Mechanism: elevated-IV regimes persist for many consecutive days, so daily signals cluster into overlapping same-expiry positions. \"214 entries\" is nowhere near 214 independent bets \u2014 effective N is a small fraction \u2014 so \"hit 79%\" and \"+1.7% mean\" are averages over a heavily autocorrelated, regime-clustered sample. Worse, concurrent overlapping strangles multi-count a single tail event, turning one 2020-scale move into a stacked, correlated loss.\n   Fix: dedup to one live position per expiry (or block re-entry while short); report effective-N and block-bootstrap confidence intervals on the per-trade mean.\n\n## VERIFICATION QUESTIONS (NOT material defects \u2014 cannot be proven from the evidence; must be closed before any resubmission is trusted)\n- **[INFERENCE]** Does `hold_to_expiry` cash-settle each leg at intrinsic from the underlying's official settlement, or read an option SETTLE_PR? (CLAUDE.md #9 landmine caused \u221215,428-pt fake losses in A4 \u2014 but that was single-stock F&O bhavcopy; here the source is a daily IV parquet on the index, so the landmine may not apply. Show one worked expiry example.)\n- **[INFERENCE]** Is the `net_pnl_pct_premium` denominator the entry-collected credit (stable, bounded away from zero for a premium seller that passes the both-legs-traded gate) and not a collapsed spread? Very likely fine for a seller; confirm.\n- **[INFERENCE]** On a daily IV source, does `next_open` map to a real \u226509:15 d+1 option print for BOTH legs \u2014 not a relabeled close/settle (lookahead) and not the 09:00 pre-open auction (CLAUDE.md #2)? If no real fill exists, model no-fill = DROP (D-031). This is the prior review's best secondary catch, but it is a question about unseen engine code.\n\n## NON-MATERIAL COMMENTS (labelled)\n- **[non-material]** `liquidity=(\"both_legs_traded\",)` is good practice; on bhavcopy-derived legs confirm it gates on CONTRACTS>0, not merely a non-null/0.00 settle row (untraded-but-priced strikes exist, esp. 2020).\n- **[non-material]** No walk-forward / DSR / PBO reported. Parameter surface is small (z_low, z_high, wings), so overfit risk is modest, but the validation battery must accompany any resubmission.\n\n## Numbered list of MATERIAL defects\n1. Full-sample z-score standardization = whole-period lookahead (`mu`/`sd` over all 2015\u20132025 used to threshold every day; `sd` fixed by the 2020 spike calibrates the crash ceiling ex-post). Fatal \u2014 invalidates all reported numbers, including the \u221221% worst-case. Fix: PIT expanding/trailing mu/sd as of the signal day, then re-run.\n2. Sample non-independence / no concurrency guard (a trade per signal day, weekly holds, no dedup). Overstates independent-N behind \"79% hit\"/\"+1.7% mean\" and multi-counts the tail. Fix: one live position per expiry, report effective-N and block-bootstrap intervals.\n\n----- ANSWER O15d -----\n# T15 \u2014 Consolidated final verdict: IV-percentile strangle seller\n\n**Arbiter role:** reconcile the two reviews, keep only defects that survive scrutiny, drop restatements and refuted mechanisms. Worked from the pasted text only; no files read, no code run.\n\n**Bottom line:** the result is not reproducible and its headline numbers are internally inconsistent. Two independent material defects survive, plus one that is fatal on its own. The reported figures (214 entries, +1.7%, 79% hit, \u221221% worst) must not be quoted until all three are fixed and the run is repeated.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 1 \u2014 Full-sample (look-ahead) standardization. Fatal, primary.\n\nLines:\n```\nmu = hist[\"iv\"].mean()\nsd = hist[\"iv\"].std()\nhist[\"iv_z\"] = (hist[\"iv\"] - mu) / sd\nhist[\"entry\"] = (hist[\"iv_z\"] > 1.0) & (hist[\"iv_z\"] < 2.5)\n```\n`mu` and `sd` are computed once over the entire 2015-2025 series and then applied to every day, including the earliest days. The z-score used to decide a trade in, say, Feb-2016 is built from a mean/std that incorporate data through 2025.\n\n**Mechanism.** The z-transform is strictly monotonic in raw IV, so the gate is exactly the fixed raw-IV band `mu + 1.0\u00b7sd < iv < mu + 2.5\u00b7sd` \u2014 a band whose two edges are calibrated with full-sample hindsight. On day *d* in real time you do not yet know the 2015-2025 mean/std, so this signal could not have been computed. Every one of the 214 entry decisions peeks at the future; the entry set and all downstream stats are not reproducible out-of-sample. It also contradicts the author's stated rationale: a single global ruler is the opposite of \"generalizing across vol regimes.\"\n\n**Framing correction (from the red-team pass, adopted):** this is 2-parameter look-ahead, not a multi-parameter curve fit. Call it what it is \u2014 in-sample normalization / look-ahead \u2014 not \"overfitting.\"\n\n**Fix.** Baseline from history available strictly before each entry day (expanding or trailing window, lagged one bar so day *d*'s own IV cannot leak into its own threshold), then re-run:\n```\nw = hist[\"iv\"].rolling(504, min_periods=252)   # ~1y burn-in, ~2y window\nhist[\"iv_z\"] = (hist[\"iv\"] - w.mean().shift(1)) / w.std().shift(1)\n```\nEntry count, +1.7%, 79%, and \u221221% will all move; the current numbers are void.\n\n**Corollary (the prior review's \"defect #2\" \u2014 NOT counted separately).** The `iv_z < 2.5` crash cap and the \"Mar-2020 skipped by the crash filter\" claim share the identical root cause: the full-sample `sd` is inflated by the very Mar-2020 spike it purports to exclude, which is what makes the 2.5 cap \"happen\" to land where it does. This is the same defect restated, so it is folded into #1, not double-counted. Keep the useful observation: under a real-time expanding `sd` (small pre-crash), IV in late-Feb/early-Mar 2020 would rocket up through the 1.0-2.5 band on the way up, so the strategy could have ENTERED into the vol explosion rather than cleanly skipped it. The clean \"Mar-2020 skipped\" property is a hindsight artifact and may reverse once #1 is fixed. Re-verify after the rolling baseline is in place.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 2 \u2014 Headline stats are internally inconsistent, and the \u221221% worst is implausibly benign.\n\nClaims: `avg +1.7% of premium`, `hit 79%`, `worst trade \u221221% of premium`, `exit_rule=(\"hold_to_expiry\",)`, naked \u00b13% weekly short strangle.\n\n**Arithmetic inconsistency (decisive).** Under the natural reading of `net_pnl_pct_premium` (P&L as a fraction of premium collected, held to expiry), a week that expires between the strikes returns \u2248 +100% of premium. With a 79% hit rate the winners alone contribute \u2248 +79% to the mean. For the mean to be only +1.7%, the average loser would have to be \u2248 \u2212368% of premium (0.79\u00b7100 + 0.21\u00b7L = 1.7 \u21d2 L \u2248 \u2212368%). An average loser near \u2212368% cannot coexist with a stated worst trade of \u221221%. The three headline numbers are mutually contradictory under the \"% of premium\" unit.\n\n**Independent plausibility check.** \u221221% is far too benign for a naked \u00b13% weekly short strangle held to expiry over 2015-2025 even with Mar-2020 removed. Non-crash weeks that the `iv_z < 2.5` cap would NOT filter and that breach a 3% wing include Aug-2015 (CNY deval), Feb-2016, the Nov-2016 demonetization week, Feb-2018, and especially the Jun-2024 election-result week (~\u22126% on result day, elevated-but-sub-2.5\u03c3 pre-event IV \u2014 a textbook in-band entry). A ~5% weekly move past a 3% wing is ~2% intrinsic against ~1-1.5% premium, i.e. roughly \u221250% to \u221270% of premium on the breached leg. Several such weeks exist; a \u221221% worst is not credible.\n\n**Two candidate explanations \u2014 must be pinned before any number is quoted:**\n- **(a) Mislabeled unit.** The values may be returns on notional/spot, not on premium (a between-strikes week \u2248 +1% of notional; a big-move week \u2248 \u221221% of notional). This fits the +1.7%/79%/\u221221% pattern self-consistently. If so, \"avg +1.7% of premium\" is a misnomer \u2014 and materially misleading: +1.7% of *premium* weekly is trivial, whereas +1.7% of *notional* weekly is enormous. A reader would misjudge the edge either way.\n- **(b) Mispriced / capped exits inside `sell_weekly_strangle`.** The exit pricing is not shown. Note the reconciliation the two reviews reached: the prior review's specific \"expiry SETTLE_PR read as the underlying's settlement so tail losses vanish\" landmine is REFUTED by the data pattern \u2014 that bug erases losses and would push the mean strongly positive (tens of %), not +1.7%. So that exact mechanism does not fit. The residual, legitimate concern is that this is the cash-settled index and hold-to-expiry legs must be settled at **intrinsic value vs the settlement level**; verify `sell_weekly_strangle` does this and does not apply a loss cap or partial-exit that truncates tails.\n\n**Fix.** State the exact P&L denominator; inspect `sell_weekly_strangle`'s expiry settlement; then reconcile mean / hit / worst on one explicit unit. The \u221221% worst is the pivot that discriminates (a) from (b).\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 3 \u2014 Overlapping, high-IV-clustered daily entries make the 214 non-independent.\n\nThe loop fires on **every** day with `iv_z` in band. Because IV is strongly autocorrelated (vol clustering), in-band days come in runs, and each consecutive in-band day sells a fresh weekly strangle. The result is many simultaneously open positions, frequently sharing the same expiry with highly correlated outcomes.\n\n**Mechanism / consequences.** (1) \"hit 79%\" and \"mean +1.7%\" are computed as if 214 independent bets; the effective sample size is far smaller, so the statistical confidence is badly overstated (and a single bad week can register as many correlated \"losses\" or \"wins\"). (2) The concurrent margin/capital required to hold the stacked positions is unmodeled, so realism and capacity are misrepresented.\n\n**Fix.** De-overlap before quoting statistics \u2014 one open position per expiry (or non-overlapping holding windows) \u2014 and report hit/mean/worst on the independent trade set; model concurrent margin for capacity.\n\n---\n\n## Confirmed non-defects (do not manufacture problems here)\n- **Entry timing is correct.** Signal on day *d*, fill at `next_open`, is a proper one-day lag on an EOD IV value. Both reviews agree; no defect.\n- **Liquidity gate is appropriate.** `both_legs_traded` correctly skips untraded-strike contamination. Good.\n\n## Non-material comments (labelled as such)\n- Band edges `(1.0, 2.5)` and wings `(0.97, 1.03)` are hand-chosen with no train/test split; overfit *exposure* remains even after #1 is fixed \u2014 hygiene comment, not a confirmed defect.\n- Premise-level adverse selection: entering only on high-IV days loads pre-event/pre-crisis weeks where high IV is often justified by high subsequent realized vol, so \"IV rich\" \u2260 \"vol overpriced.\" Interpretation caveat, not a coding defect.\n- Costs cannot be confirmed from the bare `\"net\"`; IV-source cleanliness of `nifty_iv_daily.parquet` is unassessable from the text.\n\n---\n\n## Numbered list of material defects\n1. **Full-sample look-ahead standardization** \u2014 `mu`/`sd` computed over all of 2015-2025 and applied to every day; the entry gate is a hindsight-calibrated raw-IV band, unknowable in real time. Voids reproducibility of the 214 entries and every headline stat. Fix: rolling/expanding, one-bar-lagged baseline, then re-run. (The prior review's \"crash-filter\" item is the same defect and is folded in here, not counted separately.)\n2. **Internally inconsistent + implausibly benign headline P&L** \u2014 a +1.7% mean with a 79% hit rate implies an average loser \u2248 \u2212368% of premium, which cannot coexist with a \u221221% worst; and \u221221% is not credible for a naked \u00b13% hold-to-expiry weekly strangle (Jun-2024, Feb-2018, Nov-2016, etc.). The unit is either mislabeled (likely returns on notional) or the exits are mispriced/capped. The prior review's specific \"expiry-settle erases losses\" mechanism is refuted by the low mean; the residual concern is intrinsic settlement inside `sell_weekly_strangle`. Fix: pin the P&L denominator and inspect the expiry-settlement path before quoting anything.\n3. **Overlapping, high-IV-clustered daily entries** \u2014 the 214 trades are not independent (vol clustering \u21d2 stacked positions sharing correlated expiries); \"79% hit\" and \"+1.7% mean\" have grossly overstated significance and the concurrent margin/capital is unmodeled. Fix: de-overlap to one position per expiry and report on independent trades.", "ids": ["O15a", "O15b", "O15c", "O15d"]}, {"tid": "T16", "key": "## T16 \u2014 statistics \u2014 turnover-cost confound (HIGH)\n**Defect.** The hurdle (monthly-refresh random baskets, ~330%/yr one-way) churns\n~8.7x the strategy (semiannual, 38%/yr); both are charged 45bp/side. Cost drag:\nhurdle ~2.97pp/yr vs strategy ~0.34pp/yr, so ~2.6pp of the claimed +3.1pp \"net edge\"\nis the comparator paying more costs. Gross-vs-gross (the memo's own table) the edge\nis +0.3pp \u2014 noise. The statistical gates passed because they test the number, not\nthe comparator. Certifying \"+3.1pp selection edge\" is wrong.\n**Mechanism.** un-matched turnover between strategy and null.\n**Fix.** Turnover-matched placebo (semiannually-refreshed random baskets), or\ncompare gross-vs-gross and charge strategy costs explicitly. Full credit requires\ndoing (or gesturing at) the drag arithmetic or citing the gross rows.\n**Accept.** \"The hurdle pays ~2.6pp more costs \u2014 the net edge is cost savings, not\nselection\"; \"compare against a turnover-matched null; gross edge is only 0.3pp\";\n\"low-churn strategies always 'beat' full-churn random baskets net of costs\".\n**Secondary.** Noting that p95-clearing also collapses under matching: correct,\nbonus.\n", "answers": "----- ANSWER O16a -----\n# T16 \u2014 Consolidated final verdict: quality-tilt certification memo\n\n**VERDICT: REJECT certification.** The claimed \"+3.1pp/yr selection edge over segment\nrandom\" is an artifact of an unfair benchmark, not skill. Do NOT register.\n\nBoth source reviews reach the correct headline. I adopt the substance they agree on,\ndiscard the first reviewer's broken arithmetic and fabricated post-fix numbers (the red\nteam is right to flag these), and keep only what the memo text actually supports.\n\n---\n\n## Material defects (survive)\n\n### 1. Rebalance-frequency / turnover mismatch under an identical per-side cost model \u2014 the killer.\n\nThe two arms are charged the same 45bp/side but trade at radically different frequencies:\n\n- Strategy: **semiannual**, one-way turnover **38%/yr**.\n- Hurdle (random baskets): **monthly refresh**, one-way turnover **~330%/yr**.\n\nThe observed gross\u2192net gaps confirm the arms are bled at completely different rates:\n- Strategy: 15.0% \u2192 14.6% \u2248 **40bp/yr** drag.\n- Random p50: 14.7% \u2192 11.5% \u2248 **320bp/yr** drag.\n- Random p95: 16.9% \u2192 13.7% \u2248 **320bp/yr** drag.\n\n**Mechanism.** ~9x more rebalancing at the same per-trade cost bleeds the hurdle ~300bp/yr\nwhile the strategy loses ~40bp/yr. That ~280bp cost differential is a *rebalance-schedule\nchoice*, not stock-selection skill. The entire net outperformance is manufactured by it.\nNo one would run a passive random 40-name basket on a monthly forced refresh; the correct\nnull for a semiannual strategy is a semiannual random draw.\n\n**Reconciliation note (correcting the first reviewer).** The drag reconciles only under a\n**round-trip** convention (buy + sell). One-way turnover T \u21d2 2\u00b7T of book traded \u21d2 drag \u2248\n2\u00b7T\u00b745bp. Strategy: 2\u00b70.38\u00b745 \u2248 34bp \u2248 observed ~40bp. Hurdle: 2\u00b73.30\u00b745 \u2248 297bp \u2248 observed\n~320bp. The first reviewer's one-way figure (0.38\u00b745 = 17bp) does NOT reconcile the strategy's\nown 40bp gap and should not be relied on; the round-trip convention reconciles both arms and\nstill yields the same conclusion.\n\n**Fix.** Rebalance the hurdle on the **same schedule as the strategy (semiannual)** \u2014 or\nmatch turnover \u2014 *before* charging costs, then re-run. A semiannual random basket would carry\nturnover in the strategy's ballpark (drift + occasional segment/liquidity drops, nowhere near\n330%), collapsing its cost drag from ~300bp to tens of bp.\n\n**Do NOT quote a specific corrected hurdle number.** The first reviewer's \"net p50 ~14.4%,\np95 ~16.5%, strategy fails to clear the median\" are fabricated \u2014 a semiannual random basket is\na *different set of portfolios with a different return distribution*, not the monthly baskets\nminus a cost line. Gross return is not invariant to rebalance schedule for a random strategy.\nThe honest statement is: the corrected hurdle is **unknown until re-run**; it might fail to\nclear even the median, it might barely clear \u2014 the memo does not let us say which.\n\n### 2. No gross selection edge \u2014 the memo's own numbers contradict the claim.\n\nOn GROSS CAGR, before any cost treatment:\n- Strategy 15.0% vs random p50 14.7% = **+0.3pp** (a rounding-scale edge over the median).\n- Strategy 15.0% vs random p95 **16.9%** = the strategy **LOSES** to the 95th percentile.\n\nSo the strategy sits *inside* the random-selection distribution and is below its upper tail.\nThe +3.1pp appears only after the turnover-driven cost gap is applied. A +0.3pp gross edge over\nthe median, quoted with no standard error, is an order of magnitude below the +3.1pp registered\nclaim and is not distinguishable from noise without a t-stat.\n\n### 3. The certification claim rests entirely on the unfair net figures.\n\n\"Clears even the 95th percentile (14.6% vs 13.7%)\" and \"+3.1pp/yr expected outperformance\" are\ntrue only on the turnover-inflated net numbers. Under a schedule-/turnover-matched hurdle the\nedge is unproven and likely collapses. Registering +3.1pp would enshrine an edge that does not\nsurvive a fair comparison. Reject; re-run against a matched hurdle.\n\n### 4. DSR / PBO / plateau gates do not validate \u2014 and may be contaminated by \u2014 the benchmark.\n\nDSR 0.98, PBO 22%, plateau, and lookahead 0-FAIL are statements about the *strategy's own*\nbacktest robustness. They are silent on whether the *benchmark comparison* is fair, so they give\nfalse comfort against Defect 1. **Worse:** if the firm's DSR/PBO null is built against the same\nmonthly-refresh random-basket series, DSR 0.98 is itself inflated by the turnover artifact \u2014 the\nstrategy looks abnormal versus a cost-crippled null. The null used for DSR must be verified before\nthe stat is quoted at all.\n\n### 5. No t-stat / confidence interval anywhere.\n\nRegistering \"+3.1pp expected outperformance\" as a point estimate with zero dispersion, over\n~18\u201320 semiannual rebalances (2016\u20132025), is not statistically supportable and violates\nestimate-labeling conduct (an unlabeled point estimate presented as fact). A bootstrap CI /\nt-stat on the outperformance is mandatory before any registration. This also fixes the\ncategory error in the \"reading\": a single strategy path compared to the p50/p95 of 10,000\nbaskets is not a significance statement unless construction is matched and dispersion is reported.\n\n---\n\n## Material-pending-check\n\n### 6. Survivorship direction of the random baskets (opposite-signed to Defect 1).\n\nIf the \"standing random series\" draws from *current* mid/small membership while the strategy uses\na PIT survivorship-clean snapshot, the hurdle is survivorship-inflated **upward** \u2014 biasing the\nmeasured edge *downward*, the opposite direction from Defect 1. Both biases are unquantified, so\nthe sign of a corrected re-run is genuinely uncertain. Must confirm both arms draw from the same\npoint-in-time universe. (Upgraded from the first reviewer's non-material note.)\n\n---\n\n## Non-material comments (labelled)\n\n- **[non-material] Per-name cost asymmetry.** Even with matched schedules, a random mid/small\n  basket may carry worse per-name impact/liquidity than a profitability + low-leverage screen\n  (quality names skew larger/more liquid). This should be modeled per-name, not assumed symmetric \u2014\n  but it is second-order versus the 330% turnover gap. A check, not a proven defect.\n- **[non-material] Era-split decay.** 2016\u20132025 spans very different regimes for a low-churn factor\n  sleeve; an era-split on the outperformance is worth having before registration, though not a\n  correctness defect in what is shown.\n\n---\n\n## Numbered list of material defects\n\n1. **Rebalance-frequency / turnover mismatch under an identical per-side cost model** (strategy\n   semiannual ~38% one-way; hurdle monthly ~330% one-way). Same 45bp/side bleeds the hurdle\n   ~300bp/yr vs the strategy ~40bp/yr; the entire +3.1pp net spread is this cost artifact, not\n   selection skill. Fix: match the hurdle's schedule (semiannual) / match turnover before charging\n   costs, then re-run. Do not quote a specific corrected hurdle \u2014 it is unknown until re-run.\n2. **No gross selection edge.** Gross, strategy 15.0% \u2248 random p50 14.7% (+0.3pp) and LOSES to\n   random p95 16.9%; the strategy is inside the random distribution. The edge exists only post-cost.\n3. **Certification claim rests entirely on the turnover-inflated net figures** (\"+3.1pp\", \"beats\n   p95\"); it does not survive a matched-turnover hurdle. Reject / re-run.\n4. **DSR / PBO / plateau / lookahead gates do not validate benchmark fairness** and \u2014 if the DSR/PBO\n   null is the same monthly-refresh random series \u2014 may themselves be contaminated by the turnover\n   artifact. Verify the null before quoting DSR 0.98.\n5. **No t-stat / confidence interval** on the +3.1pp; a point estimate with no dispersion over\n   ~18\u201320 rebalances cannot be registered as \"expected outperformance.\"\n6. **[pending-check] Survivorship direction of the random baskets:** if drawn from current (not PIT)\n   membership while the strategy is PIT, the hurdle is inflated upward, biasing the edge the opposite\n   way from Defect 1. Corrected sign is uncertain until both arms are confirmed on the same PIT universe.\n\n----- ANSWER O16b -----\n# T16 review \u2014 quality-tilt certification memo\n\n**Verdict: MATERIAL DEFECT. Do not certify at +3.1pp. The claimed \"selection edge\" is a turnover/cost artifact of an unfair hurdle.**\n\n## The core defect: rebalance-frequency (turnover) mismatch between strategy and hurdle\n\nThe two arms are NOT charged like-for-like, despite the memo's claim of \"identical cost assumptions.\" The cost *rate* is identical (45bp/side), but the cost *base* \u2014 turnover \u2014 is wildly different by construction:\n\n- Strategy: semiannual rebalance, **one-way turnover 38%/yr**\n- Random-basket hurdle: **refreshed monthly**, **one-way turnover ~330%/yr**\n\nA monthly re-draw of a *random* 40-name basket shares almost no names with the prior month, so it mechanically incurs ~10x the turnover of a persistent quality screen. Charging both at 45bp/side then penalizes the hurdle ~10x harder. That cost gap \u2014 not stock selection \u2014 is what produces the headline number.\n\n## Decomposition (the memo's own numbers prove it)\n\n| arm | gross | net | cost drag |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Quality-tilt (semiannual, 38% turnover) | 15.0% | 14.6% | **0.4pp** |\n| Random p50 (monthly, 330% turnover) | 14.7% | 11.5% | **3.2pp** |\n| Random p95 (monthly, 330% turnover) | 16.9% | 13.7% | **3.2pp** |\n\n- Net \"edge\" over median = 14.6 \u2212 11.5 = **+3.1pp**.\n- Of that, gross (true selection) difference = 15.0 \u2212 14.7 = **+0.3pp**; cost-drag difference = 3.2 \u2212 0.4 = **+2.8pp**.\n- **0.3 + 2.8 = 3.1.** Exactly 2.8 of the 3.1pp \"edge\" is the turnover-cost asymmetry. Only 0.3pp is selection.\n\nThe cost drags are internally consistent with the stated turnover (\u2248 one-way% \u00d7 2 sides \u00d7 45bp: strategy \u2248 34bp \u2248 0.4pp; hurdle \u2248 297bp \u2248 3.2pp), which confirms the gap is purely mechanical, not skill.\n\n## The falsification: on a like-for-like basis the strategy FAILS its own hurdle\n\nSelection edge is a **gross-return** concept (it must be measured before the cost confound). Gross:\n\n- vs median: 15.0% vs 14.7% \u2192 **+0.3pp** (indistinguishable from the middle of the random distribution).\n- vs p95: 15.0% vs **16.9%** \u2192 the strategy is **1.9pp BELOW the 95th percentile**. It does not clear it; it sits near the median of random draws.\n\nThe memo's claim that it \"clears even the 95th percentile (14.6% vs 13.7%)\" is true only *net*, and only because the p95 basket was docked 3.2pp of monthly-churn costs while the strategy was docked 0.4pp. Remove the artifact and the p95 claim inverts.\n\n## Correct fix\n\nTo isolate selection edge, the random-basket hurdle must be constructed on the **same rebalance schedule as the strategy** (semiannual \u2014 and if the intent is to also credit the persistence/low-churn advantage, use a *held* / buy-and-hold random basket so its turnover is comparable, not a monthly re-draw). Equivalently/additionally, run the primary comparison **gross-to-gross**.\n\nEither way the result collapses:\n- True selection edge \u2248 **+0.3pp/yr gross** vs random median \u2014 economically negligible and inside the noise band.\n- Strategy **fails the p95 gross hurdle** (15.0% < 16.9%).\n\nRegistering \"+3.1pp/yr expected outperformance over segment random\" would bank a cost artifact as forward alpha; it will not repeat, because it reflects an arbitrary monthly re-draw imposed on the benchmark, not a repeatable stock-picking edge.\n\nSecondary point (not a rescue): a genuine *low-churn cost advantage* is a real economic benefit, but (a) it is not \"selection edge,\" and (b) it can only be sized against a like-turnover hurdle \u2014 which the memo does not provide. Certify nothing until the hurdle is turnover-matched.\n\n## Non-material comments (labelled)\n\n- DSR 0.98 / PBO 22% / plateau / lookahead 0-FAIL are stated as passed and are not verifiable from the memo; on their face none is a red flag, though DSR 0.98 is high for ~10yr of semiannual data and worth a sanity check on trials count. Non-material to this review \u2014 the certification fails on the hurdle-construction defect regardless of these gates.\n\n## Material defects (numbered)\n\n1. **Turnover/rebalance-frequency mismatch in the hurdle.** Strategy turns over 38%/yr (semiannual) vs hurdle 330%/yr (monthly refresh), both charged 45bp/side. This is NOT \"identical cost assumptions\" \u2014 it is a ~10x cost-base asymmetry that manufactures the edge. Mechanism: 2.8pp of the 3.1pp net gap is pure cost differential, only 0.3pp is gross selection. Fix: match hurdle rebalance frequency to the strategy (semiannual, held basket) and/or compare gross-to-gross.\n\n2. **The \"clears the 95th percentile\" claim is false on a like-for-like basis.** Gross, the strategy (15.0%) sits ~1.9pp *below* the random p95 (16.9%) and only ~+0.3pp above the median \u2014 i.e., it does not beat the hurdle once the cost artifact is removed. The +3.1pp registration figure is unsupported; the honest selection number is ~+0.3pp/yr gross and fails p95. Do not certify.\n\n----- ANSWER O16c -----\n# T16 \u2014 Review: quality-tilt certification memo (arm B)\n\n**Verdict: DO NOT CERTIFY. The \"+3.1pp/yr selection edge\" is a cost artifact, not\nselection skill. The comparison is rigged by an apples-to-oranges turnover mismatch.**\n\n---\n\n## The core defect: rebalance-cadence / turnover mismatch between test and hurdle\n\n- Strategy: rebalanced **semiannually**, measured one-way turnover **38%/yr**.\n- Random-basket hurdle: **refreshed monthly**, measured one-way turnover **~330%/yr**.\n- Both arms charged the **same** 45bp/side cost.\n\nCharging an *identical per-unit* cost to two arms whose turnover differs **8.7x**\n(330 / 38) does not produce a fair comparison \u2014 it produces a systematic handicap on\nthe higher-turnover arm. The entire net gap is that handicap.\n\n**Mechanism, verified numerically** (annual drag \u2248 2 \u00d7 one-way-turnover \u00d7 45bp):\n\n| arm | turnover | predicted drag | observed drag (gross\u2192net) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Quality-tilt | 38%/yr | 0.34pp | 15.0\u219214.6 = 0.40pp |\n| Hurdle p50 | 330%/yr | 2.97pp | 14.7\u219211.5 = 3.20pp |\n| Hurdle p95 | 330%/yr | 2.97pp | 16.9\u219213.7 = 3.20pp |\n\nThe net-drag ratio (3.20 / 0.40 = **8.0x**) tracks the turnover ratio (**8.68x**)\nalmost exactly. The ~2.8pp of the claimed 3.1pp \"edge\" is purely the cost\ndifferential from the strategy turning over 8.7x slower \u2014 a property of the chosen\nrebalance *cadence*, which has nothing to do with stock *selection*. The memo's own\nphrase, \"Net of identical cost assumptions,\" is the sleight of hand: the cost\n*assumption* is identical, the cost *drag* is not, and it is the drag that decides\nthe ranking.\n\n**Fix:** reconstitute / rebalance the 10,000 random baskets at the **same cadence as\nthe strategy (semiannual)** before charging costs, then compare net-to-net.\n(Monthly refresh is fine for generating the *distribution of independent draws* to\nmeasure selection dispersion, but each drawn basket must be *held* on the strategy's\ncadence for the net-cost comparison to be fair. The memo conflates draw-frequency\nwith holding-turnover.)\n\n## Consequence: the edge collapses on any fair basis\n\n**Gross (turnover-neutral, the honest test of selection):**\n- Strategy 15.0% vs hurdle p50 14.7% \u2192 **+0.3pp** (not +3.1pp).\n- Strategy 15.0% vs hurdle p95 16.9% \u2192 **\u22121.9pp** \u2014 the strategy **FAILS to clear\n  the 95th percentile**, the opposite of the memo's headline claim.\n\n**Net, with the hurdle rebalanced at the strategy's cadence (drag \u2248 0.34pp both arms):**\n- Hurdle p50 net \u2248 14.36% \u2192 strategy edge \u2248 **+0.24pp**.\n- Hurdle p95 net \u2248 16.56% \u2192 strategy edge \u2248 **\u22121.96pp** \u2014 still **fails p95**.\n\nSo the true, like-for-like selection edge is ~**+0.2 to +0.3pp/yr over the median**,\nand the strategy does **not** clear the 95th percentile. Registering \"+3.1pp/yr\nexpected outperformance over segment random\" overstates the real selection edge by\nroughly **10x** and inverts the p95 result. Both certification claims are false.\n\n## Note on the other gates (why they don't rescue this)\n\nDSR 0.98, PBO 22%, parameter plateau, lookahead 0-FAIL test the *strategy's own*\nrobustness/overfit. They say nothing about whether the *hurdle* was constructed\nfairly, so passing them is irrelevant to this defect. (No independent problem found\nin those gates from the text as written \u2014 non-material.) The arithmetic in the memo\n(3.1 = 14.6\u221211.5; 14.6 > 13.7) is correct; the defect is methodological, not a\ncalculation slip.\n\n## Non-material comments\n\n- A 38%/yr one-way turnover for a semiannual 40-name quality basket (~19%/rebalance)\n  is plausible; 330%/yr for a monthly-refreshed random basket (~27%/month) is\n  plausible. Turnover figures themselves are not in dispute \u2014 only that they were\n  compared under a common cost rate.\n\n---\n\n## Numbered list of material defects\n\n1. **Turnover / rebalance-cadence mismatch between strategy and hurdle** (38%/yr\n   semiannual vs 330%/yr monthly) charged an identical 45bp/side. The ~2.8pp net cost\n   differential is an artifact of the 8.7x turnover gap, not selection. Net-drag\n   ratio (8.0x) \u2248 turnover ratio (8.68x), confirming the gap is entirely cost.\n   **Fix:** hold/rebalance the random hurdle baskets at the strategy's semiannual\n   cadence, then compare net-to-net (or compare gross-to-gross).\n\n2. **The certified figures are false on a fair basis.** Gross and turnover-matched-net\n   both give a selection edge of only ~**+0.2 to +0.3pp/yr over the median**, and the\n   strategy **fails to clear the 95th percentile** (\u22121.9pp gross / \u22122.0pp net) \u2014 not\n   the claimed \"+3.1pp/yr\" and \"clears even the 95th percentile.\" Registering\n   +3.1pp/yr overstates the real edge by ~10x and mis-states the p95 outcome.\n\n----- ANSWER O16d -----\n# T16 \u2014 CIO CONSOLIDATED VERDICT (arm C)\n\n**Item:** Quality-tilt certification memo \u2014 request to register \"+3.1pp/yr selection edge over segment random, clears p95.\"\n**Consolidating:** Quant review (Arjun Rao) + Red Team (Nikhil Bose).\n**Evidence:** memo text + the two reviews only (no file access; all figures below are the memo's own four-number table or arithmetic derived from it).\n**CIO:** Rajan Mehta.\n\n---\n\n## VERDICT: REJECT (do not certify; do not register +3.1pp)\n\n**Rationale (3 lines).**\n1. The +3.1pp is a **cost artifact**: a 38%/yr-turnover semiannual strategy is benchmarked against a 330%/yr monthly-refresh random null under an identical per-side charge, so the null is cost-clubbed by a construction choice that has nothing to do with the quality screen.\n2. On the only axis that tests selection \u2014 **gross, costs stripped** \u2014 the strategy sits at the random **median** (15.0 vs 14.7, ~59th percentile, \u03c3\u22481.34pp) and is **1.9pp below gross p95 (16.9)**. Matched-cadence, selection edge \u2248 0 and the cost-efficiency edge is *also* \u2248 0.\n3. The certification-blocking gates the memo asserts but does not show (lookahead/PIT audit, survivorship-free universe on both arms) could push the true selection edge **negative**, not merely to zero.\n\nBoth reviewers independently reach **FAKE as-certified**. There is no genuine conflict to adjudicate: Nikhil sustains Arjun's core, tightens it, corrects one figure, and adds two attack surfaces. I adopt the consolidated position below.\n\n---\n\n## The kill, in the memo's own numbers\n\n| arm | one-way turnover | gross CAGR | net CAGR | cost drag | drag per 1% turnover |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Quality-tilt (semiannual) | 38%/yr | 15.0 | 14.6 | 0.40pp | ~0.0105pp |\n| Random p50 (monthly) | 330%/yr | 14.7 | 11.5 | 3.20pp | ~0.0097pp |\n| Random p95 (monthly) | 330%/yr | 16.9 | 13.7 | 3.20pp | ~0.0097pp |\n\n- **Identical per-side cost rate both arms** (~0.01pp drag per 1% one-way turnover). This forecloses the \"the arms just used different cost models\" defence: they did not. The net gap is *mechanically* the turnover ratio.\n- **Selection axis (gross):** strategy z = (15.0\u221214.7)/1.34 = **+0.22 \u2192 ~59th percentile of random.** Below gross p95 (16.9) by 1.9pp.\n- **Net axis:** both random percentiles carry the same 3.20 drag, so the net band is the gross band shifted down 3.20 with dispersion preserved (net \u03c3 also \u22481.34). Strategy net z = (14.6\u221211.5)/1.34 = **+2.3 \u2192 ~99th percentile.**\n- The strategy travels **59th \u2192 99th percentile purely because the null got cost-bludgeoned.** That 40-percentile-point jump *is* the entire \"+3.1pp edge.\" There is no selection content in it.\n\nThis is the IC-1 precedent exactly: a headline that **decomposes**. Register incremental edge (signal minus regime/benchmark base), never the headline. Here the incremental selection edge is \u2248 0.\n\n---\n\n## Tail-risk assessment (adapted \u2014 this is a certification, not a live position)\n\nThe tail here is **institutional, not market**: registering a phantom 3.1pp anchors future sizing (firewall breach of the IC-1 precedent). Concretely:\n- **Worst case if certified:** the firm over-allocates to a sleeve credited with 3.1pp of alpha it does not have. When the cost handicap on the strawman null is removed, realised outperformance vs a *properly-built* passive mid/small quality tilt is ~0 \u2014 a 3.1pp shortfall against expectation, compounded across the sleeve's capital.\n- **Left-tail beyond that:** the unshown PIT/survivorship gates. On a **semiannual fundamental screen** (profitability + leverage), a one-quarter availability leak inflates gross 15.0; correcting it pushes the strategy **below** the random median \u2192 **selection edge goes negative.** Survivorship in mid/small 2016\u20132025 (heavy delisting) inflates *both* gross bands and interacts with turnover (monthly refresh rotates out of dying names; semiannual eats the loss). Until both are shown clean, the sign of the true edge is unknown and could be adverse.\n- **Correlated-blowup analogue:** a mislabeled \"quality alpha\" sleeve is precisely the calm-looking large/quality name that the firm's own lessons flag \u2014 the tail is not in the pre-trade signal, it is in the sizing that a phantom edge invites.\n\n---\n\n## Sizing ruling\n\n- **Register selection edge = 0.0pp** over segment random. Do not register +3.1pp.\n- **The sleeve is not killed.** Its 14.6% net is a real, deployable number and its low turnover is genuinely bankable \u2014 but only versus a **churnier** comparator (active managers, a monthly product), which is a *different, legitimate* claim. Carry it as a low-cost passive mid/small quality tilt; credit it with **cost efficiency vs high-churn peers**, sized to that claim, not with alpha over a passive same-cadence random.\n- **Firewall:** this rejected certification must not anchor any future sizing (IC-1 precedent, binding).\n\n---\n\n## Kill criteria / conditions to re-open certification\n\nRe-submit only when ALL hold:\n1. **Turnover-matched null:** regenerate the 10,000 random baskets at 40 names, same segment, **semiannual** rebalance (~38%/yr turnover), same 45bp/side. Report **gross AND net** percentile bands.\n2. The claim must clear the matched-cadence bar on **gross** (selection) and be corroborated on net \u2014 not hold on net alone via a cost handicap.\n3. **Shown** lookahead pass: lag+1 / `available_date` provenance on the profitability + leverage inputs; gross 15.0 must survive it (CLAUDE.md landmine #3, D-028 \u2014 an asserted \"0-FAIL\" with nothing shown is not a pass).\n4. **PIT universe both arms:** confirm strategy and all random draws are drawn from PIT membership snapshots (`NIFTY500_TICKER_2005_2025_Final.xlsx`), not a survivor/current list (landmine #6).\n5. Re-attribute: report selection edge (matched null) and any cost-efficiency edge (vs a stated churnier comparator) **separately**; register only what survives.\n\n**Review date:** on resubmission with items 1\u20135 satisfied. No standing review otherwise \u2014 the certification is dead until rebuilt.\n\n---\n\n## Dissents / recorded technical corrections\n\n- **No dissent on the verdict.** Both reviewers reach FAKE-as-certified; I concur. Defect ranking below is the consolidated firm position.\n- **Correction to the Quant review (Arjun Rao), adopted:** his \"corrected read\" (semiannual random p95 \u2248 16.5, p50 \u2248 14.3 by subtracting a scalar 0.40 drag from the monthly gross band) is **not derivable that way.** Cadence changes gross *dispersion*: a monthly re-draw averages ~12\u00d7/yr toward the segment mean (tight band); a semiannual basket has ~2 independent draws/yr (wider band, higher gross p95). So the true matched-cadence p95 is **\u226516.9, not 16.5** \u2014 his estimate *understates* the bar and is therefore conservative in favour of his own conclusion. Flag: illustrative, not computed. Non-material to the verdict; the strategy loses to matched p95 by *more*, not less.\n- **Sharpening (Nikhil Bose), adopted:** Arjun's consolation \u2014 \"register the low turnover as a cost-efficiency edge against a same-frequency null\" \u2014 is self-defeating: a same-cadence (semiannual) null carries the *same* ~38% turnover and ~0.40pp drag, so the cost edge is **also 0**. The cost efficiency is real only vs a churnier comparator, which is a different claim than \"+3.1pp over segment random.\" This is why the sizing ruling credits cost efficiency only against a *stated* high-churn peer.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECTS (consolidated, ranked; kept only those that survive)\n\n1. **Turnover-mismatched null \u2014 the kill.** 38%/yr semiannual strategy vs a 330%/yr monthly-refresh random series charged an identical per-side rate (verified: ~0.010pp drag per 1% one-way turnover, both arms). The full +3.1pp net gap is manufactured by the cost differential, not selection. **Fix:** rebuild the null at the strategy's own semiannual cadence.\n2. **Selection axis (gross) refutes the claim, quantified.** Strategy ~59th percentile of random (15.0 vs p50 14.7, \u03c3\u22481.34pp) and 1.9pp **below** gross p95 (16.9). The move to ~99th percentile on net is pure cost contamination. \"Clears p95\" holds only on net and only because of the handicap.\n3. **Mislabeling.** A turnover/cost differential is registered as a \"+3.1pp selection edge.\" Matched-cadence, **both** the selection edge and the cost edge collapse to \u2248 0; real cost efficiency exists only vs a churnier comparator (a different, smaller, honestly-labelled claim).\n4. **\"Lookahead audit 0-FAIL\" asserted, not shown, on a semiannual fundamental screen.** Highest-leverage unverified item; a PIT availability leak on the profitability/leverage inputs would inflate gross 15.0 and drive the true selection edge **negative**. Per D-028, an unshown audit is a certification blocker in itself. **Fix:** show lag+1 / `available_date` provenance.\n5. **Survivorship / PIT-universe of both arms unverified** (mid/small 2016\u20132025, heavy delisting). Survivor bias inflates both gross bands and interacts with turnover. **Fix:** confirm PIT membership for the strategy and all 10,000 random draws.\n\n**Non-material comments (clearly labelled, not defects):**\n- (N1, framing) \"Clears p95\" is the wrong bar for a *selection* edge \u2014 beating p95 means landing in the top 5% of random draws, which no median-quality screen should. Correct test = the strategy's **percentile in a matched-cadence distribution** (~59th), not \"beat p95.\" The memo (net) and the Quant rebuttal (gross) both inherit the mis-frame.\n- (N2, stat gates) DSR 0.98 / PBO 22% / plateau / lookahead-0FAIL validate the strategy's own parameter selection and internal robustness \u2014 **not** the benchmark comparison, which is where the registrable number is manufactured. They cannot support the +3.1pp claim. Ancillary: PBO 22% is marginal against the 25% cap and DSR's honest-trials count is asserted, not shown \u2014 do not treat either as independent corroboration.\n- (N3, cost realism) Uniform 45bp/side likely *understates* impact on the 330%-turnover null (impact convex in participation) \u2014 this makes the artifact worse, not better; it does not help the strategy.\n\n**Bottom line:** REJECT certification. Register selection edge 0.0pp. Keep the sleeve as a low-cost passive quality tilt at its honest 14.6% net; do not credit it with alpha over segment random. Re-open only on a turnover-matched, gross-corroborated, PIT-clean, lookahead-shown resubmission.", "ids": ["O16a", "O16b", "O16c", "O16d"]}, {"tid": "T17", "key": "## T17 \u2014 lookahead \u2014 argmax-over-window entry, T9 (CRITICAL)\n**Defect.** `win.loc[win[\"ff\"].idxmax()]` picks the best-priced day of the ENTIRE\nT-30..T-10 window. Knowing day d was the window maximum requires having seen every\nday after d \u2014 no causal trader can do it. Entering at the window extreme fabricates\nedge from pure noise (verified: +3.5%/cycle on a driftless walk). The\nnext-session-open fill does NOT repair it: identifying the peak still requires the\nunseen remainder of the window. Firm precedent: forward_factor v2's argmax entry.\n**Line.** `idxmax()` over the full window followed by entry at that day.\n**Fix.** Pre-registered causal trigger \u2014 first crossing of a fixed ff threshold, or\na fixed entry lead; re-run.\n**Accept.** \"Argmax over the window is hindsight entry-day selection\"; \"picking the\npeak-ff day needs the whole window \u2014 non-causal\"; \"perfect entry timing inside the\nwindow; use first-threshold-cross instead\".\n**Secondary.** Liquidity skip and T-2 exit are fine.\n", "answers": "----- ANSWER O17a -----\n# T17 \u2014 Review: calendar-spread timing engine (arm B)\n\n**Verdict: FATAL LOOKAHEAD. The +7.2 pts / 69% result is not tradeable and is fully explainable by selection bias with zero true edge.**\n\n---\n\n## Material defect #1 (fatal) \u2014 in-window argmax entry selection is forward-looking\n\n**Line:**\n```python\nbest = win.loc[win[\"ff\"].idxmax()]          # over ALL of T-30..T-10\nentry_day = next_session(best[\"date\"])\n```\n\n**Mechanism.** `win` is the entire T-30..T-10 window (~20 trading days). `idxmax()`\nselects the single day with the highest `ff` *across the whole window*, and the\ntrade is then entered *inside* that window (at `next_session(best[\"date\"])`). To\nknow which day is the window-wide maximum you must have already observed every\nday in the window \u2014 including all the days that fall **after** the entry day.\n\nConcretely: if `ff` peaks at lead=28 (T-28), the engine enters ~T-27. But you\ncannot know T-28 was the window maximum until T-10, because a higher `ff` could\nstill print on T-20, T-15, etc. \u2014 all of which are in the future relative to a\nT-27 entry. The rule requires seeing the future to pick the entry. In live\ntrading you must decide *each day* whether to enter with no sight of later `ff`\nvalues. So the entry day is chosen with perfect hindsight of the whole window.\n\n`next_session()` at the *open* is a red herring \u2014 it correctly avoids same-bar\nfill for a *single* decision day, but it does nothing about the selection: the\nchoice of *which* day is the argmax over future data. This is the T-taxonomy\nlookahead class (peeking / in-window optimization), and it is the entire result.\n\n**Quantified (scratch sim, `scratch/sim.py`).** I modelled a window of 20 days\nwith `ff ~ N(0,1)` and `pnl = beta*ff + noise`, calibrated so a *random or\nfixed-lead* entry has expected P&L of **exactly 0** \u2014 i.e. NO real timing edge:\n\n| entry rule | avg pts | hit % | true edge |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| fixed-lead (causal) | -0.02 | 49.8 | 0 |\n| random day (causal) | +0.02 | 50.1 | 0 |\n| **argmax-ff over window (this engine)** | **+3.7 \u2026 +5.6** | **70 \u2026 73** | **0** |\n\nThe reported **+7.2 pts / 69%** sits inside the band that pure argmax selection\nproduces from a mechanism with **no edge at all**. The number is consistent with\nbeing 100% selection artifact; nothing in the code rules that out.\n\n**Fix.** Replace the argmax with a *causal* rule using only information available\nup to the decision day, then walk-forward:\n- enter the **first** day `ff` crosses a pre-registered threshold, or\n- enter on `ff`'s **rolling percentile / z-score** computed from *prior* data only, or\n- a fixed lead as the honest baseline.\nThen report the timing rule's P&L **against the fixed-lead baseline** \u2014 the only\nnumber that shows timing adds anything. Expect the +7.2 to collapse toward the\nfixed-lead result.\n\n---\n\n## Defect #2 (material, data-dependent) \u2014 `ff` is built from settlement prints, which include model/untraded settles\n\n`ff` is \"computed once per day from that day's settlement prints.\" On non-expiry\ndays the F&O bhavcopy `SETTLE_PR` is a *theoretical/model* settle for illiquid\nstrikes (the untraded-but-priced landmine: strikes with `CONTRACTS=0` still carry\na model settle, and 0.00-price strikes exist). The liquidity gate\n(`both_legs_traded_else_skip`) is applied only to the **execution** of the spread\n\u2014 it does **not** filter the days that feed `idxmax`. So the \"best-priced\" day\nthe argmax latches onto can be a **phantom/stale settle**, not a price anyone\ncould have traded. Combined with #1 this doubly inflates the entry.\n\n**Fix.** Build `ff` only from strikes with `CONTRACTS>0` (traded prints), and\napply the same traded-volume gate to the *signal* days, not just the fill.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material notes (labelled, not defects)\n\n- **Exit at T-2 (`close_at_lead(lead=2)`, \"verified prints\").** Fine \u2014 T-2 is not\n  expiry day, so the expiry-day settle=underlying landmine does not bite, and\n  \"verified prints\" addresses fill realism. No issue.\n- **Entry at next session's open vs settlement-derived signal.** Causally correct\n  for a single day (avoids same-bar fill). The problem is the *selection*, not\n  this.\n- **`costs_pts(2)`** \u2014 the literal `2` is opaque (lead? side count?); confirm it\n  is a real per-spread cost, not a placeholder. Minor.\n- **No benchmark / no OOS split.** Even a corrected engine must be reported vs a\n  fixed-lead baseline over a walk-forward split; the whole 2019\u20132026 span here is\n  one in-sample selection.\n- **86 of ~90 monthly cycles** is a plausible count after `len<8` and liquidity\n  skips \u2014 not a defect, but note the liquidity skip drops the *whole cycle* when\n  the argmax day is illiquid, a further selection knock-on once #1 is fixed.\n\n---\n\n## Material defects (numbered)\n\n1. **In-window `ff.idxmax()` entry selection is lookahead** \u2014 the entry day is the\n   argmax over the *entire* T-30..T-10 window, knowable only after the window\n   ends, yet the trade is entered inside the window. Not executable live; a\n   zero-edge mechanism reproduces the reported +7.2 pts / 69% purely from this\n   selection. Fix: causal threshold/percentile entry, walk-forward, report vs a\n   fixed-lead baseline.\n2. **`ff` signal built from settlement prints without a traded-volume gate** \u2014\n   can select phantom/model settles of untraded strikes as \"best pricing\"; the\n   liquidity gate protects only execution, not the signal. Fix: restrict `ff` to\n   `CONTRACTS>0` traded strikes.\n\n----- ANSWER O17b -----\n# T17 \u2014 Final Arbiter Verdict: calendar-spread timing engine\n\nVERDICT: MATERIAL DEFECT FOUND. The headline result (2019\u20132026, 86 cycles, +7.2 pts\navg/cycle net, 69% hit) is FAKE \u2014 it rests on a look-ahead / in-sample peak-picking\nentry rule that cannot be executed live. One fatal defect is confirmed by both reviews\nand survives adversarial scrutiny. One additional exit-side liquidity gap is a genuine\nverification-required flag (raised by the red team, correctly refuting the first\nreviewer's over-certification). Secondary and cost notes are non-material.\n\n---\n\n## Defect 1 (FATAL \u2014 invalidates the whole result): argmax-over-window entry selection is look-ahead / in-sample peak-picking\n\nLines:\n```\nwin  = ff[(ff[\"expiry\"]==cyc.expiry) & (ff[\"lead\"]>=10) & (ff[\"lead\"]<=30)]\nbest = win.loc[win[\"ff\"].idxmax()]\nentry_day = next_session(best[\"date\"])\n```\n\nMechanism:\n- The scenario states `ff` is \"computed once per day from THAT day's settlement\n  prints.\" So the ff value for any day D inside the T-30..T-10 window is only\n  observable at D's close. It is not knowable in advance.\n- `win[\"ff\"].idxmax()` selects the single day whose ff was the highest across the\n  ENTIRE T-30..T-10 window. To evaluate that global argmax you must already stand at\n  the end of the window (T-10) with every intervening day's ff in hand, then \"enter\"\n  retroactively on whichever earlier day (e.g. T-25) turned out to be the peak.\n- At the real decision moment inside the window (say, standing at T-25) you cannot know\n  that T-25 will be the window's ff maximum \u2014 days T-24\u2026T-10 have not printed yet. The\n  entry date is therefore chosen using ff prints from days that had not yet occurred at\n  entry time. The rule can never be executed live.\n- Consequence: the engine is not answering \"which day should we enter?\" It is answering\n  \"which day, in hindsight, had the best pricing?\" It systematically enters at the\n  in-sample optimum of the signal. The +7.2 pts avg / 69% hit is a peak-picking\n  artifact \u2014 an unattainable upper bound, not a tradeable edge. This is future-data\n  selection (T-class look-ahead under the firm's LOOKAHEAD_CONTROLS taxonomy) and fails\n  any one-day-lag / causal-decision test.\n\nThe `next_session(best[\"date\"])` fill lag does NOT rescue this. It correctly prevents a\nsame-bar fill on the signal day, but a correctly-lagged fill of a look-ahead-SELECTED\nentry date is still look-ahead. Both reviewers agree on this; it is correct.\n\nCorrect fix \u2014 the decision on each day must use only information available up to and\nincluding that day:\n- Replace the global argmax with a CAUSAL trigger, pre-registered before seeing results.\n  E.g. \"enter on the FIRST session within T-30..T-10 on which ff exceeds a threshold set\n  from data available before the window opens (prior-cycle or expanding-window\n  quantile).\" Such a rule uses only past/current ff, so it is executable.\n- Alternatively, if a within-window relative rank is genuinely intended, it must be an\n  EXPANDING (running) statistic: at each day D compare ff(D) only to ff on days \u2264 D in\n  this cycle's window, and commit the entry irrevocably \u2014 never referencing future days\n  in the same window.\n- Re-run 2019\u20132026 with the causal rule. The honest avg/hit will almost certainly be\n  well below +7.2 / 69%. If it is negative or trivial, the timing edge does not exist.\n\n---\n\n## Defect 2 (MATERIAL \u2014 verification-required): exit path has NO liquidity guard on the far leg\n\nLine:\n```\npnl = close_at_lead(spread, lead=2)   # exit T-2, verified prints\n```\n\nMechanism:\n- `liquidity=\"both_legs_traded_else_skip\"` is a parameter of `open_calendar_spread`\n  (ENTRY only). The exit `close_at_lead(spread, lead=2)` carries no such guard in the\n  shown code.\n- The spread is `near_short_far_long`, so the far long leg belongs to a LATER expiry\n  that is still far-dated at T-2 of the near expiry. Firm landmine #9 warns that\n  far/less-liquid expiries are frequently LISTED with model settles but CONTRACTS=0\n  (untraded-but-priced). If `close_at_lead` values that far leg off a model/settle price\n  on an untraded strike at exit, the exit P&L is fictional on those cycles and net pts\n  are overstated.\n- This cannot be confirmed from the text alone (the \"verified prints\" comment asserts,\n  but does not demonstrate, a traded-print exit), so it is a REQUIRED-VERIFICATION flag\n  rather than a second independently-confirmed fatal defect. It is retained as material\n  because it can move the headline number and the code shows no guard.\n- Note on scope: landmine #9 as usually cited bites the NEAR leg on its OWN expiry day.\n  Here the near leg exits at T-2 (not T-0), so #9 does not bite the near leg \u2014 which\n  makes the first reviewer's \"verified prints implies #9 avoided\" an over-claim. The\n  real exposure is the far-leg untraded-print at exit, and that is unguarded.\n\nCorrect fix: apply a both-legs-traded (CONTRACTS>0) guard at EXIT as well as at entry,\nand cash out only on verified traded prints for both legs; skip/flag cycles where the\nfar leg is untraded at T-2 rather than marking it off a model settle.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material notes (clearly labelled \u2014 NOT defects)\n\n- (Non-material, secondary) `if len(win) < 8: continue` counts rows over the FULL\n  T-30..T-10 window, so cycle eligibility also uses end-of-window information \u2014 it shares\n  the same non-causal structure as Defect 1. It is reinforcing, not independent; under a\n  causal rewrite this gate must also be reframed to use only history available at/before\n  the trigger day. Secondary to Defect 1.\n- (Non-material, verify) `costs_pts(2)` \u2014 a calendar spread is 4 option transactions\n  round-trip. If this is a flat 2-point stub rather than a true both-legs round-trip\n  cost, net pts are overstated. Unconfirmable from the text; verify against\n  COST_STANDARDS.\n- (Non-material, correct) The correctly-guarded parts: fill lagged to\n  `next_session(...open)` (no same-bar fill); entry (\u2265 T-10) strictly precedes exit\n  (T-2); entry liquidity gate `both_legs_traded_else_skip` guards the 0.00-price\n  untraded-strike issue at entry. None of these is a defect \u2014 but none rescues Defect 1.\n\n---\n\n## Numbered list of claimed material defects\n\n1. Entry-day selection via `win[\"ff\"].idxmax()` over the full T-30..T-10 window is\n   future-data selection / in-sample peak-picking: the entry date is chosen using ff\n   prints from days that had not yet occurred at entry time, so the rule cannot be run\n   live and the reported +7.2 pts/cycle, 69% hit is a hindsight-optimum artifact, not a\n   real edge. Fix: replace with a causal rule (first day ff crosses a pre-registered or\n   prior-cycle/expanding-window threshold, decision committed using only ff \u2264 D), then\n   re-run 2019\u20132026.\n\n2. The exit `close_at_lead(spread, lead=2)` has no liquidity guard on the far long leg\n   (a later, less-liquid expiry). Per landmine #9, far expiries are often model-settled\n   with CONTRACTS=0; if the far leg is valued off an untraded model/settle print at exit,\n   those cycles' P&L is fictional and net pts overstated. Fix: apply a both-legs-traded\n   (CONTRACTS>0) guard at exit as well as entry, and cash out only on verified traded\n   prints. (Verification-required: not provable from the text, but the code shows no\n   guard and the \"verified prints\" comment does not demonstrate one.)\n\nNon-material notes: `len(win)<8` inclusion gate is also non-causal but secondary to (1);\n`costs_pts(2)` may under-charge a 4-transaction round-trip \u2014 verify against\nCOST_STANDARDS.\n\n----- ANSWER O17c -----\n# T17 \u2014 Review: calendar-spread timing engine (arm A)\n\n## Verdict\n**Material defect found \u2014 the headline result (+7.2 pts/cycle, 69% hit) is fake.** It measures a\nperfect-hindsight entry oracle, not a tradeable rule.\n\n## The fatal defect \u2014 future-peeking entry selection\n\n```python\nwin = ff[(ff[\"expiry\"] == cyc.expiry) & (ff[\"lead\"] >= 10) & (ff[\"lead\"] <= 30)]\n...\nbest = win.loc[win[\"ff\"].idxmax()]           # <-- lookahead\nentry_day = next_session(best[\"date\"])\n```\n\n**Line:** `best = win.loc[win[\"ff\"].idxmax()]`.\n\n**Mechanism.** `win` is the *entire* T-30..T-10 window for the cycle \u2014 every daily `ff` print from\n30 days-to-expiry down to 10 days-to-expiry. `idxmax()` picks the single day whose `ff` was the\nhighest *across the whole window*. To know on day D that D's `ff` is the window maximum, you must\nhave already observed every later day in the window (D+1 \u2026 T-10), which have not happened yet at D.\nSo the engine is choosing the entry day with information from the future. In live trading you cannot\nknow, standing on (say) T-18, that T-18's `ff` will beat every remaining day down to T-10.\n\nThis is a textbook lookahead (T-class in `07_RISK_OFFICE/LOOKAHEAD_CONTROLS.md`; a selection-on-\nfuture-information peek). It is also **circular / self-fulfilling**: `ff` is *defined* as \"better\nentry pricing for the spread,\" and the code hand-picks, in hindsight, the richest-priced day of each\ncycle. The strategy is guaranteed to enter at the best pricing every single cycle. The +7.2 pts avg\nand 69% hit are the payoff of that oracle, not of any executable decision rule. The number tells you\nnothing about what the engine would have earned live, and it cannot be used to answer the stated\nquestion (\"which day should we enter?\") because the \"answer\" requires seeing the whole window first.\n\nThe next-session fill (`next_session(best[\"date\"])`) does **not** cure this. Lagging the fill by one\nsession only avoids using D's own EOD print at D's open; it does nothing about the fact that the *day\nselected* was chosen with knowledge of the entire window's future prints.\n\n**Correct fix \u2014 the day-D decision may use only `ff` on days \u2264 D.** Replace the in-sample `idxmax`\nwith a causal rule, e.g. one of:\n- **Fixed-lead benchmark(s):** always enter at a pinned lead (T-25, T-20, \u2026) and compare. No peeking;\n  this is the honest way to ask \"which day is best.\"\n- **Pre-registered threshold:** enter the *first* day `ff` exceeds a threshold \u03c4 calibrated purely\n  out-of-sample / pre-registered, accepting that some cycles reach T-10 without entering (and are\n  skipped or entered at the T-10 fallback).\n- **Online running-max rule:** enter when `ff(D) > max(ff over prior N days in this window)` \u2014 again\n  using only history up to D.\n\nThen re-run with honest trials counting (the \u03c4 / N / lead grid is a search space; report DSR/PBO per\n`/oos-audit`).\n\n## Non-material comments (labelled)\n\n- **[non-material, cannot verify] `costs_pts(2)`** \u2014 a two-legged calendar (near short + far long)\n  is a 4-leg round trip (2 to open, 2 to close). If the `2` passed here means \"2 legs\" and the\n  function charges one spread's worth rather than the full open+close, costs are understated by ~half.\n  Cannot confirm without the function body; flag for verification. Not the reason the result is fake.\n- **[non-material] `next_session` boundary leak:** if the selected day sits at `lead == 10` (T-10),\n  `next_session` fills at ~T-9, just outside the declared window. Cosmetic given the window is a soft\n  choice, but the entry can land one session tighter to expiry than advertised.\n- **[positive, not a defect]** The signal\u2192fill timing is otherwise handled correctly: `ff` is built\n  from EOD settlement prints of day D and the fill is at the *next* session's open, so there is no\n  intraday same-bar lookahead in the fill mechanics, and the T-2 exit avoids the expiry-day\n  settle-price landmine (#9). The corruption is purely in the day-selection, not the fill/exit.\n\n## Material defects (numbered)\n1. **Future-peeking entry selection** \u2014 `best = win.loc[win[\"ff\"].idxmax()]` picks the entry day by\n   taking the maximum `ff` over the *entire* T-30..T-10 window, i.e. using days later than the chosen\n   day. This is a lookahead and is circular (the signal *is* entry richness), turning the engine into\n   a hindsight oracle. It makes the reported +7.2 pts/cycle and 69% hit non-reproducible live. Fix:\n   replace `idxmax` over the full window with a causal rule that uses only `ff` on days \u2264 the decision\n   day (fixed-lead benchmark, pre-registered threshold, or running-max), then re-audit with honest\n   trial counting.\n\n----- ANSWER O17d -----\n# T17 \u2014 CIO CONSOLIDATED VERDICT: calendar-spread timing engine\n\n**Issued by:** Rajan Mehta (CIO, E-001) \u2014 consolidated after Quant Review + Red Team (Nikhil Bose).\n**Evidence base:** the pasted code + the two reviews ONLY. No file/data access. Every claim below tagged `[INFERENCE]`/`[OPINION]` unless it is directly on the code text. Data lineage (files/rows/max-dates) is **unverified** and I make no `[DATA]` claim.\n\n---\n\n## VERDICT: REJECT \u2014 the reported result is FAKE / non-executable.\n\n**Rationale (3 lines):**\n1. Entry day is chosen by `win[\"ff\"].idxmax()` \u2014 an argmax over the **entire** not-yet-observed T-30..T-10 window. That is a per-cycle hindsight peak-pick; no live rule reproduces it. `[INFERENCE]`\n2. Quant desk and Red Team reached this independently and the mechanism is airtight \u2014 so the headline **+7.2 pts avg / 69% hit is not a return, it is the retrospective best-day-per-cycle**.\n3. Independently, the `ff` signal is built from **settlement prints** and is exposed to firm landmine #9 (phantom model settles on untraded far-month strikes), a corruption of the *signal itself* that survives even a de-leaked re-run.\n\n---\n\n## What the submission does RIGHT (stated so I am not inventing problems)\n- ff from that day's **settlement**, fill at **next session's open** (`next_session(best[\"date\"])`) \u2192 no same-bar fill leakage on the *fill transition*. `[INFERENCE]`\n- P&L booked as **one number per cycle** at exit \u2192 the fake-low-variance / Sharpe-7 compounding artifact is NOT present here.\n- Exit at **lead=2 (T-2)**, not expiry \u2192 sidesteps the expiry-day `SETTLE_PR = underlying settlement` trap (landmine #9) on the *exit* leg.\n- `liquidity=\"both_legs_traded_else_skip\"` gates fills on actual trading (CONTRACTS>0) \u2014 good, but see Defect 2 for where it is applied too late.\n\nNone of that saves the number, because the *selection* rule is corrupt.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECTS THAT SURVIVE\n\n### Defect 1 \u2014 Lookahead entry selection (DISPOSITIVE; both reviews confirm) `[INFERENCE]`\n```python\nbest = win.loc[win[\"ff\"].idxmax()]     # argmax over the WHOLE T-30..T-10 window\nentry_day = next_session(best[\"date\"])\n```\n- **Exact locus:** the `idxmax()` over `win`, where `win` = every `lead \u2208 [10,30]` row for `cyc.expiry`.\n- **Mechanism:** to know that (say) T-24 is the window's max ff, you must already have observed T-23\u2026T-10 \u2014 days that have not occurred when you stand at T-24. The engine reads the *completed* window and enters its peak in hindsight. `next_session()` shifts only the **fill** by one bar; it launders nothing about the **selection**. This is per-cycle retrospective optimisation (T-class lookahead).\n- **Effect:** entry lands on the richest-priced day every cycle \u2192 inflates BOTH the +7.2 avg and the 69% hit. The number is non-reproducible by any causal rule.\n- **Fix:** replace `idxmax` with a strictly causal entry, reported side-by-side: (a) pre-registered **fixed lead** (e.g. T-20, the honest no-timing baseline); (b) **walk-forward threshold / first-cross** (threshold calibrated on expanding/rolling PAST cycles only); (c) **expanding max-so-far commit** (enter the first day whose ff beats everything seen so far this window). Then run the full battery: walk-forward, plateau, DSR>0.95 on honest trials, PBO<25%, \u226530 obs/param, regime slices, lag+1 degrade.\n\n**CIO precision ruling (I side with Red Team over the Quant desk here):** the Quant review's phrasing \"that one line manufactures the *entire* result\" is an **over-claim** and I strike it. The lookahead is on **ff**, not on P&L; it inflates the number only to the extent ff is correlated with subsequent spread P&L \u2014 a link neither review measured. The honest decomposition is **+7.2 = base calendar carry (present on ANY entry day) + a lookahead-timing premium of unknown size.** The lookahead makes the *reported* number fake and non-executable; it does **not** prove that 100% of the P&L is manufactured. This matters because a causal, cost-honest version could still print a positive carry number \u2014 at which point costs and the base-rate benchmark, not the lookahead, decide the strategy.\n\n### Defect 2 \u2014 Phantom-`ff` signal contamination (missed by the Quant desk; caught by Red Team) `[INFERENCE]` \u2014 CONDITIONAL / MUST-VERIFY\n- **Exact locus:** `ff = load_ff_series()`, \"front-back richness \u2026 from settlement prints.\"\n- **Mechanism (grounded in firm landmine #9):** far/back-month option settles are frequently **model settles on untraded strikes (CONTRACTS=0)** \u2014 a listed price with no trade behind it. If the far leg's settle is a phantom model price, `ff` is inflated on exactly the days the back leg did not trade, and the `idxmax` will preferentially select the day whose \"richness\" is a **stale model quote**, not a genuinely enter-able cheap spread. Critically, `both_legs_traded_else_skip` is applied **inside `open_calendar_spread`, AFTER selection** \u2014 it drops the cycle but never cleans the *signal* that chose the day. So even a perfectly de-leaked, causal version of this engine could be trading a non-tradable signal.\n- **Why I keep it material (not just a note):** it is an *independent* corruption pathway that survives fixing Defect 1, and it is grounded in a documented firm landmine, not invented. It is labelled **conditional/[INFERENCE]** only because we cannot see `load_ff_series` internals \u2014 if that function already restricts to CONTRACTS>0 both-leg-traded strikes, this defect is discharged.\n- **Fix:** rebuild `ff` using ONLY strikes with `CONTRACTS>0` on **both** legs that day; re-derive the series and re-run. Show the diff between old and clean ff before any result is quoted.\n\n---\n\n## NON-MATERIAL / SALVAGE-CONDITIONAL (clearly labelled \u2014 NOT counted as material defects)\n\n- **[salvage-conditional] Cost realism.** A calendar spread is a **4-option round trip** (near+far, in and out); `costs_pts(2)` is keyed **only on the exit lead** \u2014 an odd signature, since a 4-leg round-trip cost should not depend solely on when you exit. Likely under-costed \u2192 inflates the \"net\" claim. **Non-material to the FAKE verdict** (Defect 1 already voids the number), but **decisive to any salvage**: on a de-leaked residual carry of a few points, 2\u00d7 costs can flip it net-negative. Require full 4-leg cost at spread-to-mid + a **2\u00d7 cost placebo** in the re-run. *(This is Red Team's \"upgrade\"; I keep it as conditional, not a co-equal material defect \u2014 under-costing does not make the *reported* number's fakeness; the lookahead does.)*\n- **[risk-disclosure gap \u2014 not a corruption] Tail concealment.** +7.2 avg with only 69% hit is fully consistent with a near-zero median carried by a few outlier cycles \u2014 a short-vol-tail payoff in disguise. This does **not** make the number *wrong*; it makes its risk profile undisclosed. As CIO this **bars any sizing** regardless of the P&L, but per the review rules it is a required disclosure, not a defect that fakes the result. Require: full 86-cycle P&L distribution, median, and **bootstrap 5th-percentile of the mean > 0**.\n- **[validation gap \u2014 not a corruption; I DEMOTE the Quant desk's material #2] No causal base-rate benchmark.** Absence of a fixed-lead / random-day-in-window comparison makes the number **uninterpretable, not wrong**. I decline to list it as a material defect (Red Team is right: it is a taxonomy error to rank a missing benchmark alongside a live lookahead). It is a mandatory *salvage* step: the de-leaked rule must **beat random-day-in-window** on the same 86 cycles.\n- **[diagnostic, not a defect] Boundary-lead collapse.** ff (theta-driven term structure) likely trends monotonically across lead 30\u219210, so `idxmax` may nearly always land on a boundary (e.g. lead\u224810). If so the \"timing engine\" is really a **fixed-late-lead carry trade** and the lookahead is *less* harmful than it looks. Tabulate the selected-lead histogram; if it clusters, re-run at that fixed lead \u2014 if the number survives, \"timing\" is a false narrative; if it dies, ff-timing was doing something (only the causal version tells which).\n- **[minor] Post-selection `else_skip`** drops illiquid best-ff cycles after selection \u2192 mild post-selection bias compounding Defect 1. **[minor] `len(win) < 8: continue`** \u2014 reasonable sparse-cycle guard; confirm it is not silently removing a specific regime. **[minor] Opening-print fills** on option legs are optimistic (wide/stale quotes); use spread-to-mid / first-liquid-bar on re-run.\n- **[unverified]** All data lineage \u2014 files, row counts, max dates, the expiry calendar from `build_monthly_cycles`, and whether \"86 cycles\" matches 2019-01\u21922026-06 \u2014 cannot be checked from the snippet.\n\n---\n\n## TAIL-RISK ASSESSMENT (my charter, mandatory)\nUn-assessable from the submission and therefore **failing by default**: no per-cycle distribution, no worst-single-day, no worst-month, no correlated-blowup scenario. A +7.2/69% profile on a short-front-vol calendar is a classic short-tail structure \u2014 the left tail (front-leg gamma blow-up into a gap/event, back-leg vega collapse) is exactly the kind of unpriced tail that produced our calm-large-cap blowups. **This alone forbids sizing even if the P&L were real.**\n\n## SIZING RULING\n**N/A \u2014 do not register, do not paper, do not size.** No sizing anchor may be derived from +7.2. Per the IC-1 firewall precedent, this paper number is FIREWALLED from any future sizing conversation; it is a lookahead artifact, not an edge.\n\n## KILL CRITERIA / RE-RUN GATE (all required before resubmission is even a candidate)\n1. Strictly causal entry \u2014 fixed-lead **and** walk-forward threshold **and** expanding max-so-far, reported side by side.\n2. `ff` rebuilt from CONTRACTS>0 both-legs-traded strikes only (Defect 2 discharged, old-vs-clean diff shown).\n3. Base-rate shuffle \u2014 de-leaked rule must **beat random-day-in-window** on the same cycles.\n4. Honest 4-leg cost at spread-to-mid/first-liquid-bar **plus 2\u00d7 cost placebo**.\n5. Full 86-cycle distribution + **bootstrap 5th-percentile of the mean > 0** + selected-lead histogram.\n6. **lag+1 degrade** test \u2014 the causal number must fall when entry is pushed one session later.\nOnly a fixed-lead-neutral, phantom-clean, cost-honest version that STILL beats random-in-window with a positive bootstrap floor becomes REAL. **Review date: on resubmission only.**\n\n## DISSENTS / RECONCILIATION (recorded by name)\n- **Quant desk vs Red Team on \"manufactures the entire result\":** Quant over-claimed; I adopt **Red Team's** framing (base carry share is unknown; lookahead voids the *reported* number, not necessarily the whole P&L).\n- **Quant desk's material #2 (\"no causal benchmark\"):** **DEMOTED** to a validation gap per Red Team \u2014 not a corruption, removed from the material list.\n- **Red Team's M2 (tail) and M3 (cost upgrade):** accepted as **risk-disclosure / salvage-conditional**, not as fakeness-defects \u2014 kept out of the material count on purpose.\n- **No invented defects** were found in either review; both are honest. Red Team's added catch (phantom-ff) is the highest-value contribution and is elevated to material.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECTS \u2014 NUMBERED (survivors only)\n1. **Lookahead entry selection** \u2014 `win[\"ff\"].idxmax()` picks the entry by argmax over the *entire not-yet-observed* T-30..T-10 window; the reported +7.2 pts / 69% is a hindsight per-cycle peak, not an executable return. Fix: strictly causal entry (fixed lead, or walk-forward threshold / expanding max-so-far) + full validation battery. *(Confirmed by both reviews; mechanism airtight. Note: it voids the reported number but does not prove the entire P&L is manufactured \u2014 base carry share is unknown.)*\n2. **Phantom-`ff` signal contamination** `[INFERENCE]`, conditional/must-verify \u2014 `ff` from settlement prints is exposed to model settles on untraded (CONTRACTS=0) far-month strikes (firm landmine #9), inflating richness on non-tradable days; `both_legs_traded_else_skip` is applied *after* selection and never cleans the signal that chose the day. An independent corruption that survives fixing Defect 1. Fix: rebuild `ff` from CONTRACTS>0 both-legs-traded strikes only and re-derive. *(Discharged only if `load_ff_series` already gates on CONTRACTS>0 \u2014 unverifiable from the snippet.)*\n\nCost realism, tail concealment, missing causal benchmark, boundary-lead collapse, post-selection `else_skip`, `len<8` drop, and opening-print fills are explicitly **non-material to the fake verdict** (salvage-conditional or validation/risk-disclosure requirements), labelled above.", "ids": ["O17a", "O17b", "O17c", "O17d"]}, {"tid": "T18", "key": "## T18 \u2014 data-landmine \u2014 Angel ONE_DAY fromdate with intraday time (HIGH)\n**Defect.** ONE_DAY candles are stamped 00:00 IST (stated in the task). A fromdate\nof `\"<entry_date> 09:15\"` is AFTER the entry day's 00:00 stamp, so the API silently\nomits the entry-day bar; the audit then finds no entry-day bar and flags the leg\nUNFILLABLE. 501/501 UNFILLABLE including deep-liquid ATM NIFTY weeklies is the\ntell \u2014 an impossible real-world liquidity result that should have triggered a sanity\ncheck. The recommendation to void the week's paper results is wrong. (Firm incident\n2026-07-10, same bug.)\n**Line.** `\"fromdate\": leg.entry_date.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d\") + \" 09:15\"`.\n**Fix.** `fromdate = (entry_date - 1 day) 00:00` (or entry_date 00:00) for daily\ncandles; re-run the audit; treat 100%-UNFILLABLE outputs as a red flag for the tool,\nnot the market.\n**Accept.** \"fromdate 09:15 excludes the 00:00-stamped entry-day bar \u2014 the API\ndrops it silently\"; \"request window starts after the daily bar's timestamp\";\n\"the UNFILLABLE verdict is a query artifact; use 00:00 fromdate\".\n**Secondary.** Rate-limit sleep and the THIN participation check are fine.\n", "answers": "----- ANSWER O18a -----\n# T18 \u2014 CIO CONSOLIDATED VERDICT (Rajan Mehta) \u2014 paper-book fill audit script\n\n**VERDICT: FAKE (result is a script artifact) \u2014 REJECT the \"void the paper week\" recommendation. Do NOT action.**\n\nRationale (3 lines):\n1. The \"501/501 UNFILLABLE\" is manufactured by the script, not observed in the market \u2014 a deep-liquid ATM NIFTY weekly cannot print zero volume; the audit is guilty, not the book.\n2. The universal cause is firm landmine #8 (`fromdate` intraday time drops the 00:00-stamped ONE_DAY bar) \u2014 the same defect that faked the 6-Jul book on 2026-07-10.\n3. Voiding valid paper P&L on a broken audit is the expensive error here \u2014 it destroys a real track-record record on the strength of a query bug. Asymmetric harm \u2192 the recommendation must be blocked.\n\n---\n\n## Adjudication of the two reviews\n\n**QUANT review:** Both numbered defects are valid (fromdate drop; missing degenerate/canary check). Its remediation, however, **overclaims** \u2014 \"fix fromdate and the liquid legs will populate and flip to OK\" presumes fromdate is the *sole* cause and ignores that last week's weekly legs have since EXPIRED and Angel purges expired contracts from its master. Correct in direction, not proven, and wrong for the expired (weekly) legs on re-run. Corrected below.\n\n**RED team:** Invents nothing; both QUANT defects confirmed; adds two real, material findings the QUANT missed (empty-response conflation; wrong data source for a post-expiry book). I endorse RED's substance. I **downweight one RED framing:** purge is presented as a \"co-plausible cause that produces the identical 501/501.\" It cannot \u2014 purge only silences *already-expired* legs; it can explain a clean 501/501 **only if every one of the 501 is expired**, which is unproven. `fromdate` sits in every leg's params regardless of expiry, so it *alone* universally produces 501/501. Rigorous position: **`fromdate` is the confirmed universal cause of today's number; token-purge is a forward re-run RISK on the expired (weekly) legs, not a demonstrated cause of the current result.** Both reviewers thus get corrected in opposite directions, and both action items survive.\n\nDiagnostic that settles it: if any not-yet-expired leg (e.g. a Jul-expiry monthly) is among the 501, its presence proves `fromdate` is universal (purge would have spared it). Run RED's canary (one known-liquid ATM NIFTY weekly, post-fix) to separately test the purge risk before trusting the live source on expired legs.\n\n---\n\n## Surviving material defects\n\n**MD-1 \u2014 `fromdate` carries an intraday time; the 00:00-stamped entry-day ONE_DAY bar is silently dropped. (CONFIRMED, primary, fatal.)**\n- Line: `\"fromdate\": leg.entry_date.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d\") + \" 09:15\"`.\n- Mechanism: ONE_DAY bars are stamped 00:00 IST (task premise + landmine #8). `00:00 < 09:15`, so the entry-day bar falls before the requested window and the broker excludes it \u2014 no error. The loop never matches `c[0][:10] == entry_date`, `entry_bar` stays `None`, and `entry_bar is None or entry_bar[5] == 0` short-circuits to `UNFILLABLE`. The `[5]==0` zero-volume branch is never reached. This parameter is in every leg's request, so it hits all 501 independent of expiry \u2014 it alone forces 501/501. Verbatim repeat of the 2026-07-10 incident on this exact book.\n- Fix: `\"fromdate\": (leg.entry_date - pd.Timedelta(days=1)).strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d\") + \" 00:00\"`.\n\n**MD-2 \u2014 No error/empty-response handling; the audit conflates \"API returned nothing\" with \"market had zero volume.\" (CONFIRMED, structural.)**\n- Line: `candles = smart.getCandleData(params)[\"data\"]` then `for c in candles or []:`.\n- Mechanism: any empty/`None`/error payload \u2014 the fromdate window (MD-1), a purged expired token (MD-3), an auth failure, or an AB1021 rate-limit rejection (SmartAPI typically returns `data: None` on these) \u2014 flows through `candles or []` to `entry_bar = None` \u2192 `UNFILLABLE`. The tool whose entire job is to distinguish tradeable from untradeable cannot tell \"no data\" from a genuine `vol == 0` bar. MD-1 is one symptom of this broader disease; it persists after MD-1 is fixed. (Nuance vs RED: a *fully absent* `data` key would raise `KeyError` and crash rather than flag silently \u2014 but SmartAPI's usual `data: None` on error means the silent-collapse path is the real one, so the conflation stands.)\n- Fix: inspect the raw response for error/empty BEFORE interpreting absence \u2014 return `INCONCLUSIVE / NO DATA`, held distinct from a real `vol == 0` bar; never let a failed call read as `UNFILLABLE`.\n\n**MD-3 \u2014 Wrong data source for a post-expiry book. (Material design defect; purge as a re-run cause = PLAUSIBLE/[INFERENCE], not confirmed.)**\n- Claim: last week's (6-Jul) NFO weeklies are audited against LIVE `getCandleData` on `leg.token`. Today is 2026-07-14; those weeklies have expired, and Angel purges expired option contracts from its master (the reason `AngelDailyOptionCapture` exists). A call keyed on a purged token can return empty \u2192 `UNFILLABLE`.\n- Mechanism / consequence: this makes the QUANT \"re-run \u2192 OK\" overclaim false for the weekly legs, and means the correct fix for a post-expiry audit is not \"fix fromdate and re-query live.\" Note the scope limit (my correction to RED): purge cannot be the cause of *today's* 501/501 unless all 501 are expired; it is a forward risk on the expired subset at re-run.\n- Fix: audit expired legs against the firm's captured / daily NFO bhavcopy store (built precisely to survive purge), honoring landmine #9 \u2014 never read an expiry-day option SETTLE as a price; cash-settle at intrinsic from the underlying.\n\n**MD-4 \u2014 No degenerate/canary sanity gate; a \"void the week\" recommendation was shipped on manifestly broken output. (CONFIRMED, process/output defect.)**\n- Claim: \"all 501 UNFILLABLE, including deep-liquid ATM NIFTY weeklies \u2026 recommends voiding the week's paper results.\"\n- Mechanism: a 100% failure rate that includes the single most-traded derivative in India is physically impossible \u2014 the market did not stop trading. It is the \"too clean / audit is guilty\" signature and must abort the audit, not emit a verdict. Shipping an action that would destroy valid paper P&L on this output is itself the material defect (this is the one that costs the firm).\n- Fix: hard-code a canary assertion \u2014 at least one known-liquid ATM NIFTY weekly reference leg MUST return OK or the run aborts with \"AUDIT BROKEN\"; never void paper P&L on a run that fails the canary.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material notes (labelled \u2014 not causes of the 501 artifact)\n\n- **Daily granularity cannot validate a specific intraday fill.** [Methodology] Even a fromdate-fixed, token-valid re-run only proves the instrument traded that day *in aggregate* \u2014 not that YOUR marginal OTM strike printed at YOUR paper quote/timestamp (D-031: no-fill = DROP). Material to the audit's *fitness for purpose* \u2014 the week stays un-cleared until a minute-level / captured-quote reconcile is run per paper timestamp \u2014 but NOT what produced the 501. Neither \"void\" nor \"bless\" the week is justified until that reconcile is done.\n- **THIN-branch units bug.** [Latent, unreached] `entry_bar[5] * lot_value(leg) < 20 * leg.intended_notional` uses an undefined `lot_value` and mixes a volume field (Angel NFO volume is quantity, not contracts \u2192 double-counts lot size) with a notional multiplier. Real, but the branch is never reached this run, so it did not affect output.\n- **`time.sleep(1.3)`** is compliant (\u22651.2s, AB1021). No issue.\n\n---\n\n## Tail-risk / ruling\n\nThe dangerous tail here is not the audit failing \u2014 it is **acting on it**: voiding a legitimate week of paper track-record on a query bug is an irreversible corruption of the firm's evidence base (worst case: repeated across weeks, we lose the ability to judge sleeves at all). Blocked.\n\n**Required before ANY verdict on the paper week (in order):**\n1. Apply MD-1 (`fromdate = entry_date \u2212 1 day @ 00:00`) and MD-2 (error/empty \u2260 zero-volume; return INCONCLUSIVE).\n2. Add MD-4 canary; abort if the ATM NIFTY weekly reference leg does not pass.\n3. For expired (weekly) legs, source from captured/bhavcopy per MD-3 (landmine #9 intrinsic settle), not live `getCandleData`.\n4. Only then, reconcile each paper fill at minute-level / captured quote (methodology note) before either voiding or blessing the week.\n\n**Kill/review:** the \"void the week\" recommendation is REJECTED on this run; re-submit after steps 1\u20134. Review on re-run.\n\n**Dissents:** none. QUANT and RED converge on the diagnosis; disagreements were on remediation scope and are resolved above (fromdate = universal cause; purge = re-run risk on expired legs; wrong data source confirmed).\n\n---\n\n## Material defects \u2014 numbered\n\n1. `fromdate = entry_date + \" 09:15\"` drops the 00:00-stamped entry-day ONE_DAY bar \u2192 every leg forced to UNFILLABLE (landmine #8; repeat of the 2026-07-10 incident; universal cause of the 501/501). Fix: `fromdate = (entry_date \u2212 1 day) 00:00`.\n2. No error/empty-response handling: `getCandleData(...)[\"data\"]` + `candles or []` silently maps any empty/None/error payload (fromdate window, purged token, auth, AB1021) to UNFILLABLE, conflating \"no data\" with \"zero volume.\" Fix: detect error/empty and return INCONCLUSIVE, distinct from a real `vol == 0` bar.\n3. Wrong data source for a post-expiry book: last week's now-expired weeklies are queried via live `getCandleData` on tokens Angel purges at expiry (a re-run RISK on expired legs, not the cause of today's number). Fix: audit expired legs against captured/bhavcopy data, honoring landmine #9 (never read expiry-day option SETTLE as price; cash-settle at intrinsic).\n4. No degenerate/canary sanity gate, and a \"void the paper week\" recommendation was shipped on physically-impossible 100%-UNFILLABLE output that includes deep-liquid ATM NIFTY weeklies. Fix: assert a liquid reference leg passes or abort (\"AUDIT BROKEN\"); never void paper P&L on this run.\n\nNon-material (labelled): daily-aggregate volume cannot validate a specific intraday fill (audit-fitness limit that keeps the week un-cleared even after a clean re-run \u2014 not a cause of the 501); THIN-branch units bug (undefined `lot_value`, quantity-vs-contracts, volume \u00d7 notional) \u2014 real but unreached; `time.sleep(1.3)` compliant.\n\n----- ANSWER O18b -----\n# T18 \u2014 Consolidated final verdict: paper-book fill audit script\n\n**Verdict: the result is FAKE.** The \"501/501 UNFILLABLE \u2192 void the week\" output is a bug in\nthe audit script, not a property of the paper book. Deep-liquid ATM NIFTY weeklies reading as\nzero-volume / no-bar is physically impossible \u2014 the most heavily traded contracts on the exchange\ncannot all have failed to trade. When the audit says the single most liquid instrument in the\nmarket did not trade, the audit is broken. That alone should have stopped the \"void\" recommendation.\n\nThere is exactly ONE material defect that causes the observed output. The remaining points raised\nacross the two source reviews are either miscast (wrong data schema), unprovable from the snippet,\nor dead code under the primary bug \u2014 all non-material to the 501/501 and correctly demoted below.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT (the sole cause of the result)\n\n**M1 \u2014 `fromdate` carries an intraday time `\" 09:15\"` on a `ONE_DAY` request \u2192 the entry-day bar is silently dropped.**\n- Line: `\"fromdate\": leg.entry_date.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d\") + \" 09:15\"`\n- Mechanism: This is CLAUDE.md DATA LANDMINE #8 verbatim. Angel `getCandleData` ONE_DAY bars are\n  stamped `00:00 IST`. The entry day is the FIRST day of the window, so its bar (ts `00:00`) falls\n  before the requested `fromdate` of `09:15` and is excluded by the API's range filter **with no\n  error**. For every leg the entry-day candle is therefore absent from `candles`; the loop never\n  finds a candle whose `c[0][:10]` equals the entry date; `entry_bar` stays `None`; the guard\n  `if entry_bar is None ... return \"UNFILLABLE\"` fires. This hits ALL legs identically \u2014 including\n  liquid ATM weeklies \u2014 which is precisely why the count is a suspiciously clean 501/501.\n- Fix: `\"fromdate\": (leg.entry_date - pd.Timedelta(days=1)).strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d\") + \" 00:00\"`\n  (documented fix: use `<entry_date \u2212 1 day> 00:00` for daily candles). Align `todate` to a\n  `00:00` base for consistency (harmless as-is).\n- Consequence: the \"paper book was untradeable / void the week\" conclusion is INVALID. Re-run the\n  audit after the fix before drawing any conclusion about fillability.\n\nCorroboration: the `c[0][:10]` ISO-timestamp slice itself is correct (`\"YYYY-MM-DDT...\"[:10]`\nmatches `\"%Y-%m-%d\"`), so it is NOT a second bug \u2014 the date-match is fine; the window-drop is the\nonly failure mechanism. Both source reviews independently confirm M1 as the root cause.\n\n---\n\n## NON-MATERIAL / DEMOTED (raised in the source reviews; do not survive as material defects)\n\n**N1 \u2014 \"Gate on CONTRACTS>0 / bhavcopy SETTLE_PR / LANDMINE #4/#9\" \u2014 REJECTED as written (wrong data schema).**\n`smart.getCandleData` returns broker OHLCV rows `[ts, o, h, l, c, vol]` with **no `CONTRACTS`\ncolumn and no `SETTLE_PR` column**. LANDMINE #4/#9 and the \"gate on CONTRACTS>0 with liquid-expiry\nfallback / never read expiry-day SETTLE\" apparatus are properties of the NSE bhavcopy daily dataset\nin `stocks_options/`, not of the Angel candle API. That prescribed fix cannot be applied to this\ncode because those fields do not exist in the response being parsed. The correctly-scoped residual\nconcern is smaller: a lone daily `vol == 0` candle from the API is too blunt a kill \u2014 Angel can\nreturn a listed-but-untraded far strike/expiry, or the leg may have tokenized a stale/wrong expiry.\nEven in this correct form it is **non-material**: under M1 control never reaches a real bar, so\nnothing downstream of `entry_bar is None` affects the 501/501. Latent robustness note for the\npost-fix re-run only.\n\n**N2 \u2014 THIN threshold `entry_bar[5] * lot_value(leg) < 20 * leg.intended_notional` \u2014 DEMOTED to a spec/definition gap, not a proven inversion.**\n`lot_value` and `intended_notional` are undefined in the snippet and their units aren't shown, so\nthe claimed direction inversion of the `20\u00d7` term cannot be proven from the evidence \u2014 it is\nasserted, not demonstrated. This is also dead code under M1 (never reached). Resolve before\ntrusting the OK/THIN boundary: define both helpers with explicit units and express the rule as\n`intended_notional > participation_cap \u00d7 entry-day traded value \u2192 THIN`, with the cap sourced from\nan approved standard (COST_STANDARDS / RP-39). Non-material.\n\n**N3 \u2014 `lot_value` free/undefined and `pd` used inside `audit_leg` \u2014 snippet-completeness note, not a demonstrated material defect.**\nRaised as a \"NameError on any path reaching a bar.\" But note the observed output is 501 UNFILLABLE,\nNOT a crash: (a) under M1 the guard returns before `lot_value` is ever called, so it cannot raise\ntoday; and (b) `pd.Timedelta` executes in the `params` dict on EVERY call \u2014 if `pd` were genuinely\nunimported, all 501 calls would raise before the API call rather than return \"UNFILLABLE\", which\ncontradicts the observed output. Therefore `pd` must be imported in the real module, and this is a\npasted-snippet completeness issue plus a latent `lot_value` gap that only matters once M1 is fixed\nand bars are found. Define/inject `lot_value` and confirm the `pandas` import before the re-run.\n\n**N4 \u2014 robustness (all minor):**\n- `time.sleep(1.3)` sits after `getCandleData` returns but before the parse; fine for the \u22651.2s\n  AB1021 cadence, but skipped on an exception in `getCandleData`, so a retry storm could trip the\n  limit.\n- `smart.getCandleData(params)[\"data\"]` will `KeyError`/`TypeError` on a `None`/error response\n  rather than degrade gracefully.\n- No retry/backoff despite documented AB1021 flakiness; a transient failure would be miscounted as\n  UNFILLABLE \u2014 a false-negative-on-fillability bias worth a line in a fill-audit, but non-material\n  to today's 501/501.\n\n---\n\n## NUMBERED LIST OF CLAIMED MATERIAL DEFECTS\n\n1. **`fromdate` includes `\" 09:15\"` on a `ONE_DAY` request** \u2192 the entry-day bar (stamped `00:00`)\n   is silently filtered out \u2192 `entry_bar` is always `None` \u2192 501/501 UNFILLABLE. This is DATA\n   LANDMINE #8 and is the **sole** cause of the observed result. Fix: `fromdate = (entry_date \u2212 1\n   day) 00:00`. The \"void the week\" recommendation is invalid; re-run after the fix.\n\n(No other material defect. The prior review's \"CONTRACTS>0 / SETTLE_PR\" fix is inapplicable to the\nAngel candle schema; the THIN-inversion and undefined-name points are non-material \u2014 dead code under\nDefect 1 and unprovable / contradicted by the observed non-crash output. All are latent notes for\nthe post-fix re-run, listed under N1\u2013N4 above.)\n\n----- ANSWER O18c -----\n# T18 \u2014 Review: paper-book fill audit script\n\n**Verdict: MATERIAL DEFECT. The \"501/501 UNFILLABLE \u2192 void the week\" result is a\ndata-fetch artifact, not a real finding. Do NOT void the paper book on this evidence.**\n\n---\n\n## Defect 1 (MATERIAL, root cause) \u2014 `fromdate` carries an intraday time on a ONE_DAY request\n\nLine:\n```python\n\"fromdate\": leg.entry_date.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d\") + \" 09:15\",\n```\n\nMechanism (this is the documented DATA LANDMINE #8, and it is the exact bug that\nbit the 6-Jul book on 2026-07-10):\n\n- Angel `getCandleData` `ONE_DAY` bars are stamped at **00:00 IST**, not at market\n  open.\n- The request asks for `fromdate = entry_date 09:15`. The entry-day daily bar is\n  stamped `entry_date 00:00`, which is **before** 09:15, so the API **silently\n  drops the entry-day bar** (no error, no exception \u2014 the first returned bar is the\n  *next* trading day at 00:00).\n- The loop then searches for a bar whose date equals the entry date:\n  ```python\n  if c[0][:10] == leg.entry_date.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d\"):\n  ```\n  That bar is never present, so `entry_bar` stays `None` for **every** leg \u2192\n  `return \"UNFILLABLE\"` fires unconditionally \u2192 `UNFILLABLE 501`.\n\nThis is why *every* leg fails identically, including deep-liquid ATM NIFTY\nweeklies that trade lakhs of contracts a day. The output reflects a broken\nquery window, not market illiquidity. The volume check (`entry_bar[5] == 0`) and\nthe THIN branch never even execute \u2014 the function short-circuits on the missing\nbar.\n\n**Fix:** request daily candles from midnight of the *prior* calendar day, no\nintraday time:\n```python\n\"fromdate\": (leg.entry_date - pd.Timedelta(days=1)).strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d\") + \" 00:00\",\n```\n(Per landmine #8: use `<date-1> 00:00`. At minimum use `entry_date 00:00` so the\n00:00-stamped bar is `>= fromdate` and is returned.) After the fix, re-run and the\nliquid NIFTY weeklies will show real, non-zero volume.\n\n---\n\n## Defect 2 (MATERIAL) \u2014 the conclusion is unsupported and must be retracted\n\nThe recommendation to \"void the week's paper results\" is drawn entirely from the\nartifact in Defect 1. Two sanity checks that were skipped and would have caught it:\n\n- **Impossible-result tell:** deep-liquid ATM NIFTY weeklies cannot have zero\n  entry-day volume. Any leg the author *knows* is liquid returning UNFILLABLE is\n  proof the fetch is broken, not the book.\n- **Uniform-failure tell:** a *perfectly* uniform 501/501 identical outcome is the\n  signature of a systematic query bug, not of 501 independent instruments each\n  happening to be untradeable. Real illiquidity produces a mix (OK / THIN /\n  UNFILLABLE).\n\nNo known-liquid control leg was included to validate the pipeline before trusting\nits verdict. The correct action is to fix Defect 1, re-audit, and only then judge\ntradeability.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material / secondary notes (clearly labelled \u2014 none of these caused the output)\n\n- **THIN branch unit risk (latent):**\n  `entry_bar[5] * lot_value(leg) < 20 * leg.intended_notional`. If Angel candle\n  `volume` (`c[5]`) is already in units (contracts \u00d7 lot size) and `lot_value`\n  also folds in lot size, this double-counts lot size and the liquidity gate is\n  mis-scaled. Cannot confirm without the definitions of `lot_value` /\n  `intended_notional`; the branch never runs given Defect 1, so it is not the\n  cause here. Verify the units once the fetch is fixed.\n- `lot_value` is used as a free/global name inside `audit_leg` (not a parameter and\n  not defined in scope shown). Style/robustness only.\n- `todate = entry_date + 5 calendar days` uses calendar, not trading, days \u2014 fine\n  for merely locating the entry-day bar; would need care only if used for\n  multi-day liquidity windows around holidays. Non-material.\n- `time.sleep(1.3)` after the call is adequate for the AB1021 rate limit\n  (\u22651.2s/req). Fine.\n\n---\n\n## Material defects (numbered)\n\n1. `fromdate` includes an intraday time (`\" 09:15\"`) on a `ONE_DAY` request; since\n   Angel daily bars are stamped 00:00 IST, the entry-day bar is silently dropped,\n   `entry_bar` is always `None`, and every leg is falsely flagged UNFILLABLE. Fix:\n   `fromdate = (entry_date - 1 day) 00:00` (or `entry_date 00:00`).\n2. The \"void the week's paper results\" conclusion rests entirely on the Defect-1\n   artifact and is invalid; it also failed obvious sanity checks (liquid NIFTY\n   weeklies cannot be zero-volume; a uniform 501/501 result signals a fetch bug).\n   Retract and re-run after the fix.\n\n----- ANSWER O18d -----\n# T18 Review \u2014 paper-book fill audit script\n\n## Verdict\nThe result is a **data-fetch artifact, not a real finding**. \"501/501 UNFILLABLE\"\nis produced by a known Angel `getCandleData` bug baked into the very first field of\n`params`. The conclusion that the paper book was untradeable is FAKE, and the\nrecommendation to void the week's paper results must be reversed. The tell is in\nthe observation itself: deep-liquid ATM NIFTY weeklies cannot plausibly have a\nzero-volume / no-bar entry day \u2014 if the most liquid instrument on the exchange\ncomes back UNFILLABLE, the fetch is broken, not the market.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 1 (root cause \u2014 explains all 501) \u2014 `fromdate` carries an intraday time\n\n```python\n\"fromdate\": leg.entry_date.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d\") + \" 09:15\",\n```\n\n- **Mechanism.** The scenario states (and the firm CLAUDE.md landmine #8 documents)\n  that Angel ONE_DAY candles are stamped at **00:00 IST**. The entry-day bar therefore\n  carries the timestamp `entry_date 00:00`. The request asks for data\n  `fromdate = entry_date 09:15`. Because `00:00 < 09:15`, the entry-day bar falls\n  *before* the requested window and is **silently dropped by the API (no error)**.\n  The returned `candles` contain only `entry_date+1 \u2026 entry_date+5` (each stamped\n  00:00, all after 09:15). The loop then searches for a candle whose date equals\n  `entry_date`, finds none, so `entry_bar` stays `None`, and the function returns\n  `\"UNFILLABLE\"`. This fires for **every leg regardless of liquidity**, which is\n  exactly why the count is a flat 501 with no OK/THIN \u2014 a uniform result is the\n  signature of a systematic fetch bug, not a market condition.\n- **Correct fix.** Per the landmine, use the day *before* entry at midnight so the\n  entry-day 00:00 bar is unambiguously inside the window:\n  ```python\n  \"fromdate\": (leg.entry_date - pd.Timedelta(days=1)).strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d\") + \" 00:00\",\n  ```\n  (At minimum `entry_date 00:00`; `date-1 00:00` is the documented safe form because\n  the entry-day stamp sits exactly on the boundary.) After this fix the entry-day\n  bar is returned and the volume test becomes meaningful.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 2 \u2014 API failures are silently reclassified as UNFILLABLE\n\n```python\ncandles = smart.getCandleData(params)[\"data\"]\n...\nfor c in candles or []:\n```\n\n- **Mechanism.** There is no retry / error handling. Angel throttles at AB1021 and the\n  corporate proxy is flaky; a rate-limited or transient-failed call that returns\n  `None`/empty (or an exception on `[\"data\"]`) is absorbed by `candles or []`, yields\n  no `entry_bar`, and returns `\"UNFILLABLE\"` \u2014 indistinguishable from a genuinely\n  illiquid leg. So even with Defect 1 fixed, the audit cannot tell \"API hiccup\" from\n  \"no liquidity,\" and any batch failure inflates the UNFILLABLE count.\n- **Fix.** Retry with backoff on AB1021/empty, and return a distinct status\n  (e.g. `\"NO_DATA\"` / `\"API_ERROR\"`) when the call fails, so fetch problems never\n  masquerade as a liquidity verdict. `time.sleep(1.3)` should also run on the retry\n  path, not only the success path.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 3 \u2014 pulling expired-contract candles live is the wrong source\n\n- **Mechanism.** These are *last week's* legs, and the example calls out NIFTY\n  **weeklies** \u2014 many will already have expired by the 2026-07-13 audit date. Angel\n  **purges expired option contracts from its master**, so `getCandleData` on a purged\n  token returns empty data \u2192 `entry_bar = None` \u2192 `\"UNFILLABLE\"`. This is a second,\n  independent way to manufacture false UNFILLABLE that is an artifact of purging, not\n  of tradeability. Auditing fills by re-fetching expired legs from the *live* candle\n  endpoint is structurally unreliable.\n- **Fix.** For expired/weekly legs, read from the daily option-capture archive\n  (`AngelDailyOptionCapture` output) or the NFO bhavcopy for the entry date rather\n  than live `getCandleData`. (When using bhavcopy, respect landmine #9: on expiry day\n  the option `SETTLE_PR` is the underlying settlement level, and gate on `CONTRACTS>0`.)\n\n---\n\n## Secondary / non-material comments (do not drive the reported result)\n\n- **THIN branch unit consistency (verify).** `entry_bar[5] * lot_value(leg)` assumes the\n  candle volume is expressed in *lots*. If Angel returns option volume in *units*\n  (quantity already inclusive of lot size), multiplying by a per-lot rupee value\n  double-counts the lot multiplier and mis-scales the participation gate. It never\n  fires in the current output (all UNFILLABLE), so it does not affect the reported\n  result, but it should be checked before the THIN/OK split is trusted. The `20\u00d7`\n  gate itself (day traded-value \u2265 20\u00d7 intended notional, i.e. \u22645% participation) is a\n  reasonable liquidity threshold \u2014 not a defect.\n- **Fillability proxy is weak.** Day-level `volume > 0` does not prove the *intended\n  entry price* was reachable. A day can trade volume yet never touch a limit set away\n  from the bar's high/low (D-031: no-fill = DROP). A stronger audit checks the intended\n  fill price against the bar's high/low range, not just that some volume existed.\n- **Retroactive candle-based fill audit is inherently approximate** vs. the actual\n  quote / order book at entry time; treat OK/THIN as indicative, not proof.\n- `pd`, `lot_value`, and `smart` are assumed defined in scope; `c[0][:10]` date\n  matching is fine for Angel's ISO timestamps (`\"YYYY-MM-DDT00:00:00+05:30\"`).\n\n---\n\n## Numbered list of claimed material defects\n1. **`fromdate` includes an intraday time `\" 09:15\"`** while ONE_DAY bars are stamped\n   00:00 IST \u2014 the entry-day bar is silently dropped, so `entry_bar` is never found and\n   **all 501 legs return UNFILLABLE**. This is the sole cause of the reported result;\n   the \"book was untradeable / void the week\" conclusion is a fetch artifact and is\n   wrong. Fix: `fromdate = (entry_date - 1 day) 00:00`.\n2. **No retry / error handling** \u2014 API rate-limit (AB1021), proxy failures, or empty\n   responses are absorbed by `candles or []` and reclassified as UNFILLABLE,\n   indistinguishable from real illiquidity. Fix: retry with backoff and emit a distinct\n   NO_DATA/API_ERROR status.\n3. **Wrong data source for expired legs** \u2014 expired weeklies are purged from Angel's\n   master, so live `getCandleData` returns empty \u2192 false UNFILLABLE. Fix: read the\n   daily option-capture archive / NFO bhavcopy for the entry date (respecting the\n   expiry-day SETTLE_PR and CONTRACTS>0 bhavcopy landmines).", "ids": ["O18a", "O18b", "O18c", "O18d"]}, {"tid": "T19", "key": "## T19 \u2014 CLEAN (data-landmine theme)\n**No material defect.** Survivorship-complete union panel INCLUDING delisted names,\nstale-price mask applied, PIT membership as-of each rebalance, causal 12-1 momentum,\nnext-open entries with circuit/zero-volume no-fill, delisting losses realized\nexplicitly, and a turnover-matched random-basket null from the same panel. The 93rd\npercentile / +4.3pp over null p50 claim is internally consistent and honestly\nframed.\n**Reject as false positives.** \"Mar/Sep snapshots are stale between snapshot dates\"\n(that IS the point-in-time convention \u2014 the snapshots are the correct as-of data);\n\"momentum uses shift(21).pct_change(231) \u2014 lookahead\" (it is t-252..t-21, causal);\n\"stale mask hides losses\" (the mask excludes fabricated frozen prints \u2014 it removes\nfake data, standing rule); \"no stop-loss / high maxDD\" (risk characteristics, not\nresult-validity defects).\n", "answers": "----- ANSWER O19a -----\n# T19 \u2014 Consolidated arbiter verdict: mid-cap momentum on the union panel\n\nReviewing the two prior reviews (a base reviewer and a red-team) against the pasted\nsubmission only. My job is to keep defects that survive scrutiny, correct the mechanism\nwhere a reviewer got it wrong, and discard invented or over-hedged items. Evidence is the\ntask text alone; where a claim depends on unstated context (pandas version, `run_engine`\ninternals, `eligible_midcap` body, mask conformance), I say so and rank accordingly.\n\n---\n\n## VERDICT SUMMARY\n\nThe submission has at least one **material** defect that undermines the headline\n(the stale mask fed into `pct_change`), plus a **material, likely** null-construction\nbias and a **material, conditional** mask-alignment risk. The base reviewer's Defect 1 is\ndirectionally right but mechanistically mis-stated; the red-team corrected it. The base\nreviewer's Defect 2 does **not** survive. The red-team surfaced two real items the base\nreviewer missed (mask alignment; null universe). I adopt the red-team's mechanism, downgrade\nthe base reviewer's overconfidence, and reject the invented items from both.\n\n---\n\n## DEFECT 1 (MATERIAL) \u2014 `close.mask(stale).pct_change(...)`: the stale mask is fed into the return/momentum computation, and its effect is undefined-by-inspection because `fill_method` is unpinned\n\nCode:\n```python\nclose = close.mask(stale)                 # stale/frozen prices -> NaN, in place\nret = close.pct_change()                  # engine P&L series\nmom = close.shift(21).pct_change(231)     # ranking signal\n```\n\nBoth the return series `ret` (which the engine compounds into P&L) and the ranking signal\n`mom` are computed on the holed panel. This is a real defect. **But the base reviewer's\nstated mechanism \u2014 \"`pct_change(231)` takes the value 231 rows back and splices across the\nexcised interval as if the days did not exist\" \u2014 is wrong as a description of pandas\nsemantics.** `pandas` `pct_change` does not renumber the index or reach past a NaN to the\nnext valid observation. It applies `fill_method` first, then differences fixed row offsets.\nThe red-team is correct and I adopt its framing:\n\n- **Legacy pandas (< 2.1), default `fill_method='pad'`:** the masked NaN holes are\n  **forward-filled before differencing**, which *silently reverses the mask*. The frozen\n  run is padded back to the last-traded price, and on the un-freeze day the return is\n  computed against that padded price \u2014 reintroducing exactly the stale-price jump the mask\n  was meant to remove. The base reviewer's conclusion (\"books the whole frozen move as one\n  tradeable day-return\") is right, but by padding, not gap-skipping, and it happens\n  regardless of the `shift`.\n- **Modern pandas (>= 2.1 deprecation / 3.0 default `fill_method=None`):** NaN is not\n  filled; any window touching a masked cell yields NaN, so those names/months are dropped\n  by the downstream `.dropna()`. Safer (no phantom return booked), but introduces\n  **unmeasured universe attrition** \u2014 nobody counts how many names/months silently vanish.\n\nBecause the firm's pandas version is not stated, the behavior of this line is not knowable\nby inspection. In the pad regime it directly inflates returns and the ranking signal,\nconcentrated in illiquid mid-caps \u2014 precisely the names momentum overweights and precisely\nwhere stale prints live \u2014 so the 93rd-percentile claim would be biased high. That\npossibility alone is enough to withhold trust from the headline.\n\n**Fix (adopting the red-team's, which supersedes the base reviewer's incomplete version):**\nnever feed a masked series to `pct_change`. Compute momentum/returns on the RAW close with\nexplicit endpoints \u2014 `mom = close_raw.shift(21) / close_raw.shift(252) - 1` \u2014 and apply the\nstale mask as an **eligibility filter only** (drop a name from `row` when it is stale as-of\n`me` or when its trailing signal window contains a stale run), never to the price series.\nIf `pct_change` must be used, pin `fill_method=None` explicitly AND assert masked cells\nproduce NaN AND log the dropped-name count. The base reviewer's fix omits the `fill_method`\npin, which is the actual root cause.\n\nSeverity note: the base reviewer called this \"fatal to the headline number\" unconditionally.\nThat overstates certainty \u2014 the corruption is conditional on the pandas regime. It remains\n**material**: the result cannot be accepted until the regime is established and the mask is\nmoved to eligibility.\n\n---\n\n## DEFECT 2 (MATERIAL, LIKELY) \u2014 the null baskets are probably NOT drawn from the post-`eligible_midcap` mid-cap set, so the percentile is biased in the strategy's favor\n\nThe strategy universe each month is `row` **after** `eligible_midcap(row.index, asof=me)`,\ni.e. mid-caps only. The null is described as \"10,000 random 40-name baskets from the SAME\npanel, SAME PIT universe, SAME monthly rebalance dates and cost model.\" It says \"SAME PIT\nuniverse\" \u2014 it does **not** say \"same eligible mid-cap subset.\" If the null samples the full\nPIT universe (large + mid) while the strategy is confined to mid-caps, the null's return\ndistribution reflects a broader, likely lower-vol, large-cap-inclusive population. The\ncomparison then attributes a **size premium** to momentum: a mid-cap-only strategy will sit\nhigh in a large-cap-inclusive null regardless of any momentum edge. The 93rd-percentile is\nbiased high by construction.\n\nThe base reviewer gave the null full credit (\"well-specified, apples-to-apples\"), resolving\nthe ambiguity charitably without warrant. The red-team caught this and is correct.\n\n**Fix:** draw the null's 40-name baskets each month from the *identical* eligible mid-cap\nset the strategy chooses from (post-`eligible_midcap`, post-`dropna`, same >=120 gate), not\nfrom the broader PIT universe. Then re-run the 10,000-draw percentile. If the write-up in\nfact already samples the eligible mid-cap set, this is resolved \u2014 but as written it is\nambiguous and the natural reading (\"SAME PIT universe\") points to the broader set, so this\nmust be confirmed before the percentile stands.\n\n---\n\n## DEFECT 3 (MATERIAL, CONDITIONAL) \u2014 `close.mask(stale)` never verifies index/column conformance; `DataFrame.mask` aligns by label, and missing labels become False (unmasked)\n\n`stale_mask.parquet` is a separate artifact from `close_panel_return_v11.parquet`.\n`DataFrame.mask(cond)` aligns `cond` to the caller by label; any row/column label present in\n`close` but absent (or differently named) in `stale` yields NaN in the condition, which\n`mask` treats as False \u2014 **that cell is never masked even if it is stale.** Because the panel\nis the *union incl. delisted* and the mask is a distinct file, misalignment is most likely\nexactly on the delisted / illiquid tail, where staleness is worst. Stale runs on those names\nwould silently escape masking and flow into `ret`/`mom`. The red-team surfaced this; both the\nbase reviewer and the submission are silent on conformance.\n\n**Fix:** before masking, assert the mask and panel share identical index and columns (or\nreindex the mask onto the panel and fill missing cells conservatively as stale = True), and\nlog any non-conforming labels.\n\nThis is conditional on whether the two artifacts were built to conform. It is real risk, not\ndemonstrated corruption, so it ranks below Defects 1 and 2 but is still material as a required\npre-check.\n\n---\n\n## REJECTED \u2014 base reviewer's Defect 2 (`mom.loc[me]` / `me` not a real session) does NOT survive\n\nThe base reviewer worried that if `me` is a calendar month-end, `mom.loc[me]` either\nKeyErrors or returns a forward-filled/NaN row. This does not hold as a defect:\n\n- If `me` is absent from the index, `mom.loc[me, ...]` **raises KeyError** \u2014 a loud failure,\n  not a silent corruption. A run that produced a 16.2% CAGR headline did not KeyError on\n  every rebalance, so `me` must be resolving to real index labels.\n- The \"silent ffilled price\" scenario requires the panel to have been reindexed to calendar\n  days with forward-fill \u2014 an assumption the base reviewer invented and admitted it could not\n  confirm. The standard reading (`month_ends` derived from the panel's own trading-session\n  index) gives a clean read with no defect.\n- The base reviewer's own numbered list demotes this to a \"verify\" note anyway, so counting\n  it as a material defect is inflation.\n\nI keep it only as a one-line **verification item**: confirm `month_ends` are trading sessions\ndrawn from the panel index (they almost certainly are, given the run completed).\n\n---\n\n## REJECTED \u2014 the \"42 Mar/Sep snapshots vs 2014-2025\" count \"mismatch\"\n\nThe base reviewer flagged 42 semiannual snapshots as inconsistent with a ~12-year test.\nThis is a non-issue: 42 semiannual snapshots is ~21 years, which is exactly what you need if\nthe panel starts well before 2014 (you need pre-2014 snapshots to form the 12-month signal\nfor the earliest 2014 rebalances). A panel spanning roughly 2004-2025 giving 42 snapshots is\nexpected. The red-team correctly dismissed this. Not a defect.\n\n---\n\n## CONFIRMED-CORRECT PARTS (both reviewers concur, and I agree)\n\n- **Signal direction/timing:** `close.shift(21).pct_change(231)` is a genuine 12-1 momentum\n  (skip the most recent ~month; formation window ~t-252 to t-21) computed only from data\n  through the signal date. The formula's direction is right; the problem is the *panel* it\n  runs on (Defect 1), not the definition.\n- **PIT membership:** `members.asof(me)` = latest snapshot on-or-before `me` \u2192 no membership\n  lookahead. Correct.\n- **Fill realism:** entries next session at open, no-fill on circuit-locked / zero-volume\n  opens via `fill_check`, 45bp/side. Honest; addresses the firm's circuit/thin-volume\n  landmine.\n- **Delisting anti-survivorship intent:** mark to last traded price, realize loss on the\n  delisting date, no silent drop. Correct intent (see verify-item on its interaction with the\n  mask below).\n\n---\n\n## VERIFY-ONLY ITEMS (not counted as material defects)\n\n- **`eligible_midcap` breakpoints PIT vs full-period.** Both reviewers flagged this and could\n  not confirm from the text. If the mid-cap breakpoints are computed over the full sample\n  rather than as-of `me`, that is a genuine additional lookahead. Cannot be asserted from the\n  text; upgrade to a confirmed defect only if the breakpoints turn out to be full-period.\n- **Stale-mask x delisting mark-out interaction.** A name usually goes stale (frozen prints)\n  before it delists. The mask NaNs the pre-delisting freeze while the delisting logic realizes\n  a loss at last-traded price on the delisting date; the two could double-book or omit the\n  terminal move. Unresolvable without `run_engine` internals.\n- **`month_ends` are trading sessions** (see Rejected base-Defect-2). Almost certainly fine.\n- **Thin-universe skips (`len(row) < 120: continue`).** Non-material to correctness, but log\n  how many months (especially early years) are skipped, since silently dropping early years\n  changes the effective test length.\n- **Capacity / equal-weight on mid-caps (`w = 1/40`, no ADV weighting).** Non-material to\n  correctness of the reported number; material to deployability. Run a capacity check before\n  sizing.\n\n---\n\n## NUMBERED LIST \u2014 material defects that survive\n\n1. **`close.mask(stale)` is fed into `pct_change` for both `ret` and `mom`, with\n   `fill_method` unpinned.** In legacy-pandas `pad` mode the mask is silently reversed\n   (frozen prices forward-filled, stale-price jump re-enters and inflates ranks + P&L on\n   illiquid mid-caps); in modern `None` mode the masked names NaN-out and are silently\n   dropped (unmeasured universe attrition). Either way the headline is untrustworthy as\n   written. Fix: compute momentum/returns on the RAW close\n   (`close_raw.shift(21)/close_raw.shift(252)-1`), apply the stale mask to *eligibility only*,\n   never feed a masked series to `pct_change` without an explicit `fill_method=None` plus\n   assertions. (The base reviewer's \"splices across the gap\" mechanism is wrong; the pandas\n   root cause is the `fill_method` default.)\n\n2. **Null-basket universe is not stated to be the post-`eligible_midcap` mid-cap subset.** If\n   the null draws from the full PIT universe while the strategy is mid-cap-only, the\n   93rd-percentile is biased high because a size premium is attributed to momentum. Fix: draw\n   each month's null baskets from the identical eligible mid-cap set the strategy chooses from,\n   then re-run the 10,000-draw percentile.\n\n3. **`close.mask(stale)` conformance is never asserted.** `DataFrame.mask` aligns by label;\n   any label in `close` missing from `stale` is treated as False (unmasked), so stale runs on\n   the delisted/illiquid tail can silently escape masking. Fix: assert identical index and\n   columns (or reindex the mask conservatively to stale = True on missing labels) before\n   masking.\n\n(Verify-only, explicitly NOT counted as material defects: `eligible_midcap` PIT vs\nfull-period breakpoints \u2014 becomes a real lookahead if full-period; stale-mask x delisting\nmark-out interaction; confirm `month_ends` are trading sessions. Rejected as non-defects: the\n`mom.loc[me]`-not-a-session claim \u2014 `loc` KeyErrors loudly rather than silently corrupting,\nso a completed 16.2% run implies sessions were used; and the 42-snapshots-vs-12-years \"count\nmismatch\" \u2014 42 semiannual snapshots ~ 21 years, consistent with a pre-2014 panel start.)\n\n----- ANSWER O19b -----\n# T19 \u2014 Review: mid-cap momentum on the union panel (arm A)\n\n## Verdict\nOne **material defect** that invalidates the headline significance claim (the null is\nnot turnover-matched, despite the explicit claim that it is), plus one secondary\nmaterial bias (delisting mark-to-last-price). The mechanics that usually break these\nbacktests \u2014 momentum formula, survivorship, PIT membership, execution timing \u2014 are\nactually done correctly here, so I flag only what is genuinely wrong.\n\n## What is done correctly (so I am not inventing problems)\n- **Momentum formula is right.** `mom = close.shift(21).pct_change(231)` evaluates to\n  `close[t-21]/close[t-252] - 1` \u2014 a true 12-1 (11-month) momentum that skips the most\n  recent month. At the signal date `me` it uses only data through `me-21`, so there is\n  **no lookahead** in the signal. The comment \"through the signal date\" is if anything\n  understating how conservative it is.\n- **Survivorship is handled.** Union panel incl. delisted + `members.asof(me)` (latest\n  PIT snapshot on-or-before `me`, 42 Mar/Sep snapshots) + `eligible_midcap(..., asof=me)`\n  are all point-in-time. No forward-looking universe.\n- **Execution timing is clean.** Signal at `me`, target set for `first_session_after(me)`,\n  filled at that session's open, with `fill_check` suppressing fills on circuit-locked /\n  zero-volume opens. Signal (close) precedes fill (next-session open) \u2014 no fill lookahead.\n- **Delistings are not silently dropped** (directionally the honest choice \u2014 see caveat below).\n- On modern pandas (this firm runs Py 3.14 \u2192 pandas \u2265 2.2, `pct_change` default\n  `fill_method=None`) the `close.mask(stale)` NaNs are **not** forward-filled back into the\n  signal, so masked stale runs correctly yield NaN momentum that gets `dropna()`'d. Not a bug here.\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 1 \u2014 The null is NOT turnover-matched; the claim \"turnover-matched by construction\" is false, and it inflates the whole significance result\n**Where:** the `null:` comment \u2014 *\"10,000 random 40-name baskets ... SAME monthly rebalance\ndates and cost model (turnover-matched by construction).\"*\n\n**Mechanism.** A random 40-name basket **redrawn independently each month** has near-maximal\nturnover: with a mid-cap eligible universe of roughly 120-200 names, month-to-month overlap is\n~`40*40/N` \u2248 8-13 names, i.e. **~65-80% one-way turnover**. A 12-1 momentum top-40 is highly\npersistent month-to-month (11 of the 12 lookback months overlap), so its turnover is far\nlower \u2014 **~20-40% one-way**. Both baskets are charged the same 45bp/side. Therefore the null\npays roughly **double the transaction costs** of the strategy. Order-of-magnitude cost drag:\n- Momentum \u2248 35% \u00d7 2 sides \u00d7 45bp \u00d7 12 \u2248 **~3.8%/yr** [INFERENCE, illustrative].\n- Random   \u2248 73% \u00d7 2 sides \u00d7 45bp \u00d7 12 \u2248 **~7.9%/yr** [INFERENCE, illustrative].\n- Difference \u2248 **~4%/yr**, purely from trading frequency, not from selection skill.\n\nThe strategy beats the null median by 16.2% \u2212 11.9% = **4.3pp**. That entire gap is comparable\nto \u2014 likely fully explained by \u2014 the turnover/cost differential above. In other words, the\n93rd-percentile result may be measuring *\"momentum trades less than a monkey re-picking every\nmonth,\"* not *\"momentum picks better names.\"* The stated \"turnover-matched by construction\" is\nsimply not true for an independent monthly redraw; nothing in the described construction matches\nturnover. This makes the headline significance claim **not credible**.\n\n**Fix (any one):**\n1. Compare on **gross** returns (strip the 45bp/side from both legs) to isolate selection skill\n   from cost drag; *and* separately report net.\n2. Build a **genuinely turnover-matched null** \u2014 e.g. random baskets that carry over the same\n   fraction of names each month (or the same average holding period) as the realized momentum\n   book, so both legs incur the same trading cost.\n3. Use a **rank-shuffle null**: keep the momentum turnover profile but randomize which name each\n   rank maps to. This preserves turnover and universe exactly and isolates the value of the\n   momentum ordering.\nThen recompute the percentile. Expect the strategy to fall well below the current 93rd\npercentile once the cost asymmetry is removed.\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT 2 (secondary, magnitude-dependent) \u2014 delistings realized at \"last traded price\" overstate recovery\n**Where:** *\"if a held name stops trading, the position is marked to its last traded price and the\nloss realized on the delisting date.\"*\n\n**Mechanism.** \"Last traded price\" is the print immediately before a suspension/delisting halt.\nFor names that delist due to insolvency/fraud, the eventual recovery is typically ~0, well below\nthe last quoted print, so realizing the exit at last-traded price **understates the loss and\nbiases CAGR upward**. Momentum specifically holds recent winners, a subset of which are pump-then-\ncollapse / fraud names (exactly the population that delists after a halt), so the exposure is\nnon-trivial rather than theoretical. It is directionally more honest than a silent drop, but it is\nstill optimistic.\n\n**Fix.** Apply a delisting haircut / recovery assumption (e.g. exit at last-traded minus a\nbankruptcy haircut, or at a documented recovery value) and confirm the stale_mask actually catches\nthe pre-delisting frozen prints so the \"last traded price\" is not itself a stale/artificial level.\nQuantify the drag on CAGR/maxDD.\n\n## Non-material comments (clearly labelled, not defects)\n- **[VERIFY, non-conclusive] Data identity vs filename.** The file is\n  `close_panel_return_v11.parquet` (\"return\") but is consumed as a price/close panel\n  (`.pct_change()`, `.pct_change(231)`). If it is in fact a returns panel, every computation is\n  differencing returns and the whole run is nonsense. The comment (\"union panel incl. delisted\")\n  reads as prices, so this is probably just a naming convention \u2014 but confirm one known ticker's\n  values are levels, not returns, before trusting the run.\n- **[non-material] `ret = close.pct_change()`** is assigned but unused in the shown code (presumably\n  consumed inside `run_engine`); harmless, just dead in the excerpt.\n- **[non-material] 45bp/side for mid-caps at a 40-name size** may be optimistic on impact, but it is\n  a COST_STANDARDS parameter choice, not a coding defect. Note only that the *same* number applied\n  asymmetrically across turnover levels is what produces Defect 1.\n- **[non-material] `if len(row) < 120: continue`** silently skips low-breadth months; reasonable as a\n  liquidity/breadth gate but worth reporting how many early months it drops (start-window effect).\n\n## Claimed material defects\n1. **Null is not turnover-matched** despite the explicit \"turnover-matched by construction\" claim.\n   Random monthly redraw (~65-80% turnover) pays ~2x the costs of momentum (~20-40% turnover); the\n   ~4%/yr cost differential is as large as the entire 4.3pp edge over the null median, so the\n   93rd-percentile significance is an artifact of trading frequency, not selection skill. Fix:\n   gross-return comparison, a turnover-matched null, or a rank-shuffle null; then recompute the\n   percentile.\n2. **Delistings realized at \"last traded price\"** overstate recovery on insolvency/fraud exits (a\n   population momentum is exposed to), biasing CAGR up. Fix: apply a delisting haircut/recovery\n   assumption and verify the stale mask removes pre-halt frozen prints.\n\n----- ANSWER O19c -----\n# T19 \u2014 Review: mid-cap momentum on the union panel (arm B)\n\n**Verdict: one material defect \u2014 the null is not turnover-matched, and the resulting\ncost asymmetry is large enough to account for the entire reported edge.** The signal\nconstruction and execution/survivorship handling are otherwise clean.\n\n---\n\n## What is correct (checked, not defects)\n\n- **12-1 momentum is built without lookahead.** `mom = close.shift(21).pct_change(231)`\n  evaluates at date `t` to `close[t-21]/close[t-252] - 1`, i.e. the return from ~12\n  months ago to ~1 month ago, known strictly as of `t`. (Verified: `close.shift(21)`\n  gives `close[t-21]`, and `.pct_change(231)` on that gives `close[t-21]/close[t-252]-1`.\n  Equivalent to `close.pct_change(231).shift(21)`.) Correct skip-a-month construction.\n- **Signal/execution gap is conservative.** Signal uses data through `t-21`; entries are\n  `first_session_after(me)` filled at open. No same-bar or signal-day fill.\n- **PIT membership is point-in-time.** `members.asof(me)` takes the latest snapshot on or\n  before `me` \u2014 no forward peeking into a later Mar/Sep reconstitution.\n- **Survivorship is handled.** Union panel incl. delisted; delisted holds are marked to\n  last traded price and the loss realized on the delist date (no silent drop). Mark-to-\n  last-price mildly *understates* bankruptcy losses, but momentum rarely holds names on the\n  way to a bankruptcy delist (they have poor recent momentum), so this bias helps the\n  random null more than the strategy \u2014 net conservative for the strategy, not a defect\n  in its favour. (Non-material comment.)\n- **Execution realism present:** no-fill on circuit-locked/zero-volume opens; 45bp/side.\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECT\n\n### D1 \u2014 The random-basket null is NOT turnover-matched; the cost model over-penalizes it, inflating the strategy's percentile. The claim \"turnover-matched by construction\" is false.\n\n- **Where:** the null comment \u2014 *\"10,000 random 40-name baskets ... SAME monthly rebalance\n  dates and cost model (turnover-matched by construction).\"*\n- **Mechanism.** A 40-name basket redrawn from the eligible pool on every monthly rebalance\n  has structurally high turnover, because random selection has no persistence. Drawing 40\n  of `N` names, the expected month-to-month overlap is `40\u00b740/N`, so one-way turnover is\n  `1 - 40/N` \u2014 roughly **0.67\u20130.87** for a plausible eligible mid-cap pool of N\u2248120\u2013300\n  names. A 12-1 momentum top-40 book persists across months; its one-way turnover is\n  typically **~0.25\u20130.35**. Under a per-side (45bp) cost model, cost drag scales with\n  turnover magnitude, so the null is charged **~5\u20136%/yr more** than the momentum book:\n\n  | pool N | random one-way turnover | random annual cost | momentum (T\u22480.30) | cost gap |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|\n  | 150 | 0.73 | ~8.2% | ~3.3% | **~4.9%/yr** |\n  | 250 | 0.84 | ~9.5% | ~3.3% | **~6.2%/yr** |\n\n  The reported edge is only **16.2% \u2212 11.9% = 4.3%/yr**. The turnover-induced cost penalty\n  on the null (~5\u20136%/yr) is **larger than the entire apparent edge.** A correctly matched\n  null would have a materially higher p50 (likely \u2265 the strategy's 16.2%), which would drop\n  the strategy from the 93rd percentile toward \u2014 or below \u2014 the median. The headline\n  \"93rd-percentile vs random null\" is an artifact of an unfair cost handicap, not evidence\n  of momentum selection value.\n- **Why the label is wrong.** \"Turnover-matched by construction\" conflates *rebalance\n  schedule* (same dates) with *turnover magnitude* (fraction of book traded). Matching the\n  schedule does not match the magnitude; random selection cannot be turnover-matched \"by\n  construction\" \u2014 it is the opposite, near-maximal turnover.\n- **Fix (any one, ideally report all):**\n  1. Constrain each null basket to the strategy's *realized* monthly turnover (e.g. carry\n     over a matched fraction of names each rebalance so null and strategy trade the same\n     book fraction), then re-rank the percentile; **or**\n  2. Compare **gross** returns (costs stripped from both) to isolate selection from the\n     cost handicap; **or**\n  3. Charge the null the strategy's realized per-period cost schedule rather than its own\n     (much larger) turnover.\n  Re-report the percentile after matching; treat the current 93rd-percentile / 16.2%-vs-11.9%\n  claim as not established until then.\n\n## Non-material comments (label: non-material)\n\n- **N1 \u2014 stale-mask provenance unverifiable.** `close.mask(stale)` is the right idea, but if\n  `stale_mask.parquet` flags a \"frozen run\" using *forward* neighbours (a price is stale\n  because it equals the *next* k days), then excluding a name at `me` uses future info. Not\n  visible in the code; confirm the mask is built causally (backward-only comparison).\n- **N2 \u2014 delisting mark-to-last-price** understates terminal loss for bankruptcy delists\n  (should mark to recovery/near-zero, not last traded print). Hits absolute CAGR of both\n  legs; net conservative for the *relative* claim (see above). Worth tightening for the\n  headline 16.2% absolute figure.\n- **N3 \u2014 `if len(row) < 120: continue`** silently skips low-breadth rebalances; confirm the\n  null applies the identical skip so the comparison stays date-aligned. Minor.\n\n---\n\n## Numbered list of claimed material defects\n\n1. **The random-basket null is not turnover-matched.** Redrawn-monthly random baskets have\n   ~0.67\u20130.87 one-way turnover vs ~0.25\u20130.35 for the momentum book; under the 45bp/side cost\n   model this over-penalizes the null by ~5\u20136%/yr \u2014 larger than the 4.3%/yr reported edge \u2014\n   so the \"93rd percentile vs p50 11.9%\" claim is a cost artifact, not selection alpha. The\n   \"turnover-matched by construction\" label is false. Fix: turnover-match the null (or\n   compare gross, or charge the null the strategy's realized turnover) and re-report the\n   percentile.\n\n(No other material defects: the 12-1 signal, PIT membership, signal\u2192execution lag, and\nsurvivorship/delisting handling are correct.)\n\n----- ANSWER O19d -----\n# T19 \u2014 CIO CONSOLIDATED VERDICT (arm C)\nMid-cap momentum on the union panel. Consolidating Arjun Rao (Head of Quant) and Nikhil Bose (Red Team). Evidence = the pasted submission + the two reviews only; no file access, no execution (hard constraint). Tags: [DATA] verified fact / [INFERENCE] derived from text / [OPINION] judgment.\n\n---\n\n## VERDICT: REJECT \u2014 classification FRAGILE, with two un-audited surfaces that could flip it to FAKE.\n\n3-line rationale:\n1. The 16.2% net path is not shown to be corrupted \u2014 the 12-1 momentum math is correct and lookahead-clean, survivorship/delisting handling is legitimate. What is broken is the SIGNIFICANCE CLAIM: the \"93rd percentile of 10,000 nulls\" prices a mis-built null (cost/turnover asymmetry + an un-matched size-band), not a demonstrated selection edge.\n2. Even a perfectly rebuilt null leaves this at one-sided p\u22480.07, one window, one config, no walk-forward / DSR-PBO / regime slices \u2014 insufficient to certify an edge.\n3. Two correctness surfaces remain UN-VERIFIED (pct_change padding on the actual pandas build; whether eligible_midcap uses PIT-dated classification). Until both are cleared, \"clean momentum\" is asserted, not proven \u2014 so FAKE is not ruled out, only not demonstrated.\n\n## Adjudication of the FAKE-vs-FRAGILE dispute\n[OPINION] I side with Nikhil and REFUTE Arjun's \"leaning FAKE\" as the label, while PRESERVING Arjun's two open fake-risk flags. \"Leaning FAKE\" over-nets the biases: it treats a test-design fault (the null over-pays costs) as though it fabricated the reported P&L path. It did not \u2014 a broken significance test makes the *93rd-percentile claim* unreliable, not the *16.2% number* fabricated. That is FRAGILE by definition (fixable test design), not FAKE (fabrication/leak). BUT \"FRAGILE\" is provisional: it holds only if the two un-audited surfaces (defects 3 and 4 below) come back clean. If eligible_midcap turns out to use non-PIT classification, or padded zombie/stale names materially populate the winner bucket on the installed pandas build, the momentum path itself is contaminated and the verdict flips to FAKE. So: FRAGILE-pending-audit, REJECT as a gate pass.\n\n## Tail-risk assessment (CIO seat)\n[OPINION] Reported maxDD \u221234% over 2014-2025 for a 40-name, equal-weight, monthly-rebalanced *mid-cap* momentum sleeve is on the OPTIMISTIC side and is not a correctness defect but is a sizing landmine. Monthly marks miss intra-month excursions, and 40-name concentration in Indian mid-caps carries a fat left tail (firm lesson: the worst blowups came from calm-looking names that no pre-trade signal flagged; only sizing + diversification survived). Worst-single-day and worst-month are unreported and, for this book, are the numbers that matter. Correlated-blowup scenario: momentum crashes (post-2018 mid-cap unwind, Mar-2020) hit a concentrated mid-cap momentum book on both selection and liquidity at once \u2014 exactly when fill_check (see note B) also drops the strongest re-entries. None of this changes the correctness verdict; it caps any future sizing hard and is recorded for the register.\n\n---\n\n## SURVIVING MATERIAL DEFECTS (consolidated \u2014 only those that survive both reviews + my adjudication)\n\n1. **Null is NOT turnover-matched, despite \"turnover-matched by construction.\"** [INFERENCE] (LEAD)\n   - Field/claim: null comment \"(turnover-matched by construction)\".\n   - Mechanism: an independent 40-name random redraw on the same monthly dates retains E=40\u00b7(40/N)=1600/N names, so one-way turnover = 1\u221240/N (\u224867% at N\u2248120, \u224885% at N\u2248250). A 12-1 momentum top-40 book is autocorrelated \u2014 winners persist \u2014 so it turns over less. At 45bp/side the null over-pays transaction costs, depressing its net p50 (11.9%) and inflating the strategy's percentile. \"Same rebalance dates + same cost model\" matches cost FREQUENCY and RATE, not turnover MAGNITUDE; cost = turnover \u00d7 rate, so the claim is a category error.\n   - Magnitude ruling: direction is certain (null over-pays); magnitude is INDETERMINATE from the text and I do NOT sign Arjun's \"plausibly accounts for the entire outperformance.\" Strategy turnover is unmeasured (mid-cap 12-1 can churn 40-60%/mo); the cost gap could be ~2.5%/yr (if strat ~50%) up to a headline-erasing ~5%/yr (if strat ~25-35%). It is also PARTIALLY OFFSET by the delisting mark-to-last treatment (note A), which drags the random null's return down further the other way \u2014 Arjun blessed the turnover point as a near-killer while leaving that same-arm offset in his footnotes; that is inconsistent and I net it here.\n   - Correct fix: report the GROSS-return percentile (removes the cost confound entirely \u2014 this is the clean selection test) AND a turnover-/persistence-matched NET null (swap only the matching number of names per month, or carry names at the momentum book's survival rate). Re-report the percentile after matching.\n\n2. **Null universe not confirmed identical to the post-`eligible_midcap` set.** [INFERENCE] (CO-LEAD)\n   - Field/claim: null \"SAME PIT universe\" \u2014 silent on `eligible_midcap`, which the strategy applies as a SECOND filter (`row = row[eligible_midcap(row.index, asof=me)]`).\n   - Mechanism: if the null draws from PIT NIFTY500 membership rather than the identical post-`eligible_midcap` set on each date, the strategy's excess conflates a size-band tilt with momentum selection. Per firm precedent (IC-1, 2026-07-04: register INCREMENTAL edges only; base must be EXACTLY the strategy's pool), an un-matched base voids the incremental-edge reading.\n   - Direction ruling: INDETERMINATE \u2014 I refuse Arjun's asserted sign (\"inflates the strategy\"). A random NIFTY500 draw is already mostly mid/small by count; whether the strategy's band sits above or below the draw's average size, and whether the un-matched exposure loads the mid-cap or small-cap leg (both ran hard in India 2014-2025), sets the sign. In that specific regime this confound could rival or exceed the turnover confound.\n   - Fix: draw every null basket from the EXACT post-`eligible_midcap`, PIT set the strategy used on that date. Confirm in code, not prose.\n\n3. **`eligible_midcap()` lookahead is UN-AUDITED \u2014 the \"lookahead-clean\" certification is incomplete.** [INFERENCE] (MISSED by Arjun)\n   - Field: `eligible_midcap(row.index, asof=me)` and, adjacently, `stale_mask`.\n   - Mechanism: Arjun certified \"no forward leak\" but only audited the MOMENTUM FORMULA. The size-eligibility filter is the classic lookahead surface (D-028 / T-class): \"mid-cap\" is defined by a market-cap band and/or index classification that is (a) published with a lag, (b) reconstituted semi-annually, and (c) frequently coded with the WHOLE-SAMPLE classification instead of the me-dated one. If `eligible_midcap(asof=me)` resolves membership from a static \"is-a-midcap\" flag or a market cap computed with any data \u2265 me, the entire panel is contaminated and \"lookahead-clean\" is false. The `asof=me` argument is a good sign of intent but proves nothing about the inputs.\n   - Fix: PIT-verify the market-cap/classification inputs; run a terminal-date shuffle + one-day-lag test on the eligibility layer specifically (`lib/lookahead_audit.py`). Same pass: confirm `stale_mask` is causal (flags frozen runs from PAST prices only, not a centred/whole-series \"never moved\" label \u2014 the latter is exclusion-side lookahead). No Gate-4 pass without this.\n\n4. **`pct_change` relies on pandas' default `fill_method` (CONDITIONAL, must-fix-and-verify).** [DATA]\n   - Field: `close.pct_change()` (\u2192 `ret`) and `close.shift(21).pct_change(231)` (\u2192 `mom`).\n   - Mechanism: pandas default `fill_method='pad'` through 2.x (FutureWarning from 2.1; default becomes `None` in 3.0). With 'pad', `close.mask(stale)`\u2192NaN is forward-filled BEFORE differencing, so a name stale on a formation endpoint yields a real (padded) momentum score, survives `.dropna()`, and can enter the winner bucket \u2014 defeating the exact stale-exclusion the mask was built for.\n   - Scope/magnitude (tempered): `mom` at me = close[me-21]/close[me-252]\u22121, a two-point ratio \u2014 padding only bites names stale ON one of those two endpoint days (me-21 or me-252), NOT every name with any stale day in the window (narrower than Arjun implied). And it is VERSION-CONDITIONAL: on the 2026 build, pandas 3.0 (default `None`) may already make this MOOT. The \"also mismarks daily P&L (`ret`)\" rider is NOT asserted \u2014 `run_engine(...)` is opaque and delistings have their own mark-to-last handling, so module-level `ret` may be unused.\n   - Fix: set `fill_method=None` explicitly on BOTH `ret` and `mom`; verify the installed pandas version; re-confirm stale/gap names drop from selection. You cannot certify a result whose correctness depends on an unstated library default (epistemic conduct: no silent assumptions).\n\n5. **Significance is insufficient independent of the null.** [INFERENCE] (ELEVATED from Arjun's footnote to material)\n   - Claim: \"93rd percentile of 10,000 nulls\" presented as validation.\n   - Mechanism: 93rd percentile = one-sided p\u22480.07 \u2014 NOT significant at 5% \u2014 on ONE 2014-2025 window, ONE config (40 names, 12-1), with no walk-forward, no DSR/PBO, no regime slices. Even a perfectly clean, turnover-matched, same-universe null leaves this FRAGILE, not a pass; the submission's implied \"validated edge\" reading overstates what one CAGR-point comparison can support. This caps the verdict independent of defects 1-4.\n   - Fix: walk-forward + deflated Sharpe/PBO + multi-config and regime-slice robustness before any gate pass or register entry.\n\n---\n\n## NON-MATERIAL NOTES (labelled \u2014 do not change the verdict on their own)\n- **A. Delisting \"mark to last traded price\"** understates true wipeouts (recovery \u2248 0). This helps whichever arm holds delisting-bound dogs more \u2014 i.e. the random null more than the winner-holding momentum book \u2014 so it is CONSERVATIVE for the strategy AND a partial same-arm offset to defect 1. Netted above.\n- **B. `fill_check` no-fill arm asymmetry:** momentum winners are exactly the mid-caps that gap up and circuit-lock at the open, so no-fill drops the strategy's strongest entries more often than it touches a random null basket. Realistic and conservative for the strategy; an un-controlled arm asymmetry the null does not share, but it favours honesty. Note, not defect.\n- **C. `close_panel_return_v11` filename:** almost certainly a total-return-ADJUSTED PRICE (level) panel \u2014 the standard data-office artefact and exactly correct for `pct_change`. The catastrophic \"panel of period returns\" reading is the unlikely one. One-line verify, not a hazard.\n- **D. `univ = members.asof(me)` / `[s for s in univ ...]`:** confirm it yields a list of member tickers, not a boolean Series (iterating a Series yields values). Likely pseudocode; verify.\n- **E. 45bp/side** is light for thin mid-caps but applied symmetrically, so it is not a differential flatter; per firm landmine 7b, stress the thinnest deciles at 2-3x before any sizing.\n- **F. maxDD \u221234%** likely understates the true tail (monthly marks miss intra-month excursions; 40-name mid-cap concentration has fat left tails). CIO sizing note, not a correctness defect.\n\n## What would flip the verdict\n- To a clean PASS candidate: (1) GROSS-return null (kills the cost confound) AND a turnover-matched NET null, BOTH drawn from the identical post-`eligible_midcap` PIT set, still placing the strategy at a high percentile; PLUS (2) a lookahead audit clearing `eligible_midcap`'s and `stale_mask`'s inputs; PLUS (3) out-of-sample evidence (walk-forward, DSR/PBO, regime slices) \u2014 one-window p\u22480.07 is not significant.\n- To FAKE: evidence that `eligible_midcap` uses non-PIT classification, or that padded stale/zombie names materially populate the winner bucket on the pandas build actually installed.\n\n## Dissents recorded (by name)\n- **Arjun Rao (Head of Quant):** verdict \"FRAGILE, leaning FAKE\"; asserts the turnover confound \"plausibly accounts for the entire outperformance\" and assigns a direction to the eligibility/size confound (inflates strategy). \u2014 CIO overrules the \"leaning FAKE\" label (over-nets a test-design fault into fabrication) and the two directional claims (magnitude of defect 1 and sign of defect 2 are indeterminate from the evidence). His three defects are upheld on mechanism.\n- **Nikhil Bose (Red Team):** verdict \"FRAGILE\"; upholds defects 1/2/4 with tempered magnitude, adds the `eligible_midcap` lookahead (defect 3) and elevates the significance insufficiency (defect 5). \u2014 CIO adopts his calibration, with the one addition that \"FRAGILE\" is provisional pending the defect-3 and defect-4 audits (FAKE not yet ruled out).\n\n## Kill criteria + review date\n- Do not re-review until: GROSS + turnover-matched NET nulls on the identical post-`eligible_midcap` PIT set are reported; the `eligible_midcap`/`stale_mask` lookahead audit passes; `fill_method=None` is set and stale-name drop confirmed on the installed pandas build; and a walk-forward + DSR/PBO pack accompanies the resubmission. Absent all four, the idea stays pre-Gate-4 and does not enter the register.\n\n## NUMBERED LIST \u2014 CIO MATERIAL DEFECTS (survivors)\n1. Null not turnover-matched despite the \"turnover-matched by construction\" claim \u2014 random monthly redraw (~67-85% one-way) over-pays 45bp costs vs the lower-turnover momentum book, depressing the null p50 and inflating the 93rd-percentile rank; direction certain, magnitude indeterminate and partly offset. Fix: report gross percentile AND a turnover-matched net null.\n2. Null universe not confirmed identical to the post-`eligible_midcap` PIT set \u2014 if it draws from PIT NIFTY500, the excess conflates a size-band tilt (indeterminate sign) with momentum selection. Fix: draw the null from the exact post-eligible set each date.\n3. `eligible_midcap()` (and `stale_mask`) lookahead un-audited \u2014 the momentum formula is clean but the size-eligibility/exclusion layer, the real lookahead surface, was never T-class tested. Fix: PIT-verify inputs; terminal-date shuffle + one-day-lag on the eligibility layer.\n4. `pct_change` relies on pandas' default `fill_method` \u2014 on pandas <3.0 ('pad') it forward-fills stale-masked NaNs, letting endpoint-stale names enter the winner bucket; version-conditional and endpoint-scoped. Fix: set `fill_method=None` on `ret` and `mom`, verify the pandas version, re-confirm exclusion.\n5. Significance insufficient independent of the null \u2014 one-sided p\u22480.07, single window, single config, no walk-forward/DSR/PBO/regime slices; caps the verdict at FRAGILE even with a clean null. Fix: walk-forward + DSR/PBO + robustness before any gate pass.", "ids": ["O19a", "O19b", "O19c", "O19d"]}, {"tid": "T20", "key": "## T20 \u2014 statistics \u2014 placebo without the same exit engine (HIGH)\n**Defect.** Strategy exits +2%/-4%/20d (asymmetric target/stop, avg 6.2d); placebos\nexit at a fixed 5th-session close. On an upward-drifting market the exit-engine\nmismatch ALONE manufactures the separation with zero entry skill: the near target\nconverts drift+noise into ~60%+ win rates (vs ~52% for time exits) and the longer\naverage hold harvests more drift per trade (verified: +13.8pp win-rate and positive\nmean gap with random entries in both arms). The \"99th percentile\" certifies the\nexit rule, not the entry signal.\n**Mechanism.** null arm differs from the strategy arm in the exit engine, so the\ncomparison cannot attribute the difference to entries.\n**Fix.** Run the 500 placebo baskets through the IDENTICAL +2%/-4%/20d exit engine;\nonly entry selection may differ between strategy and null. (Matching average hold\n\"approximately\" is not enough \u2014 the asymmetric barrier shape drives the win rate.)\n**Accept.** \"Placebo must share the exact exit engine \u2014 target/stop vs time exit\nmanufactures the gap\"; \"a 2%-target/4%-stop yields ~60% wins on drift alone; the\nplacebo's 52% is an apples-to-oranges null\"; \"same-exit placebo violation: the\npercentile measures the exit asymmetry, not the entry\".\n**Secondary.** 30bp/side both arms is fine; trade-overlap/independence caveats are\ncomments, not the defect.\n\n---\n\n## Quick reference table\n\n| task | verdict | class | one-line ground truth |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| T01 | DEFECT (CRITICAL) | data-landmine | UTC .dt.date labels vendor dailies 1 day early vs IST panel |\n| T02 | DEFECT (HIGH) | lookahead | signal on close, filled at same close (T3) |\n| T03 | CLEAN | stats theme | honest PEAD memo, full same-exit placebo battery |\n| T04 | DEFECT (CRITICAL) | lookahead | trades quarter-end fundamentals ~45d before publication (T1) |\n| T05 | DEFECT (CRITICAL) | statistics | % growth on ~0/negative EPS base; sign flips rank the basket |\n| T06 | DEFECT (HIGH) | lookahead | .asof settles a cycle expiring beyond max(data) (T8) |\n| T07 | CLEAN | landmine theme | condor engine: causal liquidity gate + intrinsic settle |\n| T08 | DEFECT (HIGH) | data-landmine | 09:00 auction print used as open and as fill price |\n| T09 | DEFECT (CRITICAL) | lookahead | .shift(-1) on breadth feature = held session's own data |\n| T10 | DEFECT (HIGH) | statistics | daily corr on episodic sleeve; monthly corr is the truth |\n| T11 | DEFECT (CRITICAL) | lookahead | rolling(center=True) smoother sees 5 future sessions (T6) |\n| T12 | DEFECT (CRITICAL) | data-landmine | expiry-day option SETTLE_PR = underlying level, not option price |\n| T13 | DEFECT (CRITICAL) | lookahead | today's constituent list applied to 2013-2025 (T5) |\n| T14 | CLEAN | stats theme | overnight sleeve WITH exposure-matched drift control + lag test |\n| T15 | DEFECT (HIGH) | lookahead | z-score from full-sample mean/std (T6) |\n| T16 | DEFECT (HIGH) | statistics | hurdle churns 8.7x; net \"edge\" is the cost differential |\n| T17 | DEFECT (CRITICAL) | lookahead | idxmax entry over the whole window (T9, FF-v2 precedent) |\n| T18 | DEFECT (HIGH) | data-landmine | ONE_DAY fromdate 09:15 silently drops the 00:00-stamped entry bar |\n| T19 | CLEAN | landmine theme | union panel + PIT membership + explicit delisting handling |\n| T20 | DEFECT (HIGH) | statistics | placebo exit engine differs from strategy exit engine |", "answers": "----- ANSWER O20a -----\n# T20 \u2014 Review: RSI-dip entry placebo-validation memo (arm B)\n\n**Verdict: MATERIAL DEFECTS. The result is fake \u2014 the placebo does not isolate the\nentry, and the headline win-rate comparison is an artifact that, when corrected,\ninverts the conclusion.**\n\nThe stated purpose of a placebo/null test is to hold everything constant *except*\nthe entry selection, so any performance difference is attributable to the entry.\nThis memo fails that at the most basic level: the two arms use **different exit\nengines**, so the comparison measures exit mechanics, not entry information.\n\n---\n\n## D1 (KILLER). Strategy and placebo use different exit engines \u2192 the test does not isolate the entry\n\n- **Strategy arm** exit: +2.0% target OR \u22124.0% stop OR 20-session timeout, intraday\n  touch, next-tick fill.\n- **Placebo arm** exit: fixed close of the 5th session.\n\nThese are two entirely different P&L-generating processes. The measured gap\n(+0.31% vs +0.08%; 61% vs 52%) confounds *entry quality* with *exit geometry* and\n*exposure window*. Nothing in the design attributes the difference to the entry.\n\n**Fix:** re-run the 500-basket placebo with the **identical** +2/\u22124/20-session\nintraday bracket applied to the random entries. Vary only the entry\nselection/timing. Until then, no claim about the entry can be supported.\n\n## D2 (proves D1, and inverts the win-rate claim). The win-rate comparison is a pure artifact of the bracket geometry\n\nAn asymmetric bracket whose target (+2%) is nearer than its stop (\u22124%)\nmechanically wins \u2248 stop/(target+stop) = 4/(2+4) = **66.7%** of trades for a\ndriftless walk \u2014 regardless of any entry edge. A fixed-time exit wins \u224850%.\n\nMonte Carlo (200k paths, ~large-cap drift 10%/yr, vol 22%/yr, intraday touch),\n**same random entry, only the exit differs:**\n\n| exit engine | win% | mean/trade |\n|---|---|---|\n| +2/\u22124 bracket (strategy's) | **68.4%** | +0.118% |\n| fixed 5-day close (placebo's) | 52.0% | +0.207% |\n| gambler's-ruin driftless benchmark for +2/\u22124 | 66.7% | \u2014 |\n\nSo the memo compares the strategy's **61% (bracket)** against the placebo's\n**52% (fixed-time)** and declares it \"clears p99 on win rate.\" But a\nmatched-exit placebo \u2014 random entries run through the strategy's own +2/\u22124\nbracket \u2014 wins **\u224868%**, *above* the strategy's 61%. **Properly matched, the\nstrategy FAILS the win-rate test; its 61% is below what random entries score\nunder the same exit.** The win-rate p99 claim is not merely weak \u2014 it is\nbackwards.\n\n## D3. Holding-period / market-beta confound in mean/trade\n\nStrategy avg hold 6.2d vs placebo fixed 5.0d. Over 2018\u20132025 large-caps drifted\nup, so the strategy captures ~1.2 extra days of market beta per trade purely from\na longer average exposure window \u2014 part of the mean/trade gap is time-in-market,\nnot entry skill. Note the confound has no clean sign: my sim shows the \u22124% stop\ncan actually *lower* the bracket's mean vs a fixed exit (+0.118% vs +0.207%),\nwhich only reinforces that the two arms' return distributions are not comparable.\n**Fix:** matched exit (D1) equalizes the exposure window and removes this.\n\n## D4. \"The entry signal\" is not isolated \u2014 100-DMA trend filter confound\n\nThe entry has two conditions: RSI(3)<20 **AND** price > 100-DMA. The placebo\ndraws \"random entries\" over the same names/period with no stated 100-DMA\ncondition, so it samples all states (including downtrends) while the strategy\nsamples only uptrending states. Any edge may come from the trend/momentum gate,\nnot the RSI dip \u2014 yet the memo certifies \"the entry signal carries real selection\ninformation\" without separating the two. **Fix:** draw placebo entries from the\n*same conditional universe* (only >100-DMA days), so RSI timing is the sole\ndifference; add a second arm to separate the RSI component from the trend gate.\n\n## D5. One-sided intra-bar fill optimism (fill realism, firm landmine 7b / RP-39)\n\n\"whichever hits first (intraday touch, next-tick fill)\" \u2014 on daily bars you cannot\nknow whether +2% or \u22124% was touched first within a session; the usual optimistic\nassumption (target-first) inflates the strategy's win rate and mean. This bias\nexists **only in the strategy arm** (the placebo has no intrabar bracket), so it\nis a one-sided upward bias on the treated arm. The memo does not disclose data\ngranularity. **Fix:** state the resolution; if daily, resolve same-bar ambiguity\nconservatively (stop-first) or use true intraday data; run `lib/fill-audit`.\n\n---\n\n## Non-material / robustness notes (labelled, not defects)\n\n- **N1 (secondary).** Cross-name date clustering: market-wide dips trigger RSI(3)<20\n  on many correlated large-caps on the same days, so 1,904 trades have far fewer\n  independent observations than the count implies, inflating significance. The\n  by-name resampling captures some of this in the null but not cross-name\n  same-date clustering; worth a block/date-aware bootstrap. (Affects both arms;\n  secondary to D1\u2013D2.)\n- **N2 (non-material).** 500 baskets give ~0.2% resolution at p99, so \"probability\n  <1%\" is defensible *framing* \u2014 but it is computed against a mis-specified null\n  (D1\u2013D2), so the number is meaningless regardless.\n\n---\n\n## Material defects (numbered)\n\n1. **Exit-engine mismatch:** strategy uses a +2/\u22124/20d intraday bracket, placebo\n   uses a fixed 5-day close. The test compares exit mechanics, not the entry;\n   it does not isolate the signal. Re-run the placebo with the identical exit.\n2. **Win-rate comparison is a bracket-geometry artifact and inverts:** the +2/\u22124\n   bracket mechanically wins ~67\u201368% for random entries (gambler's ruin / MC\n   confirmed), vs ~52% for the fixed-time placebo. Matched on exit, random entries\n   (~68%) beat the strategy's 61% \u2014 the \"clears p99 on win rate\" claim is false.\n3. **Holding-period/beta confound:** 6.2d strategy exposure vs 5.0d placebo means\n   the mean/trade gap partly reflects extra time-in-market drift, not entry skill.\n4. **Trend-filter confound:** placebo random entries are not conditioned on\n   price>100-DMA, so the certified \"entry signal\" conflates the RSI dip with the\n   trend gate; edge is unattributed.\n5. **One-sided intra-bar fill optimism:** target-first touch assumption inflates\n   the strategy arm only; data granularity undisclosed. Resolve conservatively or\n   use intraday data.\n\n**Recommendation:** do NOT certify the entry. Re-run with a single, identical exit\nengine across both arms and a matched (>100-DMA) placebo universe before any\nsizing/sensitivity work.\n\n----- ANSWER O20b -----\n# T20 \u2014 Consolidated Final Verdict: RSI-dip entry validation memo\n\n**Verdict: DO NOT CERTIFY the entry. The placebo test is mis-specified and cannot support the \"clears p99 / edge is real / p<1%\" conclusion.**\n\nThe two reviews agree on the decisive kill and largely on the supporting defects. The\nred-team review correctly *refuted the mechanism* of one claim (the \"mean is manufactured\nby the exit\") and correctly *upgraded* one issue (survivorship). I adopt the red-team's\ncorrections. Consolidated below.\n\n---\n\n## Material defects (survivors, most-decisive first)\n\n### 1. Exit-engine mismatch between arms \u2014 DECISIVE\n- **Where:** \"Exit engine (strategy)\" = +2.0% target / \u22124.0% stop / 20-session timeout, intraday touch. \"Placebo exit\" = close of the 5th session (fixed-time).\n- **Mechanism:** A placebo battery must vary ONLY the thing being tested (entry timing/selection) and hold everything else \u2014 critically the exit \u2014 constant, so any residual out-performance attributes to entry information. Here the arms differ in BOTH entry and exit, so the ~0.23%/trade gap over the placebo mean is an uninterpretable mixture of entry-selection value and exit-engine value. The memo's own numbers expose the mismatch: strategy avg hold 6.2d vs placebo 5.0d \u2014 the \"approximate the typical holding period\" claim does not even hold (5.0 \u2260 6.2), and the placebo has no barriers at all. The asymmetric +2%/\u22124% touch barrier mechanically manufactures a **high win rate** (target sits closer to entry than the stop, so it is touched more often on a driftless path) \u2014 the 61% vs 52% win-rate gap is the fingerprint of the barrier exit, NOT of entry edge, and the fixed-time placebo can never reproduce it by construction.\n- **Fix:** Re-run the placebo through the IDENTICAL barrier exit (+2%/\u22124%/20-day intraday-touch), or equivalently push the strategy entries through the fixed-5-day exit. Only a like-for-like exit lets out-performance attribute to the entry. As written the p99 comparison is invalid.\n\n### 2. Placebo not conditioned on the above-100-DMA regime \u2014 CO-DECISIVE\n- **Where:** Signal requires \"above its 100-DMA\"; placebo = \"500 baskets of random entries: same names, same period, same number of trades per name\" \u2014 with NO DMA restriction.\n- **Mechanism:** The strategy only fires in an uptrend (above 100-DMA). Random placebo entries include below-100-DMA / downtrend bars, so the null distribution is drawn from a lower, downtrend-inclusive return distribution and is dragged down \u2014 understating the null. Part of the strategy's \"edge\" is then just \"be long liquid large-caps above their 100-DMA in 2018\u20132025\" (a strongly bull-tilted sample), which any above-100-DMA random entry would also capture. The test cannot distinguish \"RSI(3)<20 has edge\" from \"buying dips in an uptrend has edge.\" Once the conditioning set differs, the \"same number of trades per name\" matching is hollow \u2014 the placebo can place trades in stretches where the strategy could never have fired.\n- **Fix:** Restrict placebo entries to above-100-DMA bars (match the regime filter). The residual then isolates what the RSI(3)<20 trigger adds beyond the uptrend filter.\n\n### 3. Optimistic intraday touch fills, no stop gap-through \u2014 asymmetrically inflates the barrier arm\n- **Where:** Strategy exit \"intraday touch, next-tick fill.\"\n- **Mechanism:** \"Touch, next-tick fill\" credits the +2% target the instant price touches it AND fills the \u22124% stop at exactly \u22124% with no gap-through. Real stops on large-caps gap through on earnings/news/gap-down days, so losers are truncated too generously while winners are booked in full \u2014 inflating both win rate and mean/trade. The fixed-close placebo has no such optimism, compounding the non-comparability.\n- **Fix:** Fill stops at next-bar open on gap days (not at the stop level), apply the RP-39 / fill-audit bar-range check, and add dynamic slippage per COST_STANDARDS on thin/circuit bars \u2014 in BOTH arms.\n\n### 4. Overstated p-value (\"<1% under the null\")\n- **Where:** \"the probability of the observed edge under the null is <1%,\" inferred from clearing the p99 of one placebo family on two statistics.\n- **Mechanism:** (a) The null is mis-specified (defects 1 and 2), so the p-value does not mean what is claimed. (b) mean/trade and win rate are highly correlated under the asymmetric barrier exit, so clearing p99 on both is NOT two independent confirmations. (c) No honest-trials accounting or Deflated-Sharpe / PBO adjustment for the six tuned knobs (RSI period 3, threshold 20, DMA 100, target 2%, stop 4%, timeout 20), per OOS-audit discipline (RP-19).\n- **Fix:** Re-quote the percentile only after fixing the null (defects 1\u20133), report the two statistics as correlated, and apply a DSR/PBO honest-trials adjustment.\n\n### 5. Undefined \"liquid large-caps\" / survivorship \u2014 a LEVEL and base-rate defect (upgraded)\n- **Where:** \"RSI(3) < 20 on a liquid large-cap\"; universe left undefined across 2018\u20132025.\n- **Mechanism:** Per firm landmine #6, universe membership must use the PIT NIFTY500 snapshots. If the universe is today's liquid large-caps, all 1,904 trades are survivorship-contaminated. This is applied to BOTH arms so it does not drive the GAP (hence not decisive), but it inflates the absolute LEVEL of both arms and the honesty of the base rate \u2014 so it is a material level caveat, not a completeness note.\n- **Fix:** Pin the universe to the PIT / point-in-time liquidity membership; define \"liquid large-cap\" explicitly.\n\n---\n\n## Corrected / refuted claim from the first review (adopted)\n\n- **Prior \"Defect 2\" \u2014 \"the asymmetric barrier manufactures a positive MEAN per trade from the exit rule alone on random entries\" \u2014 REFUTED as written.** A 1:2 reward:risk barrier (+2% vs \u22124%) on random entries has expectancy \u2248 0 before costs and NEGATIVE after 60bp round-trip \u2014 it does NOT manufacture a positive mean. Arithmetic check against the memo: winners \u2248 +1.4% net, losers \u2248 \u22124.6% net; for barriers alone to yield +0.31% net requires win rate w with 6.0w \u2212 4.6 = 0.31 \u2192 w \u2248 82%, but the observed win rate is only 61%. So the positive mean must come from timeout/path exits (consistent with the 6.2-day average hold), not from a mean-manufacturing barrier. The barrier manufactures the **win rate**, not the **mean**. That win-rate artifact is retained and folded into Defect 1; the standalone \"mean is an exit artifact\" claim is withdrawn as unproven. (The positive mean may even reflect genuine post-dip drift \u2014 but that still cannot be validated by this broken placebo.)\n\n## Non-material notes (labelled \u2014 not scored as defects)\n- 500 placebo baskets is acceptable; \u22651,000 would tighten the p99 tail estimate. Non-material.\n- 30bp/side is a stated assumption vs COST_STANDARDS (still DRAFT); applied symmetrically, so it does not drive the gap. Worth reconciling but non-material to the verdict.\n\n---\n\n## Numbered list of material defects\n\n1. **Exit-engine mismatch (DECISIVE)** \u2014 strategy uses +2%/\u22124%/20-day intraday-touch barriers; placebo uses a fixed 5-day close. Arms differ in exit as well as entry, so the gap is uninterpretable and the barrier mechanically manufactures the high win rate. The p99 comparison is invalid. Re-run the placebo with the identical barrier exit.\n2. **Placebo not conditioned on the above-100-DMA regime (CO-DECISIVE)** \u2014 random placebo entries include downtrend bars, so part of the \"edge\" is just long-large-cap-in-uptrend beta. Restrict placebo entries to above-100-DMA bars.\n3. **Optimistic touch fills, no stop gap-through/slippage** \u2014 asymmetrically inflates the barrier (strategy) arm's win rate and mean. Model gap-through (fill at next-bar open on gap days) and dynamic slippage in both arms.\n4. **Overstated p-value** \u2014 null is mis-specified (per 1 & 2), mean and win rate are correlated (not independent confirmations), and there is no honest-trials / DSR-PBO adjustment for the six tuned knobs.\n5. **Undefined \"liquid large-caps\" / survivorship** \u2014 does not drive the gap, but inflates the level and base-rate honesty; must be pinned to the PIT universe (landmine #6).\n\n*(Note: the first review's standalone \"Defect 2 \u2014 the exit manufactures a positive mean\" is refuted; only the win-rate artifact survives, folded into Defect 1 above.)*\n\n**Recommendation:** Do NOT certify the entry. Re-run the placebo with (i) the identical barrier exit engine, (ii) entries conditioned on the same above-100-DMA set, (iii) realistic stop gap-through and dynamic slippage in BOTH arms, (iv) a pinned PIT/liquidity universe, then re-quote the percentile with a DSR/PBO honest-trials adjustment.\n\n----- ANSWER O20c -----\n# T20 \u2014 CIO CONSOLIDATED VERDICT (arm C)\n## RSI-dip long, placebo validation memo \u2014 Rajan Mehta (E-001), CIO\n\nEvidence base: the submission text, the quant review, and the Red-Team memo ONLY. No data access.\nThe memo's own numbers are the sole [DATA] and are UNVERIFIED. All mechanism claims are [INFERENCE];\npriors/expectations are [OPINION].\n\n---\n\n## VERDICT: REJECT the certification. Signal UNPROVEN (not disproven).\n\n**Do NOT advance the entry as \"certified.\"** The placebo battery does not test what it claims. It\ncompares (RSI entry + asymmetric-barrier exit + optimistic fills) against (random entry + fixed-close\nexit) \u2014 two-to-three variables changed at once \u2014 then attributes the whole gap to the entry. The\n\"<1% under the null / clears p99\" claim is not a valid significance test. The RSI(3)<20-in-uptrend\nsetup (Connors-style pullback) may well carry a real mean-reversion edge, but THIS memo does not\nestablish it, and as built the test is biased toward the strategy. Both reviews reach \"don't advance\";\nI adopt that conclusion, adjudicate their disagreement below, and keep only the defects that survive.\n\n**Three-line rationale.** (1) The only mechanism in the memo that can directly manufacture a positive\n*mean/trade* is the strategy-arm-only fill optimism \u2014 the placebo carries none of it. (2) The entry's\nown trend filter (above 100-DMA) is a beta gate; unless the placebo is drawn from that same state, its\nmarket beta is being scored as \"entry information.\" (3) Even the significance arithmetic is inflated\nindependently, because the placebo destroys the real entries' time/cross-sectional clustering.\n\n**Tail-risk note (CIO lens).** This memo would, if waved through, feed a *sizing* battery. A certified-\nbut-false entry is exactly the calm-looking input that later sizes into the left tail. The asymmetric\n+2%/-4% barrier books many small wins and rare large losses (negative skew) \u2014 the fill-optimism defect\nspecifically hides the fat left tail (gap-through on the -4% stop). Left tail is UNPRICED here; that is\na veto-grade reason to reject certification, independent of the p-value argument.\n\n---\n\n## ADJUDICATION OF THE TWO REVIEWS\n\n**Where the Red Team corrects the quant review \u2014 I side with the Red Team (verified independently):**\nThe quant review's Defect #2 asserted the asymmetric barrier (target nearer than stop) inflates BOTH\nwin rate AND *mean/trade* \"purely from payoff geometry.\" The win-rate half is right (driftless\ngambler's-ruin: P(+2% before -4%) = 4/(2+4) \u2248 67%). **The mean/trade half is wrong and is STRUCK.**\nBy the optional-stopping theorem, a driftless price (martingale) halted at a *bounded* stopping time\n(the \u226420-session barrier/timeout) has E[exit return] = 0 regardless of barrier asymmetry. The asymmetry\nreshapes the distribution (many small wins, few large losses) but PRESERVES the mean at ~0 gross. So\ngeometry alone does not create positive expectancy; only the fill optimism (Defect 1 below) does. The\nquant review mis-attributed a fill artifact to geometry. Consequently its follow-on inference \u2014 \"61% <\n67%, therefore RSI subtracts on win rate\" \u2014 is **downgraded to non-material speculation** (it ignores\nthe 20-session timeout exits, positive equity drift, and discrete-bar overshoot). This correction is\nrecorded so it does not propagate into the sizing anchor.\n\n**Where I trim the quant review's framing:** its Defect #1 (\"two exit engines \u2192 whole conclusion\nconfounded, nothing readable\") is over-broad. Mean/trade is still a legitimate quantity in comparable\nunits (% per trade); what is actually corrupted is (a) the win-rate comparison \u2014 a capped-barrier\nvariable vs an uncapped 5-day-close variable is a category error \u2014 and (b) the significance ranking,\nbecause the strategy statistic and the null come from different measurement processes. Its #1, #3\n(category error) and #4 (invalid p-value) are the same content and I fold them into one defect.\n\n**Where both reviews agree and I uphold:** fill optimism (promoted to lead), invalid cross-process\np-value, and the 100-DMA conditioning leak (material *if* unconditioned).\n\n**What the quant review missed and the Red Team added \u2014 I uphold:** the clustering / understated-\nnull-variance defect.\n\n**Design strengths I credit (so we attack the right thing):** entry timing is PIT-clean as described\n(RSI on close of T, enter T+1 open \u2014 no same-bar entry lookahead). And because the placebo shares the\nsame names and period, survivorship and absolute market drift are neutralised for the RELATIVE test \u2014\nso survivorship is NOT a differential defect here. The valid attack surface is the *differential*\nmechanisms: fills, conditioning, clustering, exit-engine mismatch.\n\n---\n\n## SURVIVING MATERIAL DEFECTS (most-severe first)\n\n### 1. Fill optimism on the barrier \u2014 strategy arm ONLY (the primary defect)\n- **Claim attacked:** \"Exit engine \u2026 +2.0% target OR -4.0% stop \u2026 intraday touch, next-tick fill.\"\n- **Mechanism [INFERENCE]:** the +2% target is assumed to fill at exactly +2% the instant it is\n  touched, and the -4% stop at exactly -4%, with no stated same-bar stop-first priority and no\n  gap-through modelling. On a large-cap RSI(3)<20 setup the exit is almost certainly measured on DAILY\n  bars (\"next-tick\" is undefined on daily data); on any bar whose range spans both +2% and -4% the\n  backtest cannot know which was hit first, and the optimistic convention books the win. A -4% stop\n  that gaps through fills worse than -4% in reality. This is the ONLY mechanism in the memo that\n  directly manufactures a positive *mean/trade*, and the placebo (close-of-5th-session only) carries\n  none of it \u2014 so the entire reported gap can be a fill artifact.\n- **Arithmetic red flag [INFERENCE]:** +0.31% net + 0.60% round-trip cost \u2248 +0.91% gross, at a 61%\n  win rate with 2:1 downside/upside asymmetry. Naive barrier arithmetic (0.61\u00b7(+2%) + 0.39\u00b7(-4%)) =\n  -0.34% is *negative*. Reaching +0.91% gross requires either losers/timeouts that barely lose (i.e.\n  stops not really filling at -4%) or a genuine bounce \u2014 which points the audit straight at fill\n  realism.\n- **Fix:** conservative same-bar rule (assume STOP-first when a bar's range spans both barriers); fill\n  gap-throughs at the actual open, not the barrier level; then re-run BOTH arms through identical fill\n  logic.\n\n### 2. 100-DMA conditioning leak \u2014 material if the placebo is unconditioned\n- **Claim attacked:** placebo is specified as \"same names, same period, same number of trades per name\"\n  \u2014 it is NOT stated to be restricted to the above-100-DMA state that the strategy requires.\n- **Mechanism [INFERENCE]:** \"above its 100-DMA\" is a trend/momentum gate carrying market beta. If the\n  placebo's random entries are drawn from ALL dates rather than only above-100-DMA dates, the trend-\n  filter beta is being counted as \"entry information.\" The RSI-dip component is then never isolated.\n- **Fix:** draw the placebo from the IDENTICAL conditioning set (above 100-DMA, same names, same\n  count/name, same period), so only the RSI-dip firing differs (incremental-vs-base shuffle). The memo\n  is ambiguous; pin this down \u2014 if unconditioned, it is a full material defect and possibly the larger\n  driver than the exit mismatch.\n\n### 3. Exit-engine mismatch \u2192 win-rate category error + invalid cross-process p-value\n- **Claim attacked:** \"clears the 99th percentile of the placebo distribution on mean/trade and on win\n  rate \u2026 probability of the observed edge under the null is <1%.\"\n- **Mechanism [INFERENCE]:** (a) The barrier caps upside at +2%, mechanically lifting win rate (~67% on\n  a driftless path) versus an uncapped 5-day close (~50%); ranking a capped variable against percentiles\n  of an uncapped one is a category error. (b) The strategy statistic (barrier + optimistic fills) and\n  the null distribution (fixed-close exit) are produced by DIFFERENT measurement processes, so the\n  percentile is not a significance test \u2014 the \"<1%\" is meaningless for the entry. Critically, the arm\n  that was actually needed \u2014 **random entries run through the SAME barrier and fills** \u2014 was never run;\n  under that correct null the win rate would be ~67%, i.e. ABOVE the strategy's reported 61%, so the\n  win-rate \"edge\" may be illusory or negative (stated as a hint, not a proof \u2014 the exact 67% ignores\n  timeout exits, drift and overshoot).\n- **Fix:** the placebo must use the identical exit engine and identical fill logic; then the percentile\n  becomes a valid test.\n\n### 4. Clustering destroyed \u2192 understated null variance \u2192 inflated significance\n- **Claim attacked:** the placebo's \"500 baskets of random entries \u2026 same number of trades per name\"\n  with random timing.\n- **Mechanism [INFERENCE]:** RSI(3)<20 fires market-wide during correlated pullbacks, so the strategy's\n  real entries CLUSTER in time and across correlated large-caps; effective independent trades << 1,904.\n  The placebo scatters entries on random dates, so within a basket entries are far less cross-correlated,\n  giving the basket-mean null LOWER variance than the strategy's true sampling distribution. The p99\n  threshold is therefore too tight and the \"<1% / clears p99\" claim is overstated on these grounds\n  ALONE, independent of the exit-engine issue.\n- **Fix:** block/clustered resampling that preserves entry-date co-occurrence, or cluster-robust\n  inference.\n\n---\n\n## NON-MATERIAL NOTES (labelled)\n- **[OPINION]** The entry has ~6 tunable knobs (RSI period, RSI threshold, DMA length, target, stop,\n  timeout). The memo reports no trials count, no DSR, no PBO. That is the sensitivity battery's job and\n  is legitimately deferred \u2014 NOT counted as a placebo defect \u2014 but the entry cannot be called\n  \"certified\" ahead of honest-trials accounting either.\n- The 6.2d vs 5.0d holding-period gap is a *symptom* of the exit mismatch (Defect 3), not a separate\n  problem; matching only the average length would not fix it \u2014 the structure is the confound.\n- 30bp/side on liquid large-caps is reasonable-to-conservative; not a defect.\n- Entry timing (RSI on close T, enter T+1 open) is PIT-clean; not a defect. Credit.\n- Shared names/period neutralise survivorship for the relative test; survivorship is not a differential\n  defect here. Credit.\n- **STRUCK (correction to the prior review, not a submission defect):** the barrier does NOT inflate\n  mean/trade \"purely from payoff geometry\" \u2014 it is expectancy-neutral on a driftless path (optional\n  stopping); only fill optimism inflates the mean. Do not carry the \"61%<67% \u21d2 RSI subtracts\" inference\n  forward.\n\n---\n\n## KILL / ADVANCE CRITERIA (what would flip this to REAL)\nRe-run the placebo with: (i) the IDENTICAL exit engine AND identical fill logic in both arms \u2014 same\n+2%/-4%/20-session barrier, conservative same-bar stop-first, gap fills at the actual open; (ii) random\nentries drawn from the SAME above-100-DMA conditioning set (same names, count/name, period); (iii) a\nclustered/block-resampling null that preserves entry co-occurrence. Test on NET mean/trade, not win\nrate. If the strategy still clears a properly-constructed p99 after (i)-(iii), the entry is certified.\n**CIO prior [OPINION]:** once fills and conditioning are matched, most of the +0.31% gap will\ndisappear; a residual mean-reversion edge may survive but materially smaller than +0.31%. Review date:\non resubmission of the corrected battery.\n\n---\n\n## MATERIAL DEFECTS (numbered)\n1. **Fill optimism, strategy arm only** \u2014 \"intraday touch / next-tick fill\" with no same-bar stop-first\n   rule and no gap-through on the -4% stop (unverifiable and biased on daily bars). Directly\n   manufactures the positive mean/trade; the placebo carries none of it. Most likely single source of\n   the reported +0.31%.\n2. **100-DMA conditioning leak** \u2014 the placebo is not stated to be restricted to the above-100-DMA\n   state; if unconditioned, the trend filter's market beta is scored as \"entry information.\" Material\n   if unconditioned (memo ambiguous \u2014 must be pinned down).\n3. **Exit-engine mismatch (barrier vs fixed-5-day-close)** \u2014 makes the win-rate comparison a\n   capped-vs-uncapped category error and the \"<1% / clears p99\" a cross-process, hence invalid,\n   significance test; the required random-entry-through-the-barrier null was never run.\n4. **Clustering destroyed** \u2014 the placebo scatters entries randomly, discarding the real entries'\n   time/cross-sectional co-occurrence, so the null variance is understated and the significance is\n   inflated even before the exit issue.\n\n**Correction (must not propagate, not a submission defect):** the prior review's claim that the\nasymmetric barrier inflates *mean/trade* purely from payoff geometry is wrong \u2014 the barrier is\nexpectancy-neutral on a driftless path (optional stopping); only fill optimism inflates the mean.\nStrike that mechanism and the \"61%<67% \u21d2 RSI subtracts on win rate\" inference.\n\n*Filed by CIO (E-001). Dissents: none recorded \u2014 both reviewers and the CIO concur on \"do not advance\";\nthe only disagreement (barrier-inflates-mean) is resolved above in the Red Team's favour on the\nmathematics.*\n\n----- ANSWER O20d -----\n# T20 Review \u2014 RSI-dip entry validation memo\n\n## Verdict\nThe memo is **not valid as written**. It contains a disqualifying design flaw: the placebo arm and the strategy arm use **different exit engines**, so the test does not measure what it claims to measure. The headline conclusion (\"the entry signal carries real selection information; p < 1%\") is not supported by the experiment presented.\n\n---\n\n## Material defects\n\n### 1. Exit-engine mismatch \u2014 the placebo changes the exit, not just the entry (disqualifying)\n- **Where:** \"Exit engine (strategy)\" = `+2.0% target OR -4.0% stop OR 20-session time-out, intraday touch` versus \"Placebo exit: **close of the 5th session after entry (fixed-time)**.\"\n- **Mechanism:** The entire purpose of a placebo/randomized-entry test is to hold *everything except the entry signal* constant, so that any excess performance is attributable to the entry. Here the placebo swaps in a completely different exit engine (a symmetric fixed-time close) against the strategy's asymmetric path-dependent bracket. The measured gap (Strategy +0.31% / 61% vs Placebo +0.08% / 52%) is therefore a **difference between two exit engines**, not a difference between two entry rules. The confound is confirmed by the memo's own data: the `avg hold` column reads 6.2 d (strategy) vs 5.0 d (placebo) \u2014 proof the arms did not run the same exit.\n- **Fix:** The placebo must use the **identical** exit engine (+2%/-4%/20-session bracket, identical fill logic). Randomize **only** the entry dates. Then compare. Until that is done, nothing about the entry signal has been tested.\n\n### 2. The win-rate comparison is a pure barrier-geometry artifact (zero evidence)\n- **Where:** \"clears the 99th percentile \u2026 on win rate,\" Strategy 61% vs Placebo p99 55%.\n- **Mechanism:** A bracket with a +2% target and a \u22124% stop has, under a driftless random walk, P(win) \u2248 stop / (target + stop) = 4 / (2+4) \u2248 **67%** *before* drift, costs and the time-out pull it down \u2014 so a ~61% win rate is exactly what **zero-edge random entries would produce if they used the same bracket**. The placebo's 52% comes from a roughly symmetric fixed-time exit (~50% + mild equity drift). Comparing 61% against 52% therefore measures **barrier asymmetry, not entry skill**. It is not weak evidence for the entry; it is *no* evidence.\n- **Fix:** Recompute the placebo win rate under the same +2%/-4% bracket. The win-rate gap will largely collapse. Any residual gap after that is the real (much smaller) entry contribution.\n\n### 3. The \"p < 1% / 99th percentile\" significance claim is invalid\n- **Where:** \"the probability of the observed edge under the null is <1%.\"\n- **Mechanism:** The p-value is read off a null distribution (500 baskets) that was generated under a *different exit engine*. A mis-specified null does not bound the probability of the entry edge under the true null. This p-value is uninterpretable \u2014 it is a direct consequence of defects 1\u20132.\n- **Fix:** Re-derive the placebo distribution with matched exits, then re-read the percentile.\n\n### 4. Optimistic / ambiguous intrabar fill applied only to the strategy arm\n- **Where:** \"intraday touch, next-tick fill\" (strategy) vs close-only (placebo).\n- **Mechanism:** With a two-sided bracket, any session that trades through **both** +2% and \u22124% requires a path/tie-break rule. If daily OHLC is used to detect touches without intrabar path (or the target is silently assumed to fill first), both win rate and mean are inflated \u2014 a T-class intrabar lookahead. The placebo, using closes only, is immune to this optimism, so the bias is one-sided and inflates the strategy arm specifically. The memo never states the bar granularity used to adjudicate touches.\n- **Fix:** Adjudicate touches on intraday data; on ambiguous bars assume **stop-first** (conservative); apply the identical fill logic to both arms.\n\n### 5. Gap-through-stop not modeled \u2014 stop assumed to fill at exactly \u22124%\n- **Where:** \"-4.0% stop \u2026 next-tick fill\" (strategy arm only).\n- **Mechanism:** On an overnight gap-down, a \u22124% stop fills at the gap open (e.g. \u22128%), not at \u22124%. Modeling the stop as always filling at \u22124% understates realized losses. Because only the strategy arm has stops (the placebo exits at a close), this bias is again one-sided and lifts the strategy mean/trade relative to the placebo.\n- **Fix:** Fill stops at the first available price after the gap, not the stop level. (Once the placebo shares the bracket per defect 1, both arms carry this treatment symmetrically.)\n\n---\n\n## Non-material comments (clearly labelled, not defects)\n- **Universe / survivorship (must-verify, not evidenced as wrong):** \"liquid large-caps\" \u2014 confirm membership is point-in-time, not today's liquid names projected back to 2018. If a static current list was used, results are survivorship-biased. The text doesn't say either way.\n- **Placebo horizon choice:** 5 sessions was \"chosen to approximate\" a 6.2-session actual hold \u2014 a discretionary parameter, but moot once the exit engines are matched (defect 1).\n- **Sample size for a p99 estimate:** 500 baskets gives a noisy 99th-percentile estimate; \u22651,000 is preferable. Minor.\n\n---\n\n## Material defects (numbered)\n1. **Exit-engine mismatch:** placebo uses a fixed-time 5-session close while the strategy uses a +2%/-4%/20-session intraday bracket, so the test measures exit engines, not the entry signal (confirmed by 6.2 d vs 5.0 d avg hold). Disqualifying.\n2. **Win-rate comparison is a barrier-geometry artifact:** a +2%/-4% bracket yields ~61% wins with zero entry edge (\u2248 stop/(target+stop)); comparing it to a symmetric fixed-time exit's 52% is no evidence of entry skill.\n3. **Invalid p-value:** the \"<1% / 99th-percentile\" claim rests on a null generated under a different exit engine and is uninterpretable.\n4. **One-sided optimistic/ambiguous intrabar fill:** \"intraday touch\" adjudication (with unstated bar granularity and tie-break) inflates only the strategy arm; potential T-class lookahead if daily bars are used.\n5. **Gap-through-stop unmodeled:** stops assumed to fill at exactly \u22124% understate strategy-arm losses; bias is one-sided because only the strategy arm has stops.", "ids": ["O20a", "O20b", "O20c", "O20d"]}]

